window.EF_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"l":1,"s":"01-how-ef-core-works","t":"How EF Core Works (and What Costs Time)","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"How EF Core Works (and What Costs Time) You can't optimize what you don't understand. This lesson walks through what EF Core does between your LINQ query and the database, and shows where the time goes. ⏱ 20 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Build an accurate mental model of what EF Core does between your LINQ code and the database, and learn the six places where time is spent, so every later optimization has a clear reason. Why it matters: You can't fix what you can't explain. Developers who understand the query pipeline and change tracker spot performance problems by reading code. Everyone else finds them in production."},{"l":1,"s":"01-how-ef-core-works","t":"How EF Core Works (and What Costs Time)","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Explain the roles of DbContext , DbSet<T> , the model and the change tracker. Describe the query pipeline: LINQ → expression tree → SQL → materialization. Name the six main cost centres of EF Core performance. Set up the sample ShopContext used throughout this course."},{"l":1,"s":"01-how-ef-core-works","t":"How EF Core Works (and What Costs Time)","a":"what-ef-core-is","h":"What EF Core is","x":"What EF Core is Entity Framework Core (EF Core) is Microsoft's open-source object-relational mapper (ORM) for .NET. You work with ordinary C# classes (entities), and EF Core translates your LINQ queries into SQL. It also tracks changes to your objects and writes them back to the database. It supports SQL Server, PostgreSQL (Npgsql), SQLite, MySQL, Oracle, Azure Cosmos DB and more through database providers . This course targets EF Core 10 , the Long-Term Support release that shipped with .NET 10 in November 2025. Nearly everything also applies to EF Core 8 and 9. Where a feature is version-specific, the lesson says so."},{"l":1,"s":"01-how-ef-core-works","t":"How EF Core Works (and What Costs Time)","a":"the-sample-model-used-in-this-course","h":"The sample model used in this course","x":"The sample model used in this course Every lesson uses the same small online-shop domain, so you can focus on the techniques instead of learning a new model each time. public class Customer { public int Id { get; set; } public string Name { get; set; } = \"\"; public string Email { get; set; } = \"\"; public DateTime CreatedUtc { get; set; } public List<Order> Orders { get; set; } = new(); } public class Order { public int Id { get; set; } public int CustomerId { get; set; } public Customer Customer { get; set; } = null!; public DateTime PlacedUtc { get; set; } public OrderStatus Status { get; set; } public decimal Total { get; set; } public List<OrderLine> Lines { get; set; } = new(); } public class OrderLine { public int Id { get; set; } public int OrderId { get; set; } public int ProductId { get; set; } public Product Product { get; set; } = null!; public int Quantity { get; set; } public decimal UnitPrice { get; set; } } public class Product { public int Id { get; set; } public string Sku { get; set; } = \"\"; public string Name { get; set; } = \"\"; public string Description { get; set; } = \"\"; // potentially large public decimal Price { get; set; } public int CategoryId { get; set; } public Category Category { get; set; } = null!; } public class Category { public int Id { get; set; } public string Name { get; set; } = \"\"; public List<Product> Products { get; set; } = new(); } public enum OrderStatus { Pending, Paid, Shipped, Cancelled } public class ShopContext : DbContext { public ShopContext(DbContextOptions<ShopContext> options) : base(options) { } public DbSet<Customer> Customers => Set<Customer>(); public DbSet<Order> Orders => Set<Order>(); public DbSet<OrderLine> OrderLines => Set<OrderLine>(); public DbSet<Product> Products => Set<Product>(); public DbSet<Category> Categories => Set<Category>(); protected override void OnModelCreating(ModelBuilder modelBuilder) { modelBuilder.ApplyConfigurationsFromAssembly(typeof(ShopContext).Assembly); } } About the code samples Snippets in this course are excerpts . Entity classes, configuration and usage normally live in different files, so a snippet can show a class next to the code that uses it. Assume the usual using directives ( Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore , etc.), plus a db variable holding a ShopContext ."},{"l":1,"s":"01-how-ef-core-works","t":"How EF Core Works (and What Costs Time)","a":"the-key-building-blocks","h":"The key building blocks","x":"The key building blocks Piece What it does Performance relevance Model ( IModel ) EF's metadata about entities, keys, relationships and column mappings. Built once per context type, then cached. Building the model is expensive with hundreds of entities. That's a startup cost (Lesson 22). DbContext A unit of work: holds a connection, a change tracker and services. Meant to be short-lived . Creating one is cheap but not free. Pooling helps at high throughput (Lesson 20). DbSet<T> The entry point for querying and adding entities of type T . It's an IQueryable<T> , so nothing runs until you enumerate it. Change tracker Remembers every entity a context loaded or attached, plus a snapshot of its original values. Costs memory and CPU on every query and every SaveChanges (Lesson 10). Query pipeline Turns a LINQ expression tree into SQL and turns rows back into objects. Query compilation is cached. Cache misses are costly (Lesson 21)."},{"l":1,"s":"01-how-ef-core-works","t":"How EF Core Works (and What Costs Time)","a":"the-life-of-a-query","h":"The life of a query","x":"The life of a query When you write: var orders = await db.Orders .Where(o => o.CustomerId == customerId && o.Status == OrderStatus.Paid) .OrderByDescending(o => o.PlacedUtc) .ToListAsync(); EF Core runs these steps: Expression tree. The C# compiler turns your lambdas into an expression tree : data that describes the code rather than compiled IL. Cache lookup. EF checks its query cache for a query with the same shape . Captured variables such as customerId become parameters, so the same query with a different id is a cache hit. Compilation (on a miss). EF translates the tree into SQL plus a shaper , the code that turns rows into objects. This is the most expensive in-process step. Execution. EF opens a connection (from ADO.NET's connection pool), sends the SQL with parameters and waits for the database. This network round trip plus the database's own work usually dominates total time. Materialization. EF reads each row and creates entity instances. Tracking. For tracking queries, EF registers each entity in the change tracker, applies identity resolution and fixes up navigation properties. SELECT [o].[Id], [o].[CustomerId], [o].[PlacedUtc], [o].[Status], [o].[Total] FROM [Orders] AS [o] WHERE [o].[CustomerId] = @customerId AND [o].[Status] = 1 ORDER BY [o].[PlacedUtc] DESC Parameter names in this course follow EF 10, which names parameters after your variables ( @customerId ). EF 9 and earlier generate names like @__customerId_0 . Rule of thumb In most applications the database dominates: round trips, rows scanned, rows transferred. Fix data access patterns first (what you fetch and how many queries you send). Micro-optimize EF itself last."},{"l":1,"s":"01-how-ef-core-works","t":"How EF Core Works (and What Costs Time)","a":"the-six-cost-centres","h":"The six cost centres","x":"The six cost centres Almost every EF Core performance problem falls into one of these buckets. The rest of the course is organized around them. # Cost centre Typical symptom Lessons 1 Too many round trips N+1 queries, saving in a loop, lazy loading in loops 12, 16 2 Too much data Fetching all columns or rows, client-side filtering, cartesian explosion 4, 11, 13, 15 3 Inefficient SQL / missing indexes Table scans, bad plans, offset paging on big tables 13, 14, 18, 19 4 EF in-process overhead Tracking you don't need, query cache misses, context allocation 10, 20, 21 5 Startup cost Slow first request, large models 22 6 Misconfiguration Wrong lifetimes, debug options in production, untuned providers, missing retries 2, 5–9"},{"l":1,"s":"01-how-ef-core-works","t":"How EF Core Works (and What Costs Time)","a":"the-unit-of-work-pattern","h":"The unit-of-work pattern","x":"The unit-of-work pattern A DbContext is a unit of work. You load some entities, change them in memory, then call SaveChanges() . EF compares current values with the snapshot it took when loading, then emits the minimum INSERT , UPDATE and DELETE statements, batched together and wrapped in a transaction. await using var db = new ShopContext(options); var product = await db.Products.SingleAsync(p => p.Sku == \"BOOK-42\"); product.Price = 19.99m; // only changes memory await db.SaveChangesAsync(); // UPDATE [Products] SET [Price] = @p0 WHERE [Id] = @p1 A DbContext is not thread-safe Never share one context across threads or run two operations on it in parallel (for example, Task.WhenAll over two queries on the same context). EF throws InvalidOperationException: A second operation was started on this context instance… . Use one context per unit of work, which in ASP.NET Core means one per request."},{"l":1,"s":"01-how-ef-core-works","t":"How EF Core Works (and What Costs Time)","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways A DbContext is a short-lived unit of work. Don't share it across threads or requests. LINQ queries are translated to SQL, and compiled translations are cached by query shape . Captured variables become parameters. Round trips and data volume usually dominate cost. Fix those before micro-optimizing EF itself. Six cost centres to look for: round trips, data volume, SQL and indexes, EF overhead, startup, configuration."},{"l":1,"s":"01-how-ef-core-works","t":"How EF Core Works (and What Costs Time)","a":"exercise","h":"Exercise","x":"Exercise 🛠 Build the playground Create a console app: dotnet new console -n EfPlayground . Add packages: dotnet add package Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer (or .Sqlite ) and dotnet add package Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design . Paste the model and ShopContext above. Install the CLI tool: dotnet tool install --global dotnet-ef . Create the database: dotnet ef migrations add Initial , then dotnet ef database update . Write a seeding loop that inserts 1,000 customers with 10 orders each. You'll reuse this data in later lessons. Show a seeding snippet var rnd = new Random(42); var category = new Category { Name = \"Books\" }; var products = Enumerable.Range(1, 200).Select(i => new Product { Sku = $\"SKU-{i:D5}\", Name = $\"Product {i}\", Description = new string('x', 2000), Price = rnd.Next(5, 200), Category = category }).ToList(); db.Products.AddRange(products); for (int c = 0; c < 1000; c++) { var customer = new Customer { Name = $\"Customer {c}\", Email = $\"c{c}@shop.test\", CreatedUtc = DateTime.UtcNow }; for (int o = 0; o < 10; o++) { var order = new Order { PlacedUtc = DateTime.UtcNow.AddDays(-rnd.Next(365)), Status = (OrderStatus)rnd.Next(4) }; for (int l = 0; l < 3; l++) { var p = products[rnd.Next(products.Count)]; order.Lines.Add(new OrderLine { Product = p, Quantity = rnd.Next(1, 5), UnitPrice = p.Price }); } order.Total = order.Lines.Sum(x => x.Quantity * x.UnitPrice); customer.Orders.Add(order); } db.Customers.Add(customer); } await db.SaveChangesAsync(); // EF batches these inserts. Lesson 16 explains how."},{"l":1,"s":"01-how-ef-core-works","t":"How EF Core Works (and What Costs Time)","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz When does EF Core send SQL to the database for db.Orders.Where(o => o.Total > 100) ? A. As soon as Where is called B. When the DbContext is created C. When the query is enumerated, e.g. with ToListAsync() or foreach D. When SaveChanges() is called LINQ queries against a DbSet are deferred. They only build an expression tree until you enumerate them. Which of these is usually the largest cost in a slow EF Core endpoint? A. Creating the DbContext instance B. Database round trips and the amount of data scanned or transferred C. Expression tree allocation D. Reflection on entity properties Network latency and database work usually dwarf in-process overhead. Fix query count and data volume first. Why is changing customerId between calls still a query-cache hit? A. Captured variables are sent as SQL parameters, so the query shape is unchanged B. EF caches results, not queries C. SQL Server caches the result set D. It isn't a cache hit. Each value compiles a new query. Closure variables become parameters ( @customerId ). Lesson 21 shows how constants can break this."},{"l":1,"s":"01-how-ef-core-works","t":"How EF Core Works (and What Costs Time)","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Microsoft Learn: EF Core performance overview How queries work What's new in EF Core 10"},{"l":2,"s":"02-setup-and-configuration","t":"Setup, Configuration & Logging SQL","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Setup, Configuration & Logging SQL Before you optimize anything you need to see the SQL EF Core runs. This lesson covers registering a DbContext properly, the options worth knowing, and making EF show you what it's doing. ⏱ 25 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Get a correctly configured DbContext running quickly and, above all, make EF show you the SQL it executes . That habit underpins the rest of the course. Why it matters: Most EF performance problems are invisible until you look at the generated SQL. Logging and the right lifetime from day one prevent whole classes of bugs. Level 2 then goes deeper into configuration."},{"l":2,"s":"02-setup-and-configuration","t":"Setup, Configuration & Logging SQL","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Register a DbContext in ASP.NET Core with the right lifetime. Configure provider options: connection strings, timeouts, retries. Log generated SQL with LogTo and ILoggerFactory , and filter the output. Know which diagnostic options are safe in production and which are not."},{"l":2,"s":"02-setup-and-configuration","t":"Setup, Configuration & Logging SQL","a":"registering-the-dbcontext","h":"Registering the DbContext","x":"Registering the DbContext In ASP.NET Core, register the context with dependency injection. AddDbContext registers it with a scoped lifetime: one instance per HTTP request. That's exactly the unit-of-work lifetime you want. var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args); builder.Services.AddDbContext<ShopContext>(options => options.UseSqlServer( builder.Configuration.GetConnectionString(\"Shop\"), sql => { sql.CommandTimeout(30); // seconds sql.EnableRetryOnFailure( // transient fault handling (Lesson 17) maxRetryCount: 5, maxRetryDelay: TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10), errorNumbersToAdd: null); })); { \"ConnectionStrings\": { \"Shop\": \"Server=.;Database=Shop;Trusted_Connection=True;TrustServerCertificate=True;Max Pool Size=200\" } } Lifetimes matter Never register a DbContext as a singleton. It isn't thread-safe and its change tracker grows forever. Don't inject a scoped DbContext into a singleton service such as a hosted BackgroundService . Inject IDbContextFactory<T> or IServiceScopeFactory and create a short-lived context per unit of work. When you need a factory Blazor Server, background workers and parallel processing need several short-lived contexts inside one scope. Register a factory: builder.Services.AddDbContextFactory<ShopContext>(o => o.UseSqlServer(connStr)); public class NightlyReportJob(IDbContextFactory<ShopContext> factory) : BackgroundService { protected override async Task ExecuteAsync(CancellationToken ct) { await using var db = await factory.CreateDbContextAsync(ct); var count = await db.Orders.CountAsync(o => o.Status == OrderStatus.Pending, ct); // ... } }"},{"l":2,"s":"02-setup-and-configuration","t":"Setup, Configuration & Logging SQL","a":"seeing-the-sql","h":"Seeing the SQL","x":"Seeing the SQL This is the most important habit in the whole course: always know what SQL your code produces . There are several ways to see it. 1. LogTo : quick and simple options.UseSqlServer(connStr) .LogTo(Console.WriteLine, LogLevel.Information); Filter the output to just executed commands: options.LogTo( Console.WriteLine, new[] { DbLoggerCategory.Database.Command.Name }, LogLevel.Information); 2. ASP.NET Core logging: the production-friendly way AddDbContext automatically picks up the application's ILoggerFactory . Control verbosity in appsettings.Development.json : { \"Logging\": { \"LogLevel\": { \"Default\": \"Information\", \"Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Database.Command\": \"Information\" } } } Every executed command is then logged with its duration: info: Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Database.Command[20101] Executed DbCommand (4ms) [Parameters=[@customerId='42'], CommandType='Text', CommandTimeout='30'] SELECT [o].[Id], [o].[CustomerId], ... FROM [Orders] AS [o] WHERE [o].[CustomerId] = @customerId 3. ToQueryString() : inspect without executing var query = db.Orders.Where(o => o.Total > 100).OrderBy(o => o.PlacedUtc); Console.WriteLine(query.ToQueryString()); // great in unit tests and in the debugger"},{"l":2,"s":"02-setup-and-configuration","t":"Setup, Configuration & Logging SQL","a":"diagnostic-options","h":"Diagnostic options","x":"Diagnostic options Option What it does Production? EnableSensitiveDataLogging() Includes parameter values and entity key values in logs and exception messages. (EF 10 also redacts constants it inlines into SQL unless this is on.) ❌ No. It leaks PII and secrets. EnableDetailedErrors() Better errors when materialization fails (e.g. a null in a non-nullable column). Small overhead. ⚠ Development only LogTo(...) Simple logging to a delegate. ⚠ Prefer ILogger ConfigureWarnings(...) Turns specific EF warnings into errors, or ignores them. ✅ Yes builder.Services.AddDbContext<ShopContext>(options => { options.UseSqlServer(connStr); if (builder.Environment.IsDevelopment()) { options.EnableSensitiveDataLogging() .EnableDetailedErrors(); } // Fail fast on patterns that hurt performance (see Lessons 12 and 15) options.ConfigureWarnings(w => w .Throw(RelationalEventId.MultipleCollectionIncludeWarning)); }); Turn performance warnings into errors in development EF logs warnings for risky patterns. Examples: loading several collections in one query (cartesian explosion), and First() without OrderBy ( CoreEventId.FirstWithoutOrderByAndFilterWarning ). Throwing on these in development catches problems before they reach production."},{"l":2,"s":"02-setup-and-configuration","t":"Setup, Configuration & Logging SQL","a":"connection-pooling-already-on","h":"Connection pooling: already on","x":"Connection pooling: already on ADO.NET providers pool physical database connections by default. Opening and closing a SqlConnection just borrows from and returns to the pool. EF opens the connection as late as possible and closes it as early as possible, which is the right behaviour. Tune pool size in the connection string ( Max Pool Size , default 100 for SqlClient) only if you see pool exhaustion errors under load. Connection pooling ≠ DbContext pooling Connection pooling (ADO.NET) reuses database connections. DbContext pooling (EF, Lesson 20) reuses DbContext objects. They solve different problems and work together. Going deeper This lesson covers just enough configuration to get started. Level 2 · Configuration covers the rest in depth: the options pipeline and design-time tools (Lesson 5), connection strings, secrets and pooling (Lesson 6), provider-specific settings (Lesson 7), model configuration at scale (Lesson 8), and environments and testing (Lesson 9)."},{"l":2,"s":"02-setup-and-configuration","t":"Setup, Configuration & Logging SQL","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways Register the context with AddDbContext (scoped). Use a factory for singletons and background work. Always know your SQL: LogTo , ILogger categories, and ToQueryString() . EnableSensitiveDataLogging and EnableDetailedErrors are for development only. Turn EF performance warnings into exceptions during development."},{"l":2,"s":"02-setup-and-configuration","t":"Setup, Configuration & Logging SQL","a":"exercise","h":"Exercise","x":"Exercise 🛠 Make EF talk Add LogTo filtered to DbLoggerCategory.Database.Command in your playground. Run db.Customers.FirstAsync() . What warning appears, and what SQL is generated? Print db.Orders.Where(o => o.Total > 500).ToQueryString() . Notice the literal 500 : constants in the expression become SQL literals, while variables become parameters. Enable EnableSensitiveDataLogging and compare the logged parameters. What you should see FirstAsync() without ordering gives a warning that results may be unpredictable, because the database doesn't guarantee row order without an ORDER BY . The SQL is SELECT TOP(1) ... FROM [Customers] . With sensitive logging on, parameters show their real values ( @id='42' ). Otherwise they appear as '?' ."},{"l":2,"s":"02-setup-and-configuration","t":"Setup, Configuration & Logging SQL","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz What lifetime does AddDbContext use by default? A. Singleton B. Scoped C. Transient D. Pooled Scoped means one instance per request, which matches the unit-of-work pattern. Which option should never be on in production? A. CommandTimeout B. EnableRetryOnFailure C. ConfigureWarnings D. EnableSensitiveDataLogging It writes parameter values (emails, tokens, personal data) into logs. A BackgroundService needs database access. What's the best approach? A. Inject IDbContextFactory<ShopContext> and create a context per unit of work B. Inject ShopContext directly into the constructor C. Store a static ShopContext instance D. Register the context as a singleton Hosted services are singletons. A factory gives you correctly scoped, short-lived contexts."},{"l":2,"s":"02-setup-and-configuration","t":"Setup, Configuration & Logging SQL","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading DbContext lifetime, configuration and initialization Simple logging Connection strings"},{"l":3,"s":"03-modeling-fundamentals","t":"Modeling Fundamentals","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Modeling Fundamentals Many performance problems start in the model. Unbounded string columns, wrong decimal types and missing relationships turn into slow queries later. This lesson shows how to configure a model deliberately. ⏱ 30 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Learn to configure the model deliberately : keys, relationships, column types, lengths and precision, rather than relying on conventions you haven't checked. Why it matters: The model decides the schema, and the schema decides what the database can do fast. Unbounded strings, truncated decimals and mismatched types become slow queries and data bugs that are expensive to fix later."},{"l":3,"s":"03-modeling-fundamentals","t":"Modeling Fundamentals","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Explain EF Core's conventions and how to override them. Choose between data annotations and the Fluent API, and organize configuration with IEntityTypeConfiguration<T> . Configure keys, relationships, column types, lengths and precision. Apply model-wide defaults with ConfigureConventions ."},{"l":3,"s":"03-modeling-fundamentals","t":"Modeling Fundamentals","a":"conventions-what-you-get-for-free","h":"Conventions: what you get for free","x":"Conventions: what you get for free EF Core builds the model by convention. A few defaults matter for performance: Convention Default Performance consequence Property named Id or <Type>Id Primary key. Integers use IDENTITY on SQL Server. Fine. Lesson 16 covers HiLo and sequences for batching. string properties nvarchar(max) on SQL Server ⚠ Can't be used as an index key. Larger memory grants. Worse plans. decimal properties decimal(18,2) , with a warning ⚠ May silently truncate values. Configure precision explicitly. Foreign key properties An index is created automatically ✅ Joins and FK lookups are indexed. Enums Stored as int ✅ Compact. Strings are more readable but larger. Nullable reference types enabled string → NOT NULL , string? → NULL ✅ Accurate nullability lets EF generate simpler SQL."},{"l":3,"s":"03-modeling-fundamentals","t":"Modeling Fundamentals","a":"data-annotations-vs-fluent-api","h":"Data annotations vs Fluent API","x":"Data annotations vs Fluent API Data annotations public class Product { public int Id { get; set; } [Required, MaxLength(32)] public string Sku { get; set; } = \"\"; [Precision(10, 2)] public decimal Price { get; set; } } Fluent API modelBuilder.Entity<Product>(b => { b.Property(p => p.Sku) .IsRequired() .HasMaxLength(32); b.Property(p => p.Price) .HasPrecision(10, 2); }); The Fluent API is more powerful: indexes, filters, table splitting, conversions and query filters are Fluent-only. It also keeps persistence concerns out of your domain classes. A common team convention is Fluent API only, one configuration class per entity : public class ProductConfiguration : IEntityTypeConfiguration<Product> { public void Configure(EntityTypeBuilder<Product> b) { b.ToTable(\"Products\"); b.HasKey(p => p.Id); b.Property(p => p.Sku).HasMaxLength(32).IsUnicode(false); // varchar(32) b.Property(p => p.Name).HasMaxLength(200); // nvarchar(200) b.Property(p => p.Description).HasMaxLength(4000); b.Property(p => p.Price).HasPrecision(10, 2); b.HasIndex(p => p.Sku).IsUnique(); b.HasOne(p => p.Category) .WithMany(c => c.Products) .HasForeignKey(p => p.CategoryId) .OnDelete(DeleteBehavior.Restrict); } } // In ShopContext.OnModelCreating: modelBuilder.ApplyConfigurationsFromAssembly(typeof(ShopContext).Assembly);"},{"l":3,"s":"03-modeling-fundamentals","t":"Modeling Fundamentals","a":"column-types-and-why-they-matter","h":"Column types and why they matter","x":"Column types and why they matter Strings: always set a max length nvarchar(max) columns are stored differently. SQL Server can't use them as index key columns, and the optimizer has to assume large values when sizing memory grants. Use HasMaxLength for anything with a natural limit. Use IsUnicode(false) ( varchar ) for ASCII-only data such as SKUs, codes and hashes. It halves storage. Parameter type mismatch: a classic index killer If a column is varchar but the parameter is sent as nvarchar , SQL Server must convert the column side and may stop using its index. EF sizes and types parameters from the model, so configure IsUnicode(false) in the model. Don't leave the database and the model out of sync. Model-wide defaults with ConfigureConventions Instead of repeating configuration per property, set defaults once. This is pre-convention model configuration : protected override void ConfigureConventions(ModelConfigurationBuilder configurationBuilder) { configurationBuilder.Properties<string>().HaveMaxLength(256); configurationBuilder.Properties<decimal>().HavePrecision(18, 2); configurationBuilder.Properties<DateTime>().HaveColumnType(\"datetime2(3)\"); configurationBuilder.Properties<OrderStatus>().HaveConversion<string>().HaveMaxLength(16); } Individual entity configuration still wins, so Description can override the 256 default with HasMaxLength(4000) ."},{"l":3,"s":"03-modeling-fundamentals","t":"Modeling Fundamentals","a":"relationships","h":"Relationships","x":"Relationships Define relationships explicitly when conventions are ambiguous, and choose delete behaviour on purpose: modelBuilder.Entity<Order>(b => { b.HasOne(o => o.Customer) .WithMany(c => c.Orders) .HasForeignKey(o => o.CustomerId) .OnDelete(DeleteBehavior.Restrict); // don't cascade-delete order history b.HasMany(o => o.Lines) .WithOne() .HasForeignKey(l => l.OrderId) .OnDelete(DeleteBehavior.Cascade); // lines belong to the order }); Expose foreign key properties Having CustomerId as a real property, not just the Customer navigation, lets you filter and set relationships without loading the related entity: order.CustomerId = 42 instead of order.Customer = await db.Customers.FindAsync(42) . That saves a whole query."},{"l":3,"s":"03-modeling-fundamentals","t":"Modeling Fundamentals","a":"keys","h":"Keys","x":"Keys int / bigint identity : compact, sequential, fast to index. The default choice. GUID : globally unique and generated client-side, but random GUIDs fragment clustered indexes. If you use GUIDs, prefer sequential ones. EF's SQL Server provider generates sequential GUIDs client-side by default for key properties. In .NET 9+, Guid.CreateVersion7() gives time-ordered GUIDs. Natural keys (e.g. Sku ): usually better as a unique index than as the primary key, because they can change and are wider."},{"l":3,"s":"03-modeling-fundamentals","t":"Modeling Fundamentals","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways Conventions are a starting point, not a design. Check what EF generated with Model.ToDebugString() . Always bound string lengths, set decimal precision, and use IsUnicode(false) for ASCII data. EF indexes foreign keys automatically. Every other index is your job. Expose foreign-key properties so you can set relationships without loading the related entity."},{"l":3,"s":"03-modeling-fundamentals","t":"Modeling Fundamentals","a":"exercise","h":"Exercise","x":"Exercise 🛠 Tighten the model Move each entity's configuration into an IEntityTypeConfiguration<T> class. Add ConfigureConventions with sensible string, decimal and DateTime defaults. Make Sku and Email non-Unicode with a unique index. Run dotnet ef migrations add TightenModel and read the generated migration. Check that every nvarchar(max) you didn't intend is gone. Hint: review the model EF built Print db.Model.ToDebugString() to see every entity, property, type mapping, key and index EF knows about. It's a quick way to catch unintended conventions."},{"l":3,"s":"03-modeling-fundamentals","t":"Modeling Fundamentals","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz What does an unconfigured string property map to on SQL Server? A. varchar(255) B. nvarchar(450) C. nvarchar(max) D. text Unbounded nvarchar(max) can't be an index key column, so configure max lengths. (Key columns default to nvarchar(450) .) Does EF Core create indexes for foreign keys by convention? A. Yes, an index is created for each foreign key B. No, you must add them manually C. Only on SQL Server D. Only for required relationships EF creates FK indexes by convention. Other indexes, like those for WHERE and ORDER BY columns, are up to you (Lesson 14). Where do you configure model-wide defaults such as \"all strings are max 256\"? A. OnConfiguring B. ConfigureConventions C. OnModelCreating with a loop over every entity D. appsettings.json Pre-convention configuration in ConfigureConventions applies before conventions run, and per-property config can still override it."},{"l":3,"s":"03-modeling-fundamentals","t":"Modeling Fundamentals","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Creating and configuring a model Model bulk configuration (ConfigureConventions) Relationships"},{"l":4,"s":"04-querying-fundamentals","t":"Querying Fundamentals","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Querying Fundamentals Knowing where a query runs is the foundation of EF Core performance: in the database, or in your application's memory. This lesson covers deferred execution, IQueryable versus IEnumerable , client evaluation and async. ⏱ 30 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Understand where and when a query runs : in the database or in memory, now or later, and choose LINQ operators that keep the work on the database server. Why it matters: One misplaced ToList() or IEnumerable can turn a 1-row query into a full table download. This is the most common and most expensive beginner mistake."},{"l":4,"s":"04-querying-fundamentals","t":"Querying Fundamentals","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Tell when a query executes, and avoid executing it twice. Keep filtering, sorting and aggregation in the database. Recognize accidental client-side evaluation. Use async queries, streaming and the right terminal operators."},{"l":4,"s":"04-querying-fundamentals","t":"Querying Fundamentals","a":"deferred-execution","h":"Deferred execution","x":"Deferred execution An IQueryable<T> is a description of a query. Each operator ( Where , OrderBy , Select ) adds to the expression tree. Nothing runs until a terminal operation enumerates it: Materializers: ToList , ToArray , ToDictionary (and their async versions) Single-result operators: First , Single , Any , Count , Sum , Max … foreach / await foreach IQueryable<Order> query = db.Orders.Where(o => o.Status == OrderStatus.Paid); if (minTotal is not null) query = query.Where(o => o.Total >= minTotal); // composable: still no SQL if (sortByDate) query = query.OrderByDescending(o => o.PlacedUtc); var page = await query.Take(50).ToListAsync(); // ONE SQL query here Enumerating twice runs the query twice var paid = db.Orders.Where(o => o.Status == OrderStatus.Paid); var count = paid.Count(); // query 1: SELECT COUNT(*) foreach (var o in paid) { } // query 2: SELECT ... var list = paid.ToList(); // query 3: SELECT ... Materialize once with ToList() if you need the data several times. If you only need a count, ask the database for the count and nothing else."},{"l":4,"s":"04-querying-fundamentals","t":"Querying Fundamentals","a":"iqueryable-vs-ienumerable","h":"IQueryable vs IEnumerable","x":"IQueryable vs IEnumerable This is the most expensive beginner mistake. Once a query becomes an IEnumerable<T> , every later operator runs in memory , on all the rows already fetched. ❌ Filters in memory IEnumerable<Order> GetOrders() => db.Orders; var big = GetOrders() .Where(o => o.Total > 1000) // LINQ-to-Objects! .ToList(); // SQL: SELECT * FROM Orders (all rows!) ✅ Filters in SQL IQueryable<Order> GetOrders() => db.Orders; var big = await GetOrders() .Where(o => o.Total > 1000) .ToListAsync(); // SQL: ... WHERE [o].[Total] > 1000.0 The same thing happens with AsEnumerable() , with a ToList() too early in the chain, and with repository methods that return IEnumerable<T> or List<T> when callers still need to filter."},{"l":4,"s":"04-querying-fundamentals","t":"Querying Fundamentals","a":"client-evaluation","h":"Client evaluation","x":"Client evaluation EF Core 3.0 and later throw an exception if a Where or OrderBy contains something that can't be translated to SQL. That's good: it stops accidental full-table downloads. // Throws InvalidOperationException: \"The LINQ expression ... could not be translated\" var vip = db.Customers.Where(c => IsVip(c)).ToList(); static bool IsVip(Customer c) => c.Email.EndsWith(\"@bigcorp.com\"); Fix it by writing the logic as a translatable expression: var vip = await db.Customers.Where(c => c.Email.EndsWith(\"@bigcorp.com\")).ToListAsync(); There is one exception: client evaluation is allowed in the final Select , because it only runs on rows already fetched: var dtos = await db.Customers .Select(c => new { c.Id, Display = FormatName(c.Name) }) // FormatName runs in .NET: OK .ToListAsync(); Explicit client evaluation If you really must evaluate something in memory, make it obvious: filter as much as possible in SQL, then call AsEnumerable() or ToListAsync() , then apply the client-side logic."},{"l":4,"s":"04-querying-fundamentals","t":"Querying Fundamentals","a":"picking-the-right-operator","h":"Picking the right operator","x":"Picking the right operator You want… Use Avoid Does any row exist? AnyAsync(predicate) → EXISTS CountAsync() > 0 , or ToList().Any() Exactly one row by unique key SingleOrDefaultAsync (fetches TOP 2 to verify) or FirstOrDefaultAsync (TOP 1) Where(...).ToList().FirstOrDefault() Entity by primary key FindAsync(id) : checks the change tracker first and may skip the database entirely Nothing wrong with FirstOrDefaultAsync , but it always queries Aggregates SumAsync , MaxAsync , GroupBy(...).Select(g => new { g.Key, Count = g.Count() }) Loading rows and aggregating in memory Left outer join LeftJoin(...) (new LINQ operator in .NET 10, translated by EF 10). Navigation properties are usually simpler. The GroupJoin + SelectMany + DefaultIfEmpty pattern (it still works, but it's hard to read) Count of children Select(c => new { c.Id, OrderCount = c.Orders.Count() }) Include(c => c.Orders) then .Orders.Count"},{"l":4,"s":"04-querying-fundamentals","t":"Querying Fundamentals","a":"async-scalability-not-speed","h":"Async: scalability, not speed","x":"Async: scalability, not speed Async queries ( ToListAsync , FirstOrDefaultAsync , SaveChangesAsync ) don't make a single query faster. They free the thread while waiting on I/O, so a web server can handle more concurrent requests with fewer threads. Use async in web apps and services, and pass the CancellationToken through so abandoned requests stop their queries. app.MapGet(\"/customers/{id:int}/orders\", async (int id, ShopContext db, CancellationToken ct) => await db.Orders.Where(o => o.CustomerId == id).ToListAsync(ct)); Known SqlClient caveat With Microsoft.Data.SqlClient , reading very large text or binary columns (several MB of nvarchar(max) or varbinary(max) ) asynchronously has been much slower than reading them synchronously. If you hit this, project those columns away, or read them separately in sync code."},{"l":4,"s":"04-querying-fundamentals","t":"Querying Fundamentals","a":"buffering-vs-streaming","h":"Buffering vs streaming","x":"Buffering vs streaming ToListAsync() buffers : all rows are loaded into memory before you see the first one. To process a large result set row by row with constant memory, stream it: await foreach (var order in db.Orders.AsNoTracking().Where(o => o.Status == OrderStatus.Paid).AsAsyncEnumerable()) { await exporter.WriteAsync(order); // one row in memory at a time } Streaming keeps the connection open for the whole loop. Keep the loop body fast, and don't run other queries on the same context inside it unless MARS is enabled. Streaming with tracking still accumulates entities in the change tracker, so use AsNoTracking() (Lesson 10)."},{"l":4,"s":"04-querying-fundamentals","t":"Querying Fundamentals","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways Queries are deferred: nothing runs until enumeration, and every enumeration runs the query again. Stay on IQueryable until filtering, sorting and aggregation are expressed. IEnumerable switches to in-memory LINQ. Untranslatable filters throw (EF Core 3+). Client evaluation is only allowed in the final projection. Pick precise operators ( Any , Count , Sum , FindAsync ). Use async for scalability, and stream large results."},{"l":4,"s":"04-querying-fundamentals","t":"Querying Fundamentals","a":"exercise","h":"Exercise","x":"Exercise 🛠 Spot the hidden queries How many SQL queries does this code run, and how much data does it fetch? Rewrite it. public List<Customer> GetCustomers() => db.Customers.ToList(); var customers = GetCustomers(); var withEmail = customers.Where(c => c.Email.Contains(\"@shop.test\")).ToList(); var hasAny = withEmail.Count() > 0; var total = db.Orders.ToList().Where(o => o.Status == OrderStatus.Paid).Sum(o => o.Total); Show solution The original runs 2 queries, but it downloads every customer and every order. Rewritten: IQueryable<Customer> Customers() => db.Customers; var hasAny = await Customers().AnyAsync(c => c.Email.Contains(\"@shop.test\")); // EXISTS(...) var total = await db.Orders.Where(o => o.Status == OrderStatus.Paid) .SumAsync(o => o.Total); // SELECT SUM(...) Two queries that each return one value, instead of two full table scans pulled into memory."},{"l":4,"s":"04-querying-fundamentals","t":"Querying Fundamentals","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz A method returns IEnumerable<Order> from db.Orders . The caller adds .Where(o => o.Total > 100) . Where does the filter run? A. In SQL B. In memory, after all orders are fetched C. EF throws an exception D. It depends on the provider IEnumerable switches to LINQ-to-Objects, so every row is fetched first. Where is client-side evaluation allowed in EF Core 3+? A. In Where B. In OrderBy C. In the top-level (final) Select projection D. Nowhere Only the final projection can run arbitrary .NET code. Untranslatable filters throw. What's the main benefit of ToListAsync() over ToList() in a web API? A. The query runs faster in SQL Server B. Less data is transferred C. Results are cached D. Threads are released while waiting on I/O, improving scalability Async improves throughput under concurrency, not single-query latency."},{"l":4,"s":"04-querying-fundamentals","t":"Querying Fundamentals","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Client vs server evaluation Efficient querying Asynchronous programming"},{"l":5,"s":"05-dbcontext-configuration","t":"DbContext Configuration in Depth","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"DbContext Configuration in Depth Lesson 2 got you running. This lesson explains how a DbContext actually receives its configuration, so you can set it up correctly in web apps, background workers, the dotnet ef tools and tests. ⏱ 35 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Understand every path by which configuration reaches a DbContext : dependency injection, OnConfiguring , factories, strongly-typed options and design-time factories. Then choose the right one for each kind of application. Why it matters: most \"it works on my machine\" EF problems are configuration problems. Typical examples are a context created without a provider, settings applied in two places that override each other, migrations that can't find the context, and lifetimes that leak state. Once you can see the whole configuration pipeline, these become easy to diagnose."},{"l":5,"s":"05-dbcontext-configuration","t":"DbContext Configuration in Depth","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Explain how DbContextOptions<T> is built and in what order configuration runs. Pick between AddDbContext , OnConfiguring , AddDbContextFactory and manual construction. Bind database settings from appsettings.json with validated, strongly-typed options. Configure several DbContexts in one application, each with its own migrations. Make the dotnet ef tools work with IDesignTimeDbContextFactory<T> ."},{"l":5,"s":"05-dbcontext-configuration","t":"DbContext Configuration in Depth","a":"the-configuration-pipeline","h":"The configuration pipeline","x":"The configuration pipeline A DbContext is configured through a DbContextOptionsBuilder . The finished, immutable DbContextOptions<TContext> holds the provider (SQL Server, PostgreSQL…), the connection, logging, interceptors and behaviour switches. Configuration can come from two places: Outside the context : the delegate passed to AddDbContext , or a DbContextOptionsBuilder you build yourself and pass to the constructor. Inside the context : the OnConfiguring override, which runs after the external options are applied, every time a context instance is initialized. public class ShopContext : DbContext { public ShopContext(DbContextOptions<ShopContext> options) : base(options) { } protected override void OnConfiguring(DbContextOptionsBuilder optionsBuilder) { // Only fall back to a local database if nobody configured us from outside if (!optionsBuilder.IsConfigured) { optionsBuilder.UseSqlite(\"Data Source=shop-dev.db\"); } } } Two sources, one winner If both AddDbContext and OnConfiguring set the same option, OnConfiguring runs last and wins. Guard any fallback configuration in OnConfiguring with IsConfigured , or, better, don't use OnConfiguring at all in applications that use DI."},{"l":5,"s":"05-dbcontext-configuration","t":"DbContext Configuration in Depth","a":"choosing-how-to-configure","h":"Choosing how to configure","x":"Choosing how to configure Approach When to use Lifetime AddDbContext<T> ASP.NET Core and most hosted apps: one context per request or scope Scoped (default) AddDbContextFactory<T> Blazor Server, background services, parallel work, any code that needs several short-lived contexts You control it: await using var db = factory.CreateDbContext() AddDbContextPool<T> / AddPooledDbContextFactory<T> High-throughput services (Lesson 20) Pooled instances OnConfiguring only Small console tools, samples, quick prototypes new ShopContext() Manual DbContextOptionsBuilder Tests, benchmarks, tools without DI new ShopContext(options) Resolving services while configuring The overload of AddDbContext that takes the IServiceProvider lets you pull interceptors, configuration or environment information from DI: builder.Services.AddSingleton<SlowQueryInterceptor>(); builder.Services.AddDbContext<ShopContext>((sp, options) => { var db = sp.GetRequiredService<IOptions<DatabaseOptions>>().Value; options.UseSqlServer(db.ConnectionString, sql => sql.CommandTimeout(db.CommandTimeoutSeconds)) .AddInterceptors(sp.GetRequiredService<SlowQueryInterceptor>()); });"},{"l":5,"s":"05-dbcontext-configuration","t":"DbContext Configuration in Depth","a":"strongly-typed-validated-settings","h":"Strongly-typed, validated settings","x":"Strongly-typed, validated settings Scattering Configuration[\"...\"] lookups and magic numbers through Program.cs makes configuration hard to review and easy to break. Bind a settings class once, validate it, and fail at startup instead of at the first query: { \"Database\": { \"ConnectionString\": \"Server=.;Database=Shop;Trusted_Connection=True;TrustServerCertificate=True\", \"CommandTimeoutSeconds\": 30, \"MaxRetryCount\": 5, \"EnableSensitiveDataLogging\": false } } public sealed class DatabaseOptions { public const string Section = \"Database\"; [Required] public string ConnectionString { get; init; } = \"\"; [Range(1, 600)] public int CommandTimeoutSeconds { get; init; } = 30; [Range(0, 10)] public int MaxRetryCount { get; init; } = 5; public bool EnableSensitiveDataLogging { get; init; } } builder.Services.AddOptions<DatabaseOptions>() .Bind(builder.Configuration.GetSection(DatabaseOptions.Section)) .ValidateDataAnnotations() .ValidateOnStart(); // app refuses to start with bad settings builder.Services.AddDbContext<ShopContext>((sp, options) => { var db = sp.GetRequiredService<IOptions<DatabaseOptions>>().Value; options.UseSqlServer(db.ConnectionString, sql => { sql.CommandTimeout(db.CommandTimeoutSeconds); if (db.MaxRetryCount > 0) sql.EnableRetryOnFailure(db.MaxRetryCount); }); if (db.EnableSensitiveDataLogging) options.EnableSensitiveDataLogging(); }); Keep secrets out of appsettings.json The example above shows the shape of the settings. The real connection string, especially one containing a password, belongs in user-secrets, environment variables or a vault. Lesson 6 covers this."},{"l":5,"s":"05-dbcontext-configuration","t":"DbContext Configuration in Depth","a":"multiple-dbcontexts","h":"Multiple DbContexts","x":"Multiple DbContexts Large applications often split the model into several contexts, for example one per bounded context (Sales, Catalog, Identity). Smaller contexts build faster and keep teams independent. Each context can have its own schema and migrations history: builder.Services.AddDbContext<SalesContext>(o => o.UseSqlServer(connStr, sql => sql.MigrationsHistoryTable(\"__EFMigrationsHistory\", \"sales\"))); builder.Services.AddDbContext<CatalogContext>(o => o.UseSqlServer(connStr, sql => sql.MigrationsHistoryTable(\"__EFMigrationsHistory\", \"catalog\"))); public class SalesContext(DbContextOptions<SalesContext> options) : DbContext(options) { protected override void OnModelCreating(ModelBuilder b) => b.HasDefaultSchema(\"sales\"); } dotnet ef migrations add InitialSales --context SalesContext --output-dir Migrations/Sales dotnet ef migrations add InitialCatalog --context CatalogContext --output-dir Migrations/Catalog Use the generic options type With more than one context, each constructor must take DbContextOptions<TContext> for its own type, not the non-generic DbContextOptions . Otherwise DI can hand one context the other context's options."},{"l":5,"s":"05-dbcontext-configuration","t":"DbContext Configuration in Depth","a":"design-time-configuration-dotnet-ef","h":"Design-time configuration (dotnet ef)","x":"Design-time configuration (dotnet ef) Commands like dotnet ef migrations add need to create your context without running your app normally . The tools try, in order: An IDesignTimeDbContextFactory<TContext> implementation in the project. The application's host: they call your Program and read the context from its service provider. A parameterless constructor on the context. When startup does things the tools can't handle (it needs secrets, connects to other services, or lives in another project), add a design-time factory: public class ShopContextDesignTimeFactory : IDesignTimeDbContextFactory<ShopContext> { public ShopContext CreateDbContext(string[] args) { var options = new DbContextOptionsBuilder<ShopContext>() .UseSqlServer(Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(\"SHOP_DESIGNTIME_CONNECTION\") ?? \"Server=.;Database=Shop;Trusted_Connection=True;TrustServerCertificate=True\", sql => sql.MigrationsAssembly(\"Shop.Migrations\")) .Options; return new ShopContext(options); } } MigrationsAssembly tells EF to store migrations in a separate project. That's useful for keeping migrations out of the domain assembly, or for maintaining separate migration sets per provider."},{"l":5,"s":"05-dbcontext-configuration","t":"DbContext Configuration in Depth","a":"common-configuration-errors-decoded","h":"Common configuration errors decoded","x":"Common configuration errors decoded Error message Usual cause No database provider has been configured for this DbContext The context was created with new without options, or OnConfiguring doesn't call UseXxx . Unable to create a DbContext of type '…' at design time The tools can't build your host. Add an IDesignTimeDbContextFactory . Cannot consume scoped service 'ShopContext' from singleton '…' A singleton (often a hosted service) injects the context. Use a factory. A second operation was started on this context instance… Concurrent use of one context, such as Task.WhenAll or a missing await . The DbContextOptions passed to the X constructor must be a DbContextOptions<X> Multiple contexts using the non-generic options type."},{"l":5,"s":"05-dbcontext-configuration","t":"DbContext Configuration in Depth","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways Configuration flows from the outside (DI or options), then OnConfiguring , which runs last. Guard fallbacks with IsConfigured . Bind database settings to a validated options class and use ValidateOnStart to fail fast. Each context in a multi-context app takes DbContextOptions<TContext> and can have its own schema and migrations history. IDesignTimeDbContextFactory makes the dotnet ef tools independent of your app's startup."},{"l":5,"s":"05-dbcontext-configuration","t":"DbContext Configuration in Depth","a":"exercise","h":"Exercise","x":"Exercise 🛠 Production-grade configuration Create DatabaseOptions , bind it with ValidateOnStart , and configure ShopContext from it. Delete the CommandTimeoutSeconds value, set it to 0 , and start the app. Confirm it refuses to start, with a clear message. Split out a small CatalogContext (Products, Categories) with its own schema and history table, and create a migration for each context. Add a design-time factory and run dotnet ef dbcontext info --context ShopContext . What you should see With an invalid value the host throws OptionsValidationException at startup, naming the failing property. That's much better than a timeout exception at 3 a.m. dotnet ef dbcontext info prints the provider, database name and options in use. It's a quick way to confirm which configuration the tools picked up."},{"l":5,"s":"05-dbcontext-configuration","t":"DbContext Configuration in Depth","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz Both AddDbContext and OnConfiguring set a command timeout. Which value is used? A. The AddDbContext value B. The OnConfiguring value, because it runs after the external options C. The smaller of the two D. EF throws an exception OnConfiguring runs last. Guard fallbacks with optionsBuilder.IsConfigured . What does ValidateOnStart() give you for database settings? A. Faster queries B. Automatic retries C. Encryption of the connection string D. The app fails at startup with a clear error instead of failing at the first database call Fail fast: configuration errors surface during deployment, not under user traffic. When do the dotnet ef tools use an IDesignTimeDbContextFactory<T> ? A. Whenever one exists. It takes precedence over building your application host. B. Never in .NET 10 C. Only for SQLite D. Only when running the app The factory is checked first, which makes design-time creation independent of your app's startup code."},{"l":5,"s":"05-dbcontext-configuration","t":"DbContext Configuration in Depth","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading DbContext lifetime, configuration and initialization Design-time DbContext creation Options pattern in .NET Using a separate migrations project"},{"l":6,"s":"06-connection-strings-and-secrets","t":"Connection Strings, Secrets & Connection Pooling","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Connection Strings, Secrets & Connection Pooling The connection string is the most important line of EF configuration. It decides security, resiliency, pooling behaviour and even how your app shows up in database monitoring. This lesson covers how to write, store and tune it. ⏱ 35 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Learn to write connection strings on purpose : know what each important keyword does, keep credentials out of source control (ideally with no password at all), and understand ADO.NET connection pooling well enough to configure and troubleshoot it. Why it matters: a leaked connection string is a security incident. A wrong timeout or pool setting causes outages under load. A missing Application Name makes production problems much harder to trace. All of these are fixed in configuration, not code."},{"l":6,"s":"06-connection-strings-and-secrets","t":"Connection Strings, Secrets & Connection Pooling","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Read and write SQL Server and PostgreSQL connection strings, keyword by keyword. Build connection strings safely in code with connection string builders. Store secrets with user-secrets, environment variables and Azure Key Vault, and go passwordless with managed identity. Explain how connection pooling works, and diagnose pool exhaustion."},{"l":6,"s":"06-connection-strings-and-secrets","t":"Connection Strings, Secrets & Connection Pooling","a":"anatomy-of-a-sql-server-connection-string","h":"Anatomy of a SQL Server connection string","x":"Anatomy of a SQL Server connection string Server=tcp:shop-sql.database.windows.net,1433;Database=Shop; Authentication=Active Directory Default;Encrypt=True; Application Name=Shop.Api;Connect Timeout=30;Max Pool Size=200; Keyword Default What it does / guidance Server / Data Source (required) Host, instance and port. tcp: forces TCP. Database / Initial Catalog Login's default DB Always set it explicitly. Authentication SQL / Windows Active Directory Default , Active Directory Managed Identity and others enable passwordless Entra ID auth. Encrypt True (Microsoft.Data.SqlClient 4+) Keep it on. Local dev against a self-signed certificate needs TrustServerCertificate=True . Never use that in production. Application Name A generic SqlClient name Shows in sys.dm_exec_sessions , Query Store and Extended Events. Set it for every app. Connect Timeout 15 s Time allowed to open a connection. This isn't the query timeout, which is EF's CommandTimeout . Max Pool Size / Min Pool Size 100 / 0 Pool limits per unique connection string. See below. MultipleActiveResultSets False MARS allows several open readers on one connection. It adds overhead, so enable it only if you need it. ApplicationIntent=ReadOnly ReadWrite Routes to readable secondaries (Always On, Azure SQL read scale-out). Use it for a read-only context. MultiSubnetFailover False Faster failover for availability-group listeners across subnets. ConnectRetryCount / ConnectRetryInterval 1 / 10 s Idle connection resiliency : transparently reconnects broken idle connections. It complements EF's EnableRetryOnFailure . PostgreSQL (Npgsql) equivalents Host=shop-pg.postgres.database.azure.com;Port=5432;Database=shop;Username=shop_api; SSL Mode=Require;Application Name=Shop.Api;Maximum Pool Size=100;Timeout=15;Command Timeout=30 Npgsql recommends configuring connections through an NpgsqlDataSource and passing that to EF. It's the central place for pooling, authentication callbacks, logging and type mappings: var dataSource = new NpgsqlDataSourceBuilder(builder.Configuration.GetConnectionString(\"Shop\")).Build(); builder.Services.AddDbContext<ShopContext>(o => o.UseNpgsql(dataSource));"},{"l":6,"s":"06-connection-strings-and-secrets","t":"Connection Strings, Secrets & Connection Pooling","a":"building-connection-strings-safely","h":"Building connection strings safely","x":"Building connection strings safely Never concatenate connection strings from user input. Use the provider's builder, which escapes values and validates keywords: var csb = new SqlConnectionStringBuilder(builder.Configuration.GetConnectionString(\"Shop\")) { ApplicationName = \"Shop.Api\", MaxPoolSize = 200, Encrypt = SqlConnectionEncryptOption.Mandatory }; options.UseSqlServer(csb.ConnectionString);"},{"l":6,"s":"06-connection-strings-and-secrets","t":"Connection Strings, Secrets & Connection Pooling","a":"storing-secrets","h":"Storing secrets","x":"Storing secrets Never commit credentials Passwords in appsettings.json end up in git history, container images and CI logs forever. Assume anything committed is public. Development: user-secrets dotnet user-secrets init dotnet user-secrets set \"ConnectionStrings:Shop\" \"Server=.;Database=Shop;User Id=dev;Password=…;TrustServerCertificate=True\" Secrets live in your user profile, outside the repository, and are loaded automatically in the Development environment. Servers and containers: environment variables The .NET configuration system maps __ (double underscore) to : , so the variable below overrides ConnectionStrings:Shop : export ConnectionStrings__Shop=\"Server=…;Database=Shop;…\" Cloud: a secret store // Azure Key Vault configuration provider (Azure.Extensions.AspNetCore.Configuration.Secrets) builder.Configuration.AddAzureKeyVault( new Uri(\"https://shop-kv.vault.azure.net/\"), new DefaultAzureCredential()); // a secret named \"ConnectionStrings--Shop\" becomes ConnectionStrings:Shop AWS Secrets Manager, HashiCorp Vault and Kubernetes secrets have equivalent configuration providers or mounting options. Best: no password at all With managed identities (Azure) or IAM authentication (AWS RDS), the app authenticates with its platform identity. There's no secret to leak or rotate: Server=tcp:shop-sql.database.windows.net;Database=Shop;Authentication=Active Directory Default;Encrypt=True Active Directory Default uses DefaultAzureCredential : your developer login locally, the managed identity in Azure. For Azure Database for PostgreSQL, Npgsql's data source builder can fetch short-lived Entra tokens as passwords through a periodic password provider."},{"l":6,"s":"06-connection-strings-and-secrets","t":"Connection Strings, Secrets & Connection Pooling","a":"connection-pooling-explained","h":"Connection pooling explained","x":"Connection pooling explained Opening a physical connection involves TCP, TLS and authentication handshakes, which take milliseconds or more. ADO.NET providers therefore keep a pool of open connections: Open() borrows an idle connection from the pool, or creates one if the pool is below Max Pool Size . Close() or Dispose() returns it to the pool after resetting session state. There is one pool per distinct connection string (and, for integrated auth, per identity). When the pool is full, Open() waits up to Connect Timeout , then throws. EF opens a connection just before each command and closes it right after (unless you hold a transaction or open it explicitly), so connections spend very little time out of the pool. Pool fragmentation Building a different connection string per tenant or per user creates a separate pool for each. Hundreds of small pools waste connections and defeat pooling. Prefer one connection string plus tenant filtering (Lesson 24), or a bounded set of tenant databases. Diagnosing pool exhaustion System.InvalidOperationException: Timeout expired. The timeout period elapsed prior to obtaining a connection from the pool. This may have occurred because all pooled connections were in use and max pool size was reached. Raising Max Pool Size is rarely the fix. Look for what is holding connections: Long transactions, or long-running streaming queries (Lessons 4 and 17). Contexts or connections that aren't disposed (a missing using , a long-lived context). Sync-over-async ( .Result , .Wait() ) causing thread-pool starvation. Requests pile up while holding connections. Slow queries. Each request holds its connection longer, so concurrency needs more connections (Little's law: connections ≈ requests per second × seconds per query)."},{"l":6,"s":"06-connection-strings-and-secrets","t":"Connection Strings, Secrets & Connection Pooling","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways Know the important connection string keywords: Encrypt , Application Name , timeouts, pool sizes, ApplicationIntent . Secrets never go in source control. Use user-secrets in dev, environment variables or a vault in prod, and managed identity where possible. There is one connection pool per distinct connection string. Per-tenant strings fragment pools. Pool exhaustion is almost always connections held too long, not a pool that's too small."},{"l":6,"s":"06-connection-strings-and-secrets","t":"Connection Strings, Secrets & Connection Pooling","a":"exercise","h":"Exercise","x":"Exercise 🛠 Secure and observable connections Move your playground connection string to dotnet user-secrets and delete it from appsettings.json . Add Application Name=EfPlayground , run a query, and find your session with SELECT session_id, program_name FROM sys.dm_exec_sessions WHERE program_name = 'EfPlayground' . Set Max Pool Size=5 and fire 50 concurrent requests that each run WAITFOR DELAY '00:00:02' through ExecuteSqlAsync . Observe the pool timeout, then explain it with Little's law. Explanation Five connections, each busy for 2 seconds, give a maximum of about 2.5 queries per second. 50 concurrent requests have to queue. Those waiting longer than Connect Timeout fail. The fix is shorter queries, not a bigger pool."},{"l":6,"s":"06-connection-strings-and-secrets","t":"Connection Strings, Secrets & Connection Pooling","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz What does Connect Timeout in a SQL Server connection string control? A. How long a query may run B. How long an idle connection stays in the pool C. How long opening a connection, including waiting for a pooled one, may take D. The transaction timeout Query duration is EF's CommandTimeout . Connect Timeout covers opening and acquiring a connection. Why is a different connection string per tenant a performance problem? A. Each distinct connection string gets its own connection pool, fragmenting connections B. EF can't cache the model C. It disables encryption D. It disables migrations Pools are keyed by connection string. Many small pools mean many more open connections. Which option avoids storing a database password anywhere? A. appsettings.Production.json B. Environment variables C. A Base64-encoded connection string D. Managed identity, e.g. Authentication=Active Directory Default Environment variables and vaults still hold a secret. Managed identity removes it entirely."},{"l":6,"s":"06-connection-strings-and-secrets","t":"Connection Strings, Secrets & Connection Pooling","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading EF Core: Connection strings SqlClient connection string syntax SQL Server connection pooling Npgsql connection string parameters Safe storage of app secrets in development"},{"l":7,"s":"07-provider-configuration","t":"Provider-Specific Configuration","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Provider-Specific Configuration EF Core's behaviour depends heavily on its database provider. Every option on the provider builder changes compatibility, translations, resiliency or performance. This lesson walks through the options for SQL Server/Azure SQL, PostgreSQL and SQLite that matter most. ⏱ 35 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Configure your database provider deliberately instead of accepting defaults: know which provider-level switches exist, what each one changes in the generated SQL or runtime behaviour, and which provider quirks to plan for. Why it matters: the same LINQ query can produce different SQL, or fail, depending on provider settings like the compatibility level or PostgreSQL version. Many production surprises, such as timestamp errors on PostgreSQL, unsupported functions on older SQL Server versions, or SQLite's limited ALTER TABLE support, are avoided by configuring the provider correctly from day one."},{"l":7,"s":"07-provider-configuration","t":"Provider-Specific Configuration","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Use the provider options builder ( UseSqlServer(conn, sql => …) ) confidently. Configure SQL Server and Azure SQL: compatibility level, timeouts, batching, retries, migrations. Configure PostgreSQL with Npgsql: server version, enums, date/time behaviour, naming. Know SQLite's main limitations and settings."},{"l":7,"s":"07-provider-configuration","t":"Provider-Specific Configuration","a":"two-levels-of-options","h":"Two levels of options","x":"Two levels of options EF configuration has general options on DbContextOptionsBuilder (logging, tracking behaviour, interceptors) and provider options inside the provider's own builder: options .UseSqlServer(connStr, sql => // ← provider options builder { sql.CommandTimeout(30); sql.EnableRetryOnFailure(); }) .UseQueryTrackingBehavior(QueryTrackingBehavior.NoTracking) // ← general option .AddInterceptors(new SlowQueryInterceptor());"},{"l":7,"s":"07-provider-configuration","t":"Provider-Specific Configuration","a":"sql-server-and-azure-sql","h":"SQL Server and Azure SQL","x":"SQL Server and Azure SQL options.UseSqlServer(connStr, sql => { sql.CommandTimeout(30); // seconds per command sql.EnableRetryOnFailure(5, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10), null); // transient fault handling (Lesson 17) sql.MaxBatchSize(100); // statements per SaveChanges round trip (Lesson 16) sql.UseQuerySplittingBehavior(QuerySplittingBehavior.SplitQuery); // Lesson 15 sql.UseCompatibilityLevel(160); // match your server (160 = SQL Server 2022) sql.MigrationsAssembly(\"Shop.Migrations\"); // Lesson 5 sql.MigrationsHistoryTable(\"__EFMigrationsHistory\", \"dbo\"); }); // EF 9+: target Azure SQL explicitly instead of on-premises SQL Server options.UseAzureSql(connStr, sql => sql.EnableRetryOnFailure()); Option Effect UseCompatibilityLevel(n) Tells EF which SQL features it may use. For example, OPENJSON -based translations need level 130+. If your database runs at an older level than EF assumes, some queries fail at runtime with syntax errors. Set it to your database's compatibility level. CommandTimeout(s) The maximum duration per command (default 30 s). Raise it for specific long operations with db.Database.SetCommandTimeout(...) rather than globally. EnableRetryOnFailure Retries known transient errors. Essential on Azure SQL. Changes how manual transactions must be written. MaxBatchSize / MinBatchSize Tune SaveChanges batching. Leave the defaults unless you've measured. UseParameterizedCollectionMode (EF 10) How Contains over a collection is translated (Lesson 21). UseAzureSql (EF 9+) Enables Azure-SQL-specific defaults and features. Newer SQL Server features EF 10 supports the native json data type and the vector type ( SqlVector<float> with EF.Functions.VectorDistance , for AI similarity search) on Azure SQL and SQL Server 2025. With UseAzureSql , or a compatibility level of 170 or higher , EF 10 defaults to the json type for JSON-mapped data. Your next migration will convert existing nvarchar JSON columns to json . Opt out by setting the column type to nvarchar(max) explicitly, or by configuring a lower compatibility level."},{"l":7,"s":"07-provider-configuration","t":"Provider-Specific Configuration","a":"postgresql-npgsql","h":"PostgreSQL (Npgsql)","x":"PostgreSQL (Npgsql) var dsb = new NpgsqlDataSourceBuilder(connStr); var dataSource = dsb.Build(); builder.Services.AddDbContext<ShopContext>(o => o .UseNpgsql(dataSource, npg => { npg.SetPostgresVersion(16, 0); // allow SQL features of your server version npg.EnableRetryOnFailure(); npg.MapEnum<OrderStatus>(\"order_status\"); // native PostgreSQL enum (Npgsql EF 9+) npg.CommandTimeout(30); }) .UseSnakeCaseNamingConvention()); // EFCore.NamingConventions package Topic What to know Date/time Since Npgsql 6, DateTime maps to timestamp with time zone , and writing a DateTime whose Kind isn't Utc throws. Store UTC everywhere ( DateTime.UtcNow ). Use timestamp without time zone only for genuinely local times. Consider NodaTime ( npg.UseNodaTime() ) for precise date/time semantics. Naming PostgreSQL folds unquoted identifiers to lower case. Snake-case naming ( order_lines.unit_price ) makes hand-written SQL much nicer. Server version SetPostgresVersion lets EF use newer SQL features. Set it to the oldest version you deploy to. Types Native arrays, jsonb , ranges and full-text search are first-class. Index them with GIN or GiST indexes, configured with HasMethod(\"gin\") . Concurrency Use the xmin system column as a row version (Lesson 17)."},{"l":7,"s":"07-provider-configuration","t":"Provider-Specific Configuration","a":"sqlite","h":"SQLite","x":"SQLite options.UseSqlite(\"Data Source=shop.db\", sqlite => sqlite.CommandTimeout(30)); Limited ALTER TABLE : many schema changes require rebuilding the table. EF's migrations do this automatically, but review them on large tables. Type affinity : SQLite has few native types. decimal , DateTimeOffset and TimeSpan are stored as text or integers, so some operations on them can't be translated. EF 10 added MIN / MAX / ORDER BY support for decimal on SQLite. Check the provider's limitations page for your version, and plan value converters if you query on these types heavily. Concurrency : one writer at a time. WAL journal mode ( PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL; ) lets readers proceed during writes. Check the current mode with PRAGMA journal_mode; . In-memory databases ( Data Source=:memory: ) live only as long as their connection stays open. That matters for tests (Lesson 9)."},{"l":7,"s":"07-provider-configuration","t":"Provider-Specific Configuration","a":"supporting-several-providers","h":"Supporting several providers","x":"Supporting several providers If the same model must run on more than one database (for example SQL Server in production and SQLite for a desktop edition): Generate separate migration sets per provider , each in its own assembly ( MigrationsAssembly ), because migrations contain provider-specific SQL and types. Keep provider-specific model configuration behind Database.IsSqlServer() / IsNpgsql() checks in OnModelCreating . Test against every provider you ship. Translation differences are real."},{"l":7,"s":"07-provider-configuration","t":"Provider-Specific Configuration","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways Provider options ( UseSqlServer(conn, sql => …) ) change the generated SQL and runtime behaviour. Configure them deliberately. Match UseCompatibilityLevel / SetPostgresVersion to your real server. EF 10 uses the json type at level 170+ or with UseAzureSql . PostgreSQL: store UTC DateTime for timestamptz . Consider snake_case naming and native enums. SQLite has type and ALTER TABLE limitations. Multi-provider apps need one migration set per provider."},{"l":7,"s":"07-provider-configuration","t":"Provider-Specific Configuration","a":"exercise","h":"Exercise","x":"Exercise 🛠 Provider tune-up Run SELECT compatibility_level FROM sys.databases WHERE name = DB_NAME() and set UseCompatibilityLevel to match. Set UseCompatibilityLevel(120) and run db.Orders.Where(o => ids.Contains(o.Id)) with a parameterized collection mode. Compare the SQL with level 160. What changed and why? If you have PostgreSQL, insert an order with DateTime.Now into a timestamptz column and read the exception. Fix it with DateTime.UtcNow . What you should see At compatibility level 120, EF can't use OPENJSON , so translations that rely on it fall back to other forms, such as inlined values. With Npgsql, writing a Local DateTime to timestamptz throws an exception explaining that only UTC is supported."},{"l":7,"s":"07-provider-configuration","t":"Provider-Specific Configuration","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz Why set UseCompatibilityLevel on SQL Server? A. To enable retries B. So EF only generates SQL features your database's compatibility level supports C. To speed up migrations D. To enable MARS EF picks translations (e.g. OPENJSON) based on the configured level. A mismatch causes runtime SQL errors. With Npgsql 6+, what happens when you save DateTime.Now into a timestamptz column? A. It's silently converted to UTC B. It's stored as local time C. It's truncated to the date D. An exception is thrown, because only UTC DateTime values are allowed for timestamptz Npgsql enforces UTC for timestamptz to prevent silent time-zone bugs. You support SQL Server and SQLite with the same model. How should migrations be organized? A. One migration set per provider, each in its own assembly B. One shared migration set C. Migrations aren't supported with SQLite D. Use EnsureCreated for both Migrations contain provider-specific SQL and types, so maintain one set per provider."},{"l":7,"s":"07-provider-configuration","t":"Provider-Specific Configuration","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading SQL Server provider Npgsql EF Core provider Npgsql: Date and time handling SQLite provider limitations Migrations with multiple providers"},{"l":8,"s":"08-model-configuration-at-scale","t":"Model Configuration at Scale","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Model Configuration at Scale Configuring five entities is easy. Configuring two hundred consistently is a design problem. This lesson covers organizing model configuration so it stays correct, reviewable and uniform as the model grows. ⏱ 35 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Learn to structure model configuration for a real, growing codebase : where each kind of configuration belongs, how precedence works, how to apply rules model-wide, and how to seed and inspect the model with confidence. Why it matters: inconsistent configuration causes subtle bugs and slow queries: some strings bounded and others not, some decimals truncated, enums stored differently in different tables. Centralized rules and a clear structure make the correct choice the default, and make deviations obvious in code review."},{"l":8,"s":"08-model-configuration-at-scale","t":"Model Configuration at Scale","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Explain configuration precedence: conventions, data annotations, Fluent API. Organize configuration with IEntityTypeConfiguration<T> and shared base configurations. Apply model-wide rules with ConfigureConventions , including global value converters. Configure schemas, naming, default values and computed columns. Choose between HasData and UseSeeding for seed data."},{"l":8,"s":"08-model-configuration-at-scale","t":"Model Configuration at Scale","a":"precedence-who-wins","h":"Precedence: who wins?","x":"Precedence: who wins? EF builds the model in layers. Later, more explicit layers override earlier ones: Pre-convention configuration ( ConfigureConventions ): defaults for types, applied before conventions run. Conventions : EF's built-in rules (keys named Id , FK discovery…) plus any custom conventions. Data annotations : [MaxLength] , [Required] , [Precision] , etc. Fluent API in OnModelCreating : always wins. Pick one primary style Mixing annotations and Fluent API for the same concern makes it hard to know what's actually configured. Common team rule: use the Fluent API for everything persistence-related, and keep entities free of EF attributes."},{"l":8,"s":"08-model-configuration-at-scale","t":"Model Configuration at Scale","a":"organizing-configuration","h":"Organizing configuration","x":"Organizing configuration Shop.Data/ ShopContext.cs Configurations/ Sales/ OrderConfiguration.cs OrderLineConfiguration.cs Catalog/ ProductConfiguration.cs CategoryConfiguration.cs Common/ AuditableConfiguration.cs Shared column rules belong in an abstract base configuration: public interface IAuditable { DateTime CreatedUtc { get; set; } DateTime? ModifiedUtc { get; set; } } public abstract class AuditableConfiguration<T> : IEntityTypeConfiguration<T> where T : class, IAuditable { public virtual void Configure(EntityTypeBuilder<T> b) { b.Property(e => e.CreatedUtc).HasDefaultValueSql(\"SYSUTCDATETIME()\"); b.Property(e => e.ModifiedUtc); } } public sealed class OrderConfiguration : AuditableConfiguration<Order> { public override void Configure(EntityTypeBuilder<Order> b) { base.Configure(b); b.ToTable(\"Orders\", \"sales\"); b.Property(o => o.Total).HasPrecision(12, 2); b.HasIndex(o => new { o.CustomerId, o.PlacedUtc }); } } // ShopContext.OnModelCreating modelBuilder.ApplyConfigurationsFromAssembly(typeof(ShopContext).Assembly);"},{"l":8,"s":"08-model-configuration-at-scale","t":"Model Configuration at Scale","a":"model-wide-rules-with-configureconventions","h":"Model-wide rules with ConfigureConventions","x":"Model-wide rules with ConfigureConventions protected override void ConfigureConventions(ModelConfigurationBuilder c) { // Type defaults c.Properties<string>().HaveMaxLength(256); c.Properties<decimal>().HavePrecision(18, 2); c.Properties<DateTime>().HaveColumnType(\"datetime2(3)\"); // Enums stored as readable strings everywhere c.Properties<Enum>().HaveConversion<string>().HaveMaxLength(32); // Strongly-typed IDs: one converter for the whole model c.Properties<CustomerId>().HaveConversion<CustomerIdConverter>(); // Never map these types c.IgnoreAny<IDomainEvent>(); } public readonly record struct CustomerId(int Value); public sealed class CustomerIdConverter() : ValueConverter<CustomerId, int>(id => id.Value, v => new CustomerId(v)); Per-property Fluent configuration still overrides these defaults, so Product.Description can use HasMaxLength(4000) while every other string defaults to 256."},{"l":8,"s":"08-model-configuration-at-scale","t":"Model Configuration at Scale","a":"schemas-naming-and-database-generated-values","h":"Schemas, naming and database-generated values","x":"Schemas, naming and database-generated values modelBuilder.HasDefaultSchema(\"shop\"); // all tables → shop.* modelBuilder.Entity<Order>(b => { b.ToTable(\"Orders\", \"sales\", t => t.HasCheckConstraint(\"CK_Orders_Total\", \"[Total] >= 0\")); b.Property(o => o.PlacedUtc).HasDefaultValueSql(\"SYSUTCDATETIME()\"); b.Property(o => o.Status).HasDefaultValue(OrderStatus.Pending); }); modelBuilder.Entity<OrderLine>() .Property<decimal>(\"LineTotal\") // shadow property .HasComputedColumnSql(\"[Quantity] * [UnitPrice]\", stored: true); // persisted computed column Defaults and the CLR default value When a property has a database default, EF only omits it from the INSERT if the property still holds its CLR default (0, null, the first enum member…). If that CLR default is also a valid value you want to save, configure a different sentinel with HasSentinel (EF 8+), or make the property nullable."},{"l":8,"s":"08-model-configuration-at-scale","t":"Model Configuration at Scale","a":"seed-data-hasdata-vs-useseeding","h":"Seed data: HasData vs UseSeeding","x":"Seed data: HasData vs UseSeeding Aspect HasData (model-managed) UseSeeding / UseAsyncSeeding (EF 9+) How it works Seed rows become part of the model. Migrations generate INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE statements to keep them in sync. Your code runs during Migrate() , EnsureCreated() and dotnet ef database update . Keys Must be specified explicitly Any logic you like Best for Small, static reference data: countries, statuses, categories Larger or computed data, data depending on other data, dev and test data // Static reference data modelBuilder.Entity<Category>().HasData( new Category { Id = 1, Name = \"Books\" }, new Category { Id = 2, Name = \"Games\" }); // Runtime seeding (EF 9+). Implement both for tools and app code. options.UseSqlServer(connStr) .UseSeeding((ctx, _) => { if (!ctx.Set<Product>().Any()) { ctx.Set<Product>().Add(new Product { Sku = \"DEMO-1\", Name = \"Demo\", Price = 9.99m, CategoryId = 1 }); ctx.SaveChanges(); } }) .UseAsyncSeeding(async (ctx, _, ct) => { if (!await ctx.Set<Product>().AnyAsync(ct)) { ctx.Set<Product>().Add(new Product { Sku = \"DEMO-1\", Name = \"Demo\", Price = 9.99m, CategoryId = 1 }); await ctx.SaveChangesAsync(ct); } });"},{"l":8,"s":"08-model-configuration-at-scale","t":"Model Configuration at Scale","a":"inspecting-the-result","h":"Inspecting the result","x":"Inspecting the result db.Model.ToDebugString() prints every entity, property, column type, key, index and relationship EF ended up with. dotnet ef dbcontext info shows the provider, database and context options. Review every migration diff. It's the clearest record of what a configuration change does to the database."},{"l":8,"s":"08-model-configuration-at-scale","t":"Model Configuration at Scale","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways Precedence: pre-convention configuration < conventions < data annotations < Fluent API. Organize with IEntityTypeConfiguration<T> and shared base configurations. Apply model-wide rules in ConfigureConventions . Use HasData for small static reference data, and UseSeeding / UseAsyncSeeding (EF 9+) for everything else. Review every migration diff. It shows exactly what a configuration change does."},{"l":8,"s":"08-model-configuration-at-scale","t":"Model Configuration at Scale","a":"exercise","h":"Exercise","x":"Exercise 🛠 Make it consistent Introduce IAuditable and AuditableConfiguration<T> , and apply them to Order and Customer. Add ConfigureConventions rules for strings, decimals and enums-as-strings. Generate a migration and review every changed column. Introduce a CustomerId strongly-typed ID with a global converter, and check that queries like Where(c => c.Id == id) still translate to SQL. Seed the categories with HasData and demo products with UseAsyncSeeding ."},{"l":8,"s":"08-model-configuration-at-scale","t":"Model Configuration at Scale","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz A property has [MaxLength(50)] and HasMaxLength(100) in OnModelCreating . Which applies? A. 50 B. The smaller value C. 100, because the Fluent API overrides data annotations D. Neither. EF throws. Fluent API configuration has the highest precedence. When is HasData the right choice for seeding? A. Small, static reference data with known keys that should be versioned with migrations B. Large generated test datasets C. Data that depends on the current date D. Data loaded from an external API HasData is part of the model and migrations. Use UseSeeding (EF 9+) for dynamic or larger data. Where do you register a value converter that should apply to every property of a type across the model? A. In every entity configuration class B. In OnConfiguring C. With a data annotation on each property D. In ConfigureConventions with Properties<T>().HaveConversion<...>() Pre-convention configuration applies type-wide defaults in one place."},{"l":8,"s":"08-model-configuration-at-scale","t":"Model Configuration at Scale","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Creating and configuring a model Model bulk configuration Data seeding Generated values"},{"l":9,"s":"09-environments-and-testing","t":"Environments, Testing & Configuration Safety","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Environments, Testing & Configuration Safety Development, CI, staging and production each need different EF settings, and you need proof that each configuration works. This lesson covers environment-specific configuration, startup validation, health checks and testing strategies. ⏱ 35 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Learn to vary EF configuration safely across environments and to verify it automatically : debug features on where they help, off where they hurt, misconfiguration caught before traffic arrives, and tests that exercise the real database behaviour. Why it matters: \"worked in dev, broke in prod\" usually means the environments were configured differently in ways nobody tested. Sensitive-data logging left on leaks personal data. Tests on the wrong provider pass while production fails. These are configuration mistakes you can prevent systematically."},{"l":9,"s":"09-environments-and-testing","t":"Environments, Testing & Configuration Safety","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Layer configuration by environment, and know which EF settings belong where. Validate configuration at startup and expose database health checks. Choose a testing strategy: real database in a container, SQLite in-memory, or fakes. Replace the DbContext configuration in integration tests with WebApplicationFactory ."},{"l":9,"s":"09-environments-and-testing","t":"Environments, Testing & Configuration Safety","a":"environment-layering","h":"Environment layering","x":"Environment layering ASP.NET Core loads configuration in layers. Later sources override earlier ones: appsettings.json : shared defaults, no secrets. appsettings.{Environment}.json : per-environment overrides ( Development , Staging , Production ). User secrets (Development only). Environment variables. Command-line arguments. Recommended EF settings per environment Setting Development CI / Test Production SQL command logging Information (see every query) Information, or captured for assertions Warning + slow-query interceptor EnableSensitiveDataLogging ✅ On ✅ On (synthetic data) ❌ Off EnableDetailedErrors ✅ On ✅ On ❌ Off Performance warnings → errors ✅ Throw ✅ Throw (fail the build) Log Retry on failure Optional Off (failures should be visible) ✅ On Migrations Migrate() at startup is fine Apply scripts or a bundle in the pipeline Scripts or bundle, never at app start builder.Services.AddDbContext<ShopContext>((sp, o) => { var env = sp.GetRequiredService<IHostEnvironment>(); o.UseSqlServer(builder.Configuration.GetConnectionString(\"Shop\"), sql => { if (env.IsProduction()) sql.EnableRetryOnFailure(); }); if (!env.IsProduction()) { o.EnableSensitiveDataLogging() .EnableDetailedErrors() .ConfigureWarnings(w => w.Throw( RelationalEventId.MultipleCollectionIncludeWarning, CoreEventId.NavigationLazyLoading)); } });"},{"l":9,"s":"09-environments-and-testing","t":"Environments, Testing & Configuration Safety","a":"fail-fast-validation-and-health-checks","h":"Fail fast: validation and health checks","x":"Fail fast: validation and health checks Validate settings at startup (Lesson 5's ValidateOnStart ), and give the platform a way to ask \"can this instance reach its database?\": // Package: Microsoft.Extensions.Diagnostics.HealthChecks.EntityFrameworkCore builder.Services.AddHealthChecks() .AddDbContextCheck<ShopContext>(\"database\", tags: [\"ready\"]); app.MapHealthChecks(\"/health/ready\", new HealthCheckOptions { Predicate = c => c.Tags.Contains(\"ready\") }); app.MapHealthChecks(\"/health/live\", new HealthCheckOptions { Predicate = _ => false }); // process is up Point the Kubernetes readiness probe, or the load balancer health check, at /health/ready . That way instances with broken configuration never receive traffic, and liveness doesn't restart pods just because the database had a blip."},{"l":9,"s":"09-environments-and-testing","t":"Environments, Testing & Configuration Safety","a":"testing-strategies","h":"Testing strategies","x":"Testing strategies Strategy Fidelity Speed Verdict Real database in a container (Testcontainers) ✅ Exact: same SQL, constraints, collations, translations Seconds to start, then fast The recommended default for data-access tests SQLite in-memory ⚠ Relational, but a different SQL dialect and type system Very fast OK for simple logic if production isn't SQLite. Translation bugs slip through. EF InMemory provider ❌ Not relational: no SQL, no constraints, no transactions, different query semantics Very fast The EF team discourages it for testing. Avoid it. Repository fakes/mocks Tests business logic, not data access Fastest Good for unit tests above the data layer Integration tests with Testcontainers // Packages: Testcontainers.MsSql, Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing, xunit (v2) public sealed class ApiFactory : WebApplicationFactory<Program>, IAsyncLifetime { private readonly MsSqlContainer _db = new MsSqlBuilder() .WithImage(\"mcr.microsoft.com/mssql/server:2022-latest\") // pin the same version as production .Build(); protected override void ConfigureWebHost(IWebHostBuilder builder) { builder.UseEnvironment(\"Testing\"); builder.ConfigureTestServices(services => { // Remove the app's registration, then add one pointing at the container // EF 9/10: remove every registration AddDbContext made, or EF reports // \"Services for database providers ... have been registered\" (two providers) services.RemoveAll<DbContextOptions<ShopContext>>(); services.RemoveAll<IDbContextOptionsConfiguration<ShopContext>>(); services.RemoveAll<ShopContext>(); services.AddDbContext<ShopContext>(o => o.UseSqlServer(_db.GetConnectionString())); }); } public async Task InitializeAsync() { await _db.StartAsync(); using var scope = Services.CreateScope(); await scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<ShopContext>().Database.MigrateAsync(); } // Explicit implementation: WebApplicationFactory already has its own DisposeAsync() async Task IAsyncLifetime.DisposeAsync() { await _db.DisposeAsync(); await base.DisposeAsync(); } } SQLite in-memory: keep the connection open var connection = new SqliteConnection(\"Data Source=:memory:\"); connection.Open(); // the database lives as long as this connection var options = new DbContextOptionsBuilder<ShopContext>().UseSqlite(connection).Options; using (var ctx = new ShopContext(options)) ctx.Database.EnsureCreated(); // ... run tests with new ShopContext(options) ... connection.Dispose(); // database disappears Test the configuration itself Add a cheap test that builds the real host with production-like settings (minus secrets) and resolves ShopContext . Then assert on db.Model or db.Database.ProviderName . It catches broken DI registrations, invalid options and model validation errors on every build."},{"l":9,"s":"09-environments-and-testing","t":"Environments, Testing & Configuration Safety","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways Layer configuration per environment. Debug features on in dev and test, off in production. Validate options at startup and expose a database readiness check (not a liveness check). Test data access against the real database engine (Testcontainers). Avoid the InMemory provider. When replacing the context in integration tests on EF 9/10, remove all of AddDbContext's registrations first."},{"l":9,"s":"09-environments-and-testing","t":"Environments, Testing & Configuration Safety","a":"exercise","h":"Exercise","x":"Exercise 🛠 Environment-proof setup Implement the environment-specific AddDbContext above. Start the app as Development and as Production , and compare the logs. Add AddDbContextCheck with ready and live endpoints. Stop the database and watch /health/ready turn unhealthy while /health/live stays healthy. Write one integration test with Testcontainers that calls a real endpoint and asserts on both the HTTP result and the number of SQL commands (see Lesson 25's command counter)."},{"l":9,"s":"09-environments-and-testing","t":"Environments, Testing & Configuration Safety","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz Why does the EF team discourage the InMemory provider for testing? A. It's slow B. It isn't a relational database, so SQL translation, constraints and transactions behave differently C. It doesn't support async D. It requires Docker Tests can pass on InMemory and fail against the real database. Prefer the real provider via Testcontainers. What must you do to keep a SQLite in-memory database alive across several contexts in a test? A. Use a file path instead B. Call EnsureCreated() on every context C. Open one SqliteConnection and pass that same connection to every context D. Enable MARS An in-memory SQLite database is destroyed when its last connection closes. Which health-check setup avoids restarting healthy pods during a short database outage? A. Database check on the readiness probe only; liveness only checks that the process is up B. Database check on the liveness probe C. No health checks D. Database check on both probes Readiness removes the instance from traffic. Liveness failures restart it, which doesn't help when the database is the problem."},{"l":9,"s":"09-environments-and-testing","t":"Environments, Testing & Configuration Safety","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Testing EF Core applications Choosing a testing strategy Integration tests in ASP.NET Core Health checks in ASP.NET Core Testcontainers for .NET"},{"l":10,"s":"10-tracking-vs-no-tracking","t":"Tracking vs No-Tracking Queries","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Tracking vs No-Tracking Queries By default, every entity EF Core loads is registered in the change tracker and snapshotted. That's essential when you plan to update entities, and pure overhead when you only read them. This lesson shows how to choose. ⏱ 25 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Learn when change tracking is needed and when it's pure overhead , and how to read and update data without paying for tracking you don't use. Why it matters: Tracking costs CPU and memory on every row. Choosing the right tracking mode is often the cheapest speed-up for read-heavy endpoints, and understanding it prevents subtle update bugs."},{"l":10,"s":"10-tracking-vs-no-tracking","t":"Tracking vs No-Tracking Queries","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Explain what tracking costs: memory, snapshots, identity resolution and DetectChanges . Use AsNoTracking , AsNoTrackingWithIdentityResolution and AsTracking . Set a context-wide default tracking behaviour. Update entities without loading them, using Attach and Entry ."},{"l":10,"s":"10-tracking-vs-no-tracking","t":"Tracking vs No-Tracking Queries","a":"what-tracking-does","h":"What tracking does","x":"What tracking does For each entity returned by a tracking query, EF: Looks up its key in the identity map. If an instance with that key is already tracked, EF returns that same instance and throws away the new row data. This is identity resolution. Otherwise it creates an InternalEntityEntry , stores a snapshot of original values, and fixes up navigation properties to other tracked entities. Later, SaveChanges() (and many other APIs) call DetectChanges() , which compares every tracked entity with its snapshot. None of this is needed for read-only scenarios such as API GET endpoints, reports, exports and view models."},{"l":10,"s":"10-tracking-vs-no-tracking","t":"Tracking vs No-Tracking Queries","a":"asnotracking","h":"AsNoTracking","x":"AsNoTracking var products = await db.Products .AsNoTracking() .Where(p => p.CategoryId == categoryId) .ToListAsync(); The SQL is identical. Only the work EF does in .NET changes. Microsoft's benchmarks and many community measurements show no-tracking queries are noticeably faster and allocate much less memory. The gap grows with the number of rows returned. The catch: no identity resolution Without tracking, the same database row returned twice becomes two separate objects . Loading 1,000 orders with Include(o => o.Customer) where all orders belong to 3 customers gives you 1,000 Customer instances, not 3. AsNoTrackingWithIdentityResolution This gives you de-duplicated instances without full tracking. EF uses a temporary identity map for the duration of the query, then discards it: var orders = await db.Orders .AsNoTrackingWithIdentityResolution() .Include(o => o.Customer) .ToListAsync(); // orders sharing a customer point to the SAME Customer object It's slower than plain AsNoTracking but faster than tracking. Use it when you serialize graphs with shared references or need reference equality."},{"l":10,"s":"10-tracking-vs-no-tracking","t":"Tracking vs No-Tracking Queries","a":"choosing-a-tracking-mode","h":"Choosing a tracking mode","x":"Choosing a tracking mode Scenario Use Load, modify, SaveChanges Tracking (the default) Read-only list or detail returned as JSON AsNoTracking() , or better, a projection (Lesson 11) Read-only graph with many shared references AsNoTrackingWithIdentityResolution() Projection to a DTO or anonymous type Nothing needed. Non-entity results aren't tracked. Projections are untracked automatically db.Products.Select(p => new ProductDto(p.Id, p.Name)) is never tracked, because DTOs aren't entities. If a projection contains an entity instance, such as new { Product = p, p.Category.Name } , that entity is tracked in a tracking query."},{"l":10,"s":"10-tracking-vs-no-tracking","t":"Tracking vs No-Tracking Queries","a":"context-wide-default","h":"Context-wide default","x":"Context-wide default For read-heavy services, make no-tracking the default and opt in where you write: builder.Services.AddDbContext<ShopContext>(o => o .UseSqlServer(connStr) .UseQueryTrackingBehavior(QueryTrackingBehavior.NoTracking)); // When you need to update: var order = await db.Orders.AsTracking().SingleAsync(o => o.Id == id); order.Status = OrderStatus.Shipped; await db.SaveChangesAsync(); You can also change it per context instance: db.ChangeTracker.QueryTrackingBehavior = QueryTrackingBehavior.NoTracking; . Read/write separation A common pattern in larger systems is a ReadShopContext with no-tracking defaults, possibly pointed at a read replica, alongside the normal writable context. Query handlers inject the read context and command handlers the write context."},{"l":10,"s":"10-tracking-vs-no-tracking","t":"Tracking vs No-Tracking Queries","a":"updating-without-loading","h":"Updating without loading","x":"Updating without loading If you already know the key and the new values, you don't need a SELECT first: // Update one column of a known row: 1 round trip instead of 2 var product = new Product { Id = productId }; db.Products.Attach(product); // tracked as Unchanged product.Price = newPrice; // only Price becomes Modified await db.SaveChangesAsync(); // UPDATE [Products] SET [Price] = @p0 WHERE [Id] = @p1 For set-based updates of many rows there's an even better tool, ExecuteUpdateAsync (Lesson 16). Careful with Update() db.Update(entity) marks every property as modified and updates all columns, including ones you didn't change, and it walks the whole reachable graph. Prefer Attach plus setting the changed properties, or Entry(e).Property(x => x.Price).IsModified = true ."},{"l":10,"s":"10-tracking-vs-no-tracking","t":"Tracking vs No-Tracking Queries","a":"keeping-the-change-tracker-small","h":"Keeping the change tracker small","x":"Keeping the change tracker small Long-lived contexts with thousands of tracked entities make every DetectChanges slower. When processing in batches: foreach (var batch in ids.Chunk(1000)) { var items = await db.Orders.Where(o => batch.Contains(o.Id)).ToListAsync(); foreach (var o in items) o.Status = OrderStatus.Shipped; await db.SaveChangesAsync(); db.ChangeTracker.Clear(); // forget everything tracked so far }"},{"l":10,"s":"10-tracking-vs-no-tracking","t":"Tracking vs No-Tracking Queries","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways Tracking costs memory and CPU per row. Use it only when you'll modify and save the entities. AsNoTracking for read paths, AsNoTrackingWithIdentityResolution when shared references matter. DTO projections are never tracked. A no-tracking default suits read-heavy services. Update known rows with Attach plus property changes, and clear the change tracker between batches."},{"l":10,"s":"10-tracking-vs-no-tracking","t":"Tracking vs No-Tracking Queries","a":"exercise","h":"Exercise","x":"Exercise 🛠 Measure it Load all 10,000 orders with Include(o => o.Customer) three ways: tracking, AsNoTracking , AsNoTrackingWithIdentityResolution . Time each with Stopwatch (run each a few times and discard the first) and check memory with GC.GetAllocatedBytesForCurrentThread() . Count distinct customer instances: orders.Select(o => o.Customer).Distinct(ReferenceEqualityComparer.Instance).Count() . What to expect No-tracking is fastest and allocates least, but you get about 10,000 Customer instances. Both other modes give about 1,000. In Lesson 25 you'll redo this properly with BenchmarkDotNet."},{"l":10,"s":"10-tracking-vs-no-tracking","t":"Tracking vs No-Tracking Queries","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz Does AsNoTracking() change the SQL that EF Core generates? A. No, it only changes what EF does in memory after reading rows B. Yes, it adds WITH (NOLOCK) C. Yes, it removes the primary key columns D. Yes, it adds READ UNCOMMITTED The SQL is identical. AsNoTracking has nothing to do with database locking hints. You load orders with their customer using AsNoTracking() . Two orders share a customer. What do you get? A. One shared Customer instance B. null for the second customer C. Two separate Customer instances with the same data D. An exception Without identity resolution each row becomes a new instance. Use AsNoTrackingWithIdentityResolution to de-duplicate. Which is true about db.Update(entity) ? A. It only updates properties you changed B. It marks all properties as modified, so every column is updated C. It runs an UPDATE immediately D. It loads the entity first Update() marks the whole entity, and reachable entities, as Modified. Use Attach and change specific properties for minimal updates."},{"l":10,"s":"10-tracking-vs-no-tracking","t":"Tracking vs No-Tracking Queries","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Tracking vs. no-tracking queries Change tracking in EF Core"},{"l":11,"s":"11-projections","t":"Projections: Fetch Only What You Need","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Projections: Fetch Only What You Need Projecting with Select is often the single biggest read optimization in EF Core. It shrinks the SQL result, skips tracking, lets the database use covering indexes and avoids loading graphs. ⏱ 25 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Master projections : shaping queries so the database returns exactly the columns your code needs, and nothing more. Why it matters: Fetching less data is often the single biggest read optimization. It reduces I/O, network traffic, memory and tracking, and it enables covering indexes."},{"l":11,"s":"11-projections","t":"Projections: Fetch Only What You Need","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Project entities into DTOs, records and anonymous types. Pull related data through navigation properties inside a projection, without Include . Write reusable projection expressions. Avoid the classic projection pitfalls."},{"l":11,"s":"11-projections","t":"Projections: Fetch Only What You Need","a":"the-problem-over-fetching","h":"The problem: over-fetching","x":"The problem: over-fetching A product list page needs id, name and price. Loading full entities fetches every column, including the 2 KB Description : ❌ Full entities var products = await db.Products .Where(p => p.CategoryId == 3) .ToListAsync(); SELECT [p].[Id], [p].[CategoryId], [p].[Description], [p].[Name], [p].[Price], [p].[Sku] FROM [Products] AS [p] WHERE [p].[CategoryId] = 3 ✅ Projection var products = await db.Products .Where(p => p.CategoryId == 3) .Select(p => new { p.Id, p.Name, p.Price }) .ToListAsync(); SELECT [p].[Id], [p].[Name], [p].[Price] FROM [Products] AS [p] WHERE [p].[CategoryId] = 3 Benefits of the projection: Less I/O and network traffic : only three columns travel. No change tracking : DTOs aren't entities. Covering indexes : an index on CategoryId that INCLUDE s Name, Price can answer the query without touching the table (Lesson 14). Shape matches the API : you stop leaking entities, and accidental lazy loads, into serialization."},{"l":11,"s":"11-projections","t":"Projections: Fetch Only What You Need","a":"navigation-properties-inside-projections","h":"Navigation properties inside projections","x":"Navigation properties inside projections Inside a Select you can walk navigation properties freely. EF translates them into JOINs or subqueries. No Include is needed , and Include is ignored when the final result isn't an entity. public record OrderSummary(int Id, DateTime PlacedUtc, string CustomerName, int LineCount, decimal Total); var summaries = await db.Orders .Where(o => o.Status == OrderStatus.Paid) .OrderByDescending(o => o.PlacedUtc) .Select(o => new OrderSummary( o.Id, o.PlacedUtc, o.Customer.Name, // JOIN Customers o.Lines.Count, // correlated COUNT subquery o.Lines.Sum(l => l.Quantity * l.UnitPrice))) .Take(50) .ToListAsync(); SELECT TOP(@p) [o].[Id], [o].[PlacedUtc], [c].[Name], (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM [OrderLines] AS [o0] WHERE [o].[Id] = [o0].[OrderId]), (SELECT COALESCE(SUM(CAST([o1].[Quantity] AS decimal(18,2)) * [o1].[UnitPrice]), 0.0) FROM [OrderLines] AS [o1] WHERE [o].[Id] = [o1].[OrderId]) FROM [Orders] AS [o] INNER JOIN [Customers] AS [c] ON [o].[CustomerId] = [c].[Id] WHERE [o].[Status] = 1 ORDER BY [o].[PlacedUtc] DESC Nested collections Projections can include child collections. EF fetches them with a JOIN, or with split queries if you ask (Lesson 15): var detail = await db.Orders .Where(o => o.Id == id) .Select(o => new { o.Id, Customer = o.Customer.Name, Lines = o.Lines.Select(l => new { l.Product.Name, l.Quantity, l.UnitPrice }).ToList() }) .SingleOrDefaultAsync();"},{"l":11,"s":"11-projections","t":"Projections: Fetch Only What You Need","a":"reusable-projections","h":"Reusable projections","x":"Reusable projections Duplicating projection code everywhere is error-prone. Store it as an Expression<Func<T, TDto>> , which stays translatable to SQL: public record ProductListItem(int Id, string Name, decimal Price, string Category) { public static readonly Expression<Func<Product, ProductListItem>> Projection = p => new ProductListItem(p.Id, p.Name, p.Price, p.Category.Name); } var items = await db.Products.Where(p => p.Price < 50) .Select(ProductListItem.Projection) .ToListAsync(); Don't hide projections in methods or constructors EF can't see inside a C# method call or constructor body. If you write .Select(p => ToDto(p)) , or a DTO constructor that takes the whole entity, then EF must fetch the entire entity and run your method client-side. The projection gives you no data reduction. Keep projections as expression trees that list exactly the columns you need. ❌ Fetches every column .Select(p => new ProductDto(p)) // ctor reads p.* .Select(p => Map(p)) // opaque method ✅ Fetches 3 columns .Select(p => new ProductDto { Id = p.Id, Name = p.Name, Price = p.Price }) What about AutoMapper and Mapster? Mapping libraries can generate expression-based projections: AutoMapper's ProjectTo<T>() , Mapster's ProjectToType<T>() , and source generators such as Mapperly's queryable projections. These are fine. Avoid Map<T>() on already-loaded entities, because that is the over-fetching pattern again. Always check the generated SQL."},{"l":11,"s":"11-projections","t":"Projections: Fetch Only What You Need","a":"projection-pitfalls","h":"Projection pitfalls","x":"Projection pitfalls Projecting an entity inside an anonymous type ( new { Product = p } ) fetches every column of that entity and tracks it. Nullable navigations : o.Customer.Name becomes a LEFT JOIN if the relationship is optional, so it can be null . Model the DTO property as nullable. Aggregates on empty sets : Sum of an empty collection becomes 0 (EF uses COALESCE ). Max or Average on empty sets need a cast to a nullable type: o.Lines.Max(l => (decimal?)l.UnitPrice) ."},{"l":11,"s":"11-projections","t":"Projections: Fetch Only What You Need","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways Project with Select to fetch only the columns the caller needs. This is often the biggest single read win. Navigations inside projections become JOINs or subqueries automatically. Include isn't needed there. Keep projections as expression trees. Opaque constructors and methods force full-entity fetches. Projections enable covering indexes and remove tracking overhead."},{"l":11,"s":"11-projections","t":"Projections: Fetch Only What You Need","a":"exercise","h":"Exercise","x":"Exercise 🛠 Build a lean customer dashboard query Write one query that returns, for the 20 newest customers: Id, Name, number of orders, date of last order (nullable), and lifetime spend. Then print the SQL with ToQueryString() . Show solution var dashboard = await db.Customers .OrderByDescending(c => c.CreatedUtc) .Select(c => new { c.Id, c.Name, OrderCount = c.Orders.Count(), LastOrderUtc = c.Orders.Max(o => (DateTime?)o.PlacedUtc), LifetimeSpend = c.Orders.Sum(o => o.Total) }) .Take(20) .ToListAsync(); That's one round trip with no tracking and no order rows loaded into memory. The database does all the aggregation."},{"l":11,"s":"11-projections","t":"Projections: Fetch Only What You Need","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz Is Include() needed to read o.Customer.Name inside a Select projection? A. Yes, otherwise it's null B. No. Navigations in projections are translated to JOINs automatically. C. Only for collection navigations D. Only with lazy loading enabled Include only matters when you return entities. In projections, EF joins whatever you reference. Why is .Select(p => new ProductDto(p)) a performance trap? A. Constructors can't be used in LINQ B. It forces a split query C. It disables the query cache D. EF can't see which properties the constructor uses, so it fetches the whole entity EF only translates what it can see in the expression tree. Opaque constructors and methods force full-entity fetches. Are DTO projections tracked by the change tracker? A. No, because DTOs aren't entity types B. Yes, always C. Only if AsTracking() is used D. Only when they include the primary key Only entity instances are tracked. A DTO containing an entity instance still tracks that entity."},{"l":11,"s":"11-projections","t":"Projections: Fetch Only What You Need","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Efficient querying: project only properties you need Complex query operators"},{"l":12,"s":"12-loading-related-data","t":"Loading Related Data & the N+1 Problem","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Loading Related Data & the N+1 Problem How you load related entities decides whether a page sends 1 query or 1,001. This lesson covers EF Core's three loading strategies and how to find and fix the N+1 problem. ⏱ 35 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Learn the three ways to load related data , and how to recognize and eliminate the N+1 query problem. Why it matters: N+1 is the most common cause of slow pages in ORM-based apps. A page that sends 1,000 queries instead of 1 is slow no matter how fast each query is."},{"l":12,"s":"12-loading-related-data","t":"Loading Related Data & the N+1 Problem","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Use eager ( Include / ThenInclude ), explicit and lazy loading correctly. Recognize and fix N+1 query patterns. Use filtered includes to limit loaded children. Decide when lazy loading is acceptable, which is rarely."},{"l":12,"s":"12-loading-related-data","t":"Loading Related Data & the N+1 Problem","a":"the-n-1-problem","h":"The N+1 problem","x":"The N+1 problem N+1 means one query to fetch a list, plus one extra query per item to fetch related data. With 100 orders, that's 101 round trips. Even at 1 ms each, the latency adds up, and on a cloud database with 2–5 ms latency the page takes half a second just waiting on the network. ❌ N+1 (explicit form) var orders = await db.Orders .Where(o => o.Status == OrderStatus.Paid) .ToListAsync(); // 1 query foreach (var o in orders) { var customer = await db.Customers .FindAsync(o.CustomerId); // N queries Console.WriteLine(customer!.Name); } ✅ One query var rows = await db.Orders .Where(o => o.Status == OrderStatus.Paid) .Select(o => new { o.Id, o.Customer.Name }) .ToListAsync(); // 1 query (JOIN) foreach (var r in rows) Console.WriteLine(r.Name);"},{"l":12,"s":"12-loading-related-data","t":"Loading Related Data & the N+1 Problem","a":"strategy-1-eager-loading-with-include","h":"Strategy 1: Eager loading with Include","x":"Strategy 1: Eager loading with Include Use eager loading when you need entities , typically to modify them, together with their related data. var order = await db.Orders .Include(o => o.Customer) .Include(o => o.Lines) .ThenInclude(l => l.Product) .SingleAsync(o => o.Id == id); SELECT [o].*, [c].*, [s].* FROM (SELECT TOP(2) ... FROM [Orders] WHERE [Id] = @id) AS [o] INNER JOIN [Customers] AS [c] ON [o].[CustomerId] = [c].[Id] LEFT JOIN ( SELECT [o0].*, [p].* FROM [OrderLines] AS [o0] INNER JOIN [Products] AS [p] ON [o0].[ProductId] = [p].[Id] ) AS [s] ON [o].[Id] = [s].[OrderId] ORDER BY [o].[Id], [c].[Id], [s].[Id] Filtered include Load only the children you need. Where , OrderBy , Skip and Take are supported inside Include : var customers = await db.Customers .Include(c => c.Orders .Where(o => o.Status != OrderStatus.Cancelled) .OrderByDescending(o => o.PlacedUtc) .Take(5)) .ToListAsync(); Filtered include + tracking With tracking, if some of a customer's other orders were already tracked by the context, they'll still appear in customer.Orders through navigation fix-up, even though they don't match the filter. Use AsNoTracking() , or a fresh context, when the filtered collection must be exact. Don't over-include Every Include fetches all columns of the related table. Including collections multiplies rows (cartesian explosion, Lesson 15). For read-only screens, a projection (Lesson 11) is almost always better than a chain of Include calls."},{"l":12,"s":"12-loading-related-data","t":"Loading Related Data & the N+1 Problem","a":"strategy-2-explicit-loading","h":"Strategy 2: Explicit loading","x":"Strategy 2: Explicit loading Load a navigation later, on demand, for an already-tracked entity: var customer = await db.Customers.SingleAsync(c => c.Id == id); if (needOrders) { await db.Entry(customer) .Collection(c => c.Orders) .Query() // optional: compose further .Where(o => o.PlacedUtc > DateTime.UtcNow.AddMonths(-3)) .LoadAsync(); } // Count without loading the collection var orderCount = await db.Entry(customer).Collection(c => c.Orders).Query().CountAsync(); Explicit loading is fine for a single entity. Doing it inside a loop is N+1 again."},{"l":12,"s":"12-loading-related-data","t":"Loading Related Data & the N+1 Problem","a":"strategy-3-lazy-loading","h":"Strategy 3: Lazy loading","x":"Strategy 3: Lazy loading With lazy loading, a navigation property loads itself from the database the first time you access it. It needs the Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Proxies package, UseLazyLoadingProxies() , and virtual navigation properties. // 1. Configuration (Program.cs) options.UseSqlServer(connStr).UseLazyLoadingProxies(); // 2. Entities: navigation properties must be virtual (Order.cs) // public virtual Customer Customer { get; set; } = null!; // public virtual List<OrderLine> Lines { get; set; } = new(); // 3. Usage: looks innocent... decimal total = 0; foreach (var order in await db.Orders.ToListAsync()) total += order.Lines.Sum(l => l.UnitPrice); // ⚠ a hidden query per order = N+1 Why most teams disable lazy loading It hides database calls behind property access, so N+1 creeps in silently. JSON serializers walk every navigation, which can trigger dozens of queries or circular references. Lazy loads are synchronous I/O: there's no await , so a thread blocks. Accessing a navigation after the context is disposed throws. If you do use it, turn on detection: EF logs CoreEventId.LazyLoadOnDisposedContextWarning , and you can throw on CoreEventId.NavigationLazyLoading in tests to find lazy loads."},{"l":12,"s":"12-loading-related-data","t":"Loading Related Data & the N+1 Problem","a":"comparing-the-strategies","h":"Comparing the strategies","x":"Comparing the strategies Aspect Eager (Include) Explicit Lazy Projection Round trips 1 (or a few with split queries) 1 per Load call 1 per navigation access 1 Columns fetched All All All Only what's selected Tracked Yes (unless AsNoTracking) Yes Yes No Best for Load-modify-save of a graph Conditional loading of one entity Prototypes, desktop apps with care Reads, APIs, reports"},{"l":12,"s":"12-loading-related-data","t":"Loading Related Data & the N+1 Problem","a":"detecting-n-1","h":"Detecting N+1","x":"Detecting N+1 Logs : repeated identical SQL with only the parameter changing is the giveaway. Count commands per request : a DbCommandInterceptor (Lesson 24) can count queries and warn above a threshold. APM / OpenTelemetry traces : a request span with dozens of child DB spans (Lesson 19). Integration tests : assert the number of commands executed by an endpoint."},{"l":12,"s":"12-loading-related-data","t":"Loading Related Data & the N+1 Problem","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways N+1 means one query per item. It's the most common ORM performance bug. Use projections for reads, Include (optionally filtered) to load graphs you'll modify, and explicit loading for single entities. Lazy loading hides queries behind property access. Avoid it, or detect it in tests. Detect N+1 with logs, traces, or a command-counting test."},{"l":12,"s":"12-loading-related-data","t":"Loading Related Data & the N+1 Problem","a":"exercise","h":"Exercise","x":"Exercise 🛠 Kill the N+1 This code builds an \"invoice\" view. Count the queries, then rewrite it as one query. var orders = await db.Orders.Where(o => o.CustomerId == customerId).ToListAsync(); var result = new List<InvoiceDto>(); foreach (var o in orders) { var lines = await db.OrderLines.Where(l => l.OrderId == o.Id).ToListAsync(); var items = new List<string>(); foreach (var l in lines) { var p = await db.Products.FindAsync(l.ProductId); items.Add($\"{l.Quantity} x {p!.Name}\"); } result.Add(new InvoiceDto(o.Id, o.PlacedUtc, items)); } Show solution The original runs 1 + N + N×M queries. FindAsync can skip some if products are already tracked. For 10 orders with 3 lines each, that's up to 41 queries. Rewritten: var result = await db.Orders .Where(o => o.CustomerId == customerId) .Select(o => new InvoiceDto( o.Id, o.PlacedUtc, o.Lines.Select(l => l.Quantity + \" x \" + l.Product.Name).ToList())) .ToListAsync(); It's one query, and the string concatenation is translated to SQL as well."},{"l":12,"s":"12-loading-related-data","t":"Loading Related Data & the N+1 Problem","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz A page lists 200 orders and shows each customer's name using lazy loading. How many queries run, worst case? A. 1 B. 2 C. 201 D. 400 1 query for the orders, plus 1 per distinct customer not already tracked. Worst case that's 200 more. What happens to Include() when the query ends with a projection to a DTO? A. It's ignored. The projection decides what's loaded. B. It loads the related entities into the DTO automatically C. EF throws an exception D. It forces a split query Include only affects queries that return entity types. Which of these is supported inside a filtered Include ? A. GroupBy B. Where , OrderBy , Skip and Take C. Join D. Distinct only Filtered include (EF Core 5+) supports Where, OrderBy/ThenBy (and descending), Skip and Take."},{"l":12,"s":"12-loading-related-data","t":"Loading Related Data & the N+1 Problem","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Loading related data Beware of lazy loading"},{"l":13,"s":"13-pagination","t":"Pagination: Offset vs Keyset","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Pagination: Offset vs Keyset Returning unbounded result sets is one of the most common production incidents. This lesson covers how to page correctly, and why the obvious Skip / Take approach slows down as users page deeper. ⏱ 25 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Learn to return large result sets in pages correctly, and understand why keyset paging stays fast where offset paging slows down. Why it matters: Unbounded queries cause outages as data grows, and deep offset paging degrades linearly. Correct paging keeps list endpoints fast at any size."},{"l":13,"s":"13-pagination","t":"Pagination: Offset vs Keyset","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Always limit result sets, and know why. Implement offset pagination correctly and understand its costs. Implement keyset (seek) pagination, including with composite sort keys. Handle total counts efficiently."},{"l":13,"s":"13-pagination","t":"Pagination: Offset vs Keyset","a":"rule-1-never-return-unbounded-results","h":"Rule #1: never return unbounded results","x":"Rule #1: never return unbounded results db.Orders.ToListAsync() is fine with 50 rows in development and an outage with 5 million rows in production. Every list endpoint should have a maximum page size enforced on the server: const int MaxPageSize = 100; pageSize = Math.Clamp(pageSize, 1, MaxPageSize);"},{"l":13,"s":"13-pagination","t":"Pagination: Offset vs Keyset","a":"offset-pagination-skip-take","h":"Offset pagination (Skip/Take)","x":"Offset pagination (Skip/Take) var skip = (pageNumber - 1) * pageSize; var page = await db.Orders .AsNoTracking() .OrderByDescending(o => o.PlacedUtc).ThenByDescending(o => o.Id) // deterministic order! .Skip(skip) .Take(pageSize) .Select(o => new { o.Id, o.PlacedUtc, o.Total }) .ToListAsync(); SELECT [o].[Id], [o].[PlacedUtc], [o].[Total] FROM [Orders] AS [o] ORDER BY [o].[PlacedUtc] DESC, [o].[Id] DESC OFFSET @skip ROWS FETCH NEXT @pageSize ROWS ONLY Always order by a unique key Without a total ordering the database may return rows in any order, so rows can repeat or vanish between pages. Add the primary key as a tie-breaker: .ThenBy(o => o.Id) . Why offset gets slow To return page 5,000 with 20 rows per page, the database must read and discard 99,980 rows first. Cost grows linearly with the offset. Offset paging also suffers from drift : if a row is inserted or deleted while the user pages, items shift and the user sees duplicates or misses rows."},{"l":13,"s":"13-pagination","t":"Pagination: Offset vs Keyset","a":"keyset-seek-pagination","h":"Keyset (seek) pagination","x":"Keyset (seek) pagination Instead of \"skip N rows\", say \"give me rows after the last one I saw \". With an index on the sort key, the database seeks directly to the position. Page 5,000 costs the same as page 1. // First page: lastId = null IQueryable<Order> query = db.Orders.AsNoTracking(); if (lastId is not null) query = query.Where(o => o.Id > lastId); // seek predicate var page = await query.OrderBy(o => o.Id).Take(pageSize).ToListAsync(); var nextCursor = page.LastOrDefault()?.Id; // return to client SELECT TOP(@pageSize) ... FROM [Orders] AS [o] WHERE [o].[Id] > @lastId ORDER BY [o].[Id] Composite sort keys Real lists sort by something non-unique, such as date. Use (date, id) as the cursor: public record OrderCursor(DateTime PlacedUtc, int Id); IQueryable<Order> q = db.Orders.AsNoTracking(); if (cursor is not null) { q = q.Where(o => o.PlacedUtc < cursor.PlacedUtc || (o.PlacedUtc == cursor.PlacedUtc && o.Id < cursor.Id)); } var page = await q .OrderByDescending(o => o.PlacedUtc).ThenByDescending(o => o.Id) .Take(pageSize + 1) // fetch one extra to know if there's a next page .Select(o => new { o.Id, o.PlacedUtc, o.Total }) .ToListAsync(); var hasMore = page.Count > pageSize; var items = page.Take(pageSize).ToList(); var next = hasMore ? new OrderCursor(items[^1].PlacedUtc, items[^1].Id) : null; Support it with a matching composite index (Lesson 14): modelBuilder.Entity<Order>() .HasIndex(o => new { o.PlacedUtc, o.Id }) .IsDescending(true, true); // EF Core 7+ Encode the cursor Return the cursor to clients as an opaque string, for example Base64 of the JSON {\"PlacedUtc\":\"…\",\"Id\":123} . Clients can't tamper with its meaning, and you can change the format later. Offset vs keyset Aspect Offset (Skip/Take) Keyset (seek) Deep page performance Degrades linearly Constant (with index) Jump to arbitrary page ✅ Yes ❌ Only next/previous Stable under inserts/deletes ❌ Drift ✅ Stable Best for Admin grids with page numbers, small tables Infinite scroll, APIs, exports, large tables"},{"l":13,"s":"13-pagination","t":"Pagination: Offset vs Keyset","a":"total-counts","h":"Total counts","x":"Total counts \"Page 3 of 1,284\" needs a COUNT(*) , which scans the whole filtered set on every request. Options: Skip it. Show \"next\" and \"previous\" only, using the Take(pageSize + 1) trick. Count only on the first page request and cache it. Show an approximate count for huge tables. var filtered = db.Orders.Where(o => o.Status == status); var total = await filtered.CountAsync(); // round trip 1 var items = await filtered.OrderBy(o => o.Id).Skip(skip).Take(take).ToListAsync(); // round trip 2"},{"l":13,"s":"13-pagination","t":"Pagination: Offset vs Keyset","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways Never return unbounded result sets. Enforce a maximum page size on the server. Offset paging cost grows with page depth and drifts when data changes. Keyset paging stays constant-time with an index and a unique cursor (e.g. date + id). COUNT(*) for totals is a full scan of the filtered set. Make it optional or cache it."},{"l":13,"s":"13-pagination","t":"Pagination: Offset vs Keyset","a":"exercise","h":"Exercise","x":"Exercise 🛠 Compare the two Seed 1,000,000 orders (use ExecuteSql with a set-based INSERT, or batch inserts, from Lesson 16). Time offset pagination for pages 1, 1,000 and 40,000 (page size 25). Implement keyset pagination on (PlacedUtc, Id) with the index above and time the same depth. In SQL Server Management Studio or Azure Data Studio, look at the actual execution plans. Compare \"rows read\" in each. What to expect Offset page 40,000 reads about 1M rows. Keyset reads about 25 using an index seek. Times typically differ by one or two orders of magnitude."},{"l":13,"s":"13-pagination","t":"Pagination: Offset vs Keyset","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz Why does Skip(100000).Take(20) get slow on large tables? A. EF downloads all 100,020 rows B. The database still has to read and discard the skipped rows C. The query cache is bypassed D. OFFSET isn't supported by SQL Server The database discards skipped rows server-side, but still has to read them. EF doesn't download them. What's required for keyset pagination to give consistent results? A. A clustered columnstore index B. AsNoTracking() C. A GUID primary key D. A unique, deterministic sort order (e.g. date + id) used in both the WHERE and ORDER BY The cursor must identify a unique position, so include a unique tie-breaker column. What's the main downside of keyset pagination? A. You can't jump directly to an arbitrary page number B. It can't be indexed C. It always needs raw SQL D. It's slower for the first page Keyset navigates relative to a cursor: next and previous only."},{"l":13,"s":"13-pagination","t":"Pagination: Offset vs Keyset","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Microsoft Learn: Pagination Use The Index, Luke: No offset"},{"l":14,"s":"14-indexes-and-migrations","t":"Indexes & Migrations","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Indexes & Migrations Perfect LINQ on top of a missing index is still slow. This lesson covers defining indexes in your EF model, designing them for your queries, and shipping schema changes safely. ⏱ 35 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Learn to design indexes from your queries , define them in the EF model, and deploy schema changes safely. Why it matters: Perfect LINQ on top of a missing index is still a full table scan. Indexes are usually the largest single lever on query speed, and migrations are how they reach production safely."},{"l":14,"s":"14-indexes-and-migrations","t":"Indexes & Migrations","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Define unique, composite, filtered, descending and covering indexes with the Fluent API. Design indexes from your actual query patterns. Recognize LINQ patterns that prevent index use. Deploy migrations safely with SQL scripts and migration bundles."},{"l":14,"s":"14-indexes-and-migrations","t":"Indexes & Migrations","a":"indexes-in-the-ef-model","h":"Indexes in the EF model","x":"Indexes in the EF model EF creates indexes for primary keys and foreign keys by convention. Every other index comes from you: modelBuilder.Entity<Customer>(b => { // Unique index b.HasIndex(c => c.Email).IsUnique(); // Named index b.HasIndex(c => c.CreatedUtc).HasDatabaseName(\"IX_Customers_Created\"); }); modelBuilder.Entity<Order>(b => { // Composite index: column order matters! b.HasIndex(o => new { o.CustomerId, o.PlacedUtc }); // Filtered (partial) index: only \"open\" orders b.HasIndex(o => o.PlacedUtc) .HasFilter(\"[Status] = 0\") // SQL Server syntax; PostgreSQL: \"\\\"Status\\\" = 0\" .HasDatabaseName(\"IX_Orders_Pending_Placed\"); // Descending index (EF Core 7+) b.HasIndex(o => new { o.PlacedUtc, o.Id }).IsDescending(true, true); }); modelBuilder.Entity<Product>(b => { // Covering index: SQL Server INCLUDE columns b.HasIndex(p => p.CategoryId) .IncludeProperties(p => new { p.Name, p.Price }); });"},{"l":14,"s":"14-indexes-and-migrations","t":"Indexes & Migrations","a":"designing-indexes-from-queries","h":"Designing indexes from queries","x":"Designing indexes from queries Index design starts from the queries, not the tables. For each important query, look at its WHERE , JOIN , ORDER BY and SELECT columns. Composite index column order An index on (CustomerId, PlacedUtc) is like a phone book sorted by last name, then first name. It efficiently supports: WHERE CustomerId = @x ✅ WHERE CustomerId = @x ORDER BY PlacedUtc DESC ✅ (no sort needed) WHERE CustomerId = @x AND PlacedUtc > @d ✅ WHERE PlacedUtc > @d ❌ (can't seek on the second column alone) Rule of thumb: equality columns first, then range or sort columns . Covering indexes and projections When an index contains every column a query needs, the database never touches the base table, so there are no key lookups. That's why projections (Lesson 11) and covering indexes work so well together: // Served entirely by IX_Products_CategoryId INCLUDE (Name, Price) db.Products.Where(p => p.CategoryId == id).Select(p => new { p.Id, p.Name, p.Price });"},{"l":14,"s":"14-indexes-and-migrations","t":"Indexes & Migrations","a":"linq-that-defeats-indexes","h":"LINQ that defeats indexes","x":"LINQ that defeats indexes Pattern SQL Why it hurts Alternative c.Name.Contains(x) LIKE '%x%' Leading wildcard means a full scan StartsWith → LIKE 'x%' , or full-text search o.PlacedUtc.Year == 2025 DATEPART(year, …) = 2025 A function on the column isn't sargable o.PlacedUtc >= new DateTime(2025,1,1) && o.PlacedUtc < new DateTime(2026,1,1) c.Email.ToLower() == x LOWER([Email]) = @x Function on the column Use a case-insensitive collation (the SQL Server default) and compare directly Type mismatch (varchar column, nvarchar param) CONVERT_IMPLICIT Index scan instead of seek Configure IsUnicode(false) in the model (Lesson 3) a == x || b == y OR across columns Often a scan Separate indexes, or Concat / Union of two queries Let the database tell you SQL Server's missing-index DMVs ( sys.dm_db_missing_index_details ), Query Store, and PostgreSQL's pg_stat_statements plus EXPLAIN ANALYZE show which indexes your workload actually needs. Treat their suggestions as hints and avoid duplicate or overlapping indexes. Every index makes inserts and updates slower."},{"l":14,"s":"14-indexes-and-migrations","t":"Indexes & Migrations","a":"migrations","h":"Migrations","x":"Migrations dotnet ef migrations add AddOrderIndexes dotnet ef migrations list dotnet ef migrations remove # undo last (if not applied) Always review the generated migration . Look for unintended table rebuilds, column drops caused by renames (EF may see a rename as drop plus add), and data-type changes on large tables. Deploying migrations safely Approach How Production? context.Database.Migrate() at startup The app migrates itself on boot ⚠ Not recommended for production: the app needs DDL permissions, startup gets slower, and a failed migration takes the app down. EF 9+ takes a lock so instances don't migrate concurrently, but the other risks remain. OK for dev and simple apps. Idempotent SQL script dotnet ef migrations script --idempotent -o migrate.sql ✅ Reviewable by a DBA and runnable from CI/CD Migration bundle dotnet ef migrations bundle --self-contained -r linux-x64 → ./efbundle --connection \"...\" ✅ A single executable, ideal for pipelines Large-table operations Creating an index on a 100M-row table can lock it. On SQL Server Enterprise or Azure SQL, use online index builds: b.HasIndex(o => o.PlacedUtc).IsCreatedOnline(); // SQL Server: WITH (ONLINE = ON) For risky changes, use custom SQL in the migration with migrationBuilder.Sql(...) , and split schema changes into expand and contract phases: add a column, backfill it, switch the code over, then drop the old column."},{"l":14,"s":"14-indexes-and-migrations","t":"Indexes & Migrations","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways Design indexes from your queries: equality columns first, then range or sort columns. Use covering ( INCLUDE ), filtered and descending indexes where queries allow. Avoid non-sargable predicates: functions on columns, leading wildcards, type mismatches. Ship schema changes as reviewed scripts or bundles, not Migrate() at production startup."},{"l":14,"s":"14-indexes-and-migrations","t":"Indexes & Migrations","a":"exercise","h":"Exercise","x":"Exercise 🛠 Index for your queries Design the indexes for these three queries, add them to the model, and generate a migration: A customer's orders, newest first: Where(o => o.CustomerId == id).OrderByDescending(o => o.PlacedUtc) The pending orders queue, oldest first: Where(o => o.Status == OrderStatus.Pending).OrderBy(o => o.PlacedUtc) A product search by SKU prefix that projects Id, Name and Price. Show solution modelBuilder.Entity<Order>(b => { b.HasIndex(o => new { o.CustomerId, o.PlacedUtc }).IsDescending(false, true); b.HasIndex(o => o.PlacedUtc).HasFilter(\"[Status] = 0\").HasDatabaseName(\"IX_Orders_PendingQueue\"); }); modelBuilder.Entity<Product>() .HasIndex(p => p.Sku).IsUnique().IncludeProperties(p => new { p.Name, p.Price }); // and use p.Sku.StartsWith(prefix) → LIKE 'prefix%' (index seek) Note: the FK convention originally created an index on CustomerId alone. When you add a composite index that starts with CustomerId , EF sees that the FK is already covered and stops creating the separate index. Your migration will drop IX_Orders_CustomerId , which means one less index to maintain on every write."},{"l":14,"s":"14-indexes-and-migrations","t":"Indexes & Migrations","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz An index exists on (CustomerId, PlacedUtc) . Which query can't use it for a seek? A. WHERE CustomerId = 5 B. WHERE CustomerId = 5 ORDER BY PlacedUtc C. WHERE PlacedUtc > '2025-01-01' D. WHERE CustomerId = 5 AND PlacedUtc > '2025-01-01' Composite indexes can only seek using a leading prefix of their columns. Which LINQ filter is index-friendly (sargable)? A. o.PlacedUtc.Year == 2025 B. o.PlacedUtc >= start && o.PlacedUtc < end C. c.Name.ToUpper() == \"BOB\" D. c.Name.Contains(\"bob\") A range on the raw column can use an index seek. Functions on the column, and leading wildcards, can't. Which is the recommended way to apply migrations in production? A. Call Database.EnsureCreated() at startup B. Call Database.Migrate() from every app instance at startup C. Delete and recreate the database D. Generate an idempotent SQL script or a migration bundle and run it from your deployment pipeline Scripts and bundles are reviewable, run once, and don't need the app to have DDL rights."},{"l":14,"s":"14-indexes-and-migrations","t":"Indexes & Migrations","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Indexes Applying migrations Use The Index, Luke (free SQL indexing book)"},{"l":15,"s":"15-split-queries","t":"Split Queries & Cartesian Explosion","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Split Queries & Cartesian Explosion Including several collections in one query can multiply the rows the database returns. This lesson explains cartesian explosion and how split queries fix it, along with their trade-offs. ⏱ 25 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Understand cartesian explosion and learn when splitting one query into several is faster, and when it isn't. Why it matters: Loading multiple collections in one query can multiply returned rows by orders of magnitude. Knowing the trade-off lets you choose based on measurements, not folklore."},{"l":15,"s":"15-split-queries","t":"Split Queries & Cartesian Explosion","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Explain cartesian explosion and data duplication in single queries. Use AsSplitQuery() and AsSingleQuery() , and set a global default. Understand the consistency and round-trip trade-offs of split queries. Choose the right approach for a given query."},{"l":15,"s":"15-split-queries","t":"Split Queries & Cartesian Explosion","a":"the-problem","h":"The problem","x":"The problem Suppose Customer has two collections, Orders and Addresses . In a relational database, one query that loads both must JOIN them: var customers = await db.Customers .Include(c => c.Orders) .Include(c => c.Addresses) .Where(c => c.Id < 100) .ToListAsync(); For a customer with 50 orders and 4 addresses, the JOIN produces 50 × 4 = 200 rows for that one customer. Every customer column is repeated in all 200 rows. Every order is repeated 4 times, and every address 50 times. Add a third collection and it multiplies again. This is cartesian explosion . Even one collection duplicates data Include(o => o.Lines) repeats all of the order's columns in every line row. If the parent has large columns, such as the 2 KB Description on Product, that duplication alone can dominate network transfer. EF warns you when a query loads multiple collections with single-query behaviour and no explicit choice: warn: Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Query[20504] Compiling a query which loads related collections for more than one collection navigation, either via 'Include' or through projection, but no 'QuerySplittingBehavior' has been configured..."},{"l":15,"s":"15-split-queries","t":"Split Queries & Cartesian Explosion","a":"the-solution-assplitquery","h":"The solution: AsSplitQuery","x":"The solution: AsSplitQuery var customers = await db.Customers .Include(c => c.Orders) .Include(c => c.Addresses) .Where(c => c.Id < 100) .AsSplitQuery() .ToListAsync(); SELECT [c].* FROM [Customers] AS [c] WHERE [c].[Id] < 100 ORDER BY [c].[Id]; SELECT [o].*, [c].[Id] FROM [Customers] AS [c] INNER JOIN [Orders] AS [o] ON [c].[Id] = [o].[CustomerId] WHERE [c].[Id] < 100 ORDER BY [c].[Id]; SELECT [a].*, [c].[Id] FROM [Customers] AS [c] INNER JOIN [Addresses] AS [a] ON [c].[Id] = [a].[CustomerId] WHERE [c].[Id] < 100 ORDER BY [c].[Id]; Total rows are now 100 + (orders) + (addresses) instead of their product. Split queries also work with collections inside projections: var dto = await db.Customers .Select(c => new { c.Name, Orders = c.Orders.Select(o => o.Total).ToList(), Cities = c.Addresses.Select(a => a.City).ToList() }) .AsSplitQuery() .ToListAsync();"},{"l":15,"s":"15-split-queries","t":"Split Queries & Cartesian Explosion","a":"trade-offs","h":"Trade-offs","x":"Trade-offs Aspect Single query (default) Split query Round trips 1 1 + number of collection includes Data duplication High with collections None Consistency Atomic snapshot ⚠ Data can change between queries unless you use a serializable or snapshot transaction Skip/Take on the root Fine ⚠ Before EF 10, a non-unique ordering could make child queries load data for different root rows. EF 10 adds the key to the ordering of every split query automatically. A deterministic ordering is still good practice. Best when Reference navigations only, or one small collection; high-latency networks Several or large collections, wide parent rows Buffering note On SQL Server without MARS (Multiple Active Result Sets), EF must buffer the results of all split queries except the last one in memory. That's usually fine, but worth knowing for very large results."},{"l":15,"s":"15-split-queries","t":"Split Queries & Cartesian Explosion","a":"setting-a-global-default","h":"Setting a global default","x":"Setting a global default options.UseSqlServer(connStr, sql => sql.UseQuerySplittingBehavior(QuerySplittingBehavior.SplitQuery)); // Opt back into a single query where it's better: var order = await db.Orders.Include(o => o.Customer).Include(o => o.Lines) .AsSingleQuery().SingleAsync(o => o.Id == id); How to decide Don't choose by rule. Measure both with realistic data and realistic network latency. A split query trades data volume for round trips. With 0.2 ms local latency, splitting almost always wins for multiple collections. With a 20 ms cross-region link, the extra round trips may cost more than the duplicated data. Often the best answer is neither: project only what you need (Lesson 11), so the duplication is a few narrow columns."},{"l":15,"s":"15-split-queries","t":"Split Queries & Cartesian Explosion","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways Multiple collection includes in one query multiply rows (cartesian explosion). AsSplitQuery trades extra round trips for no duplication. Measure both with realistic latency. Split queries aren't one atomic snapshot. EF 10 keeps their ordering consistent automatically. Often the best fix is a narrow projection instead of either strategy."},{"l":15,"s":"15-split-queries","t":"Split Queries & Cartesian Explosion","a":"exercise","h":"Exercise","x":"Exercise 🛠 Watch the rows multiply Add an Address entity (Id, CustomerId, City) and seed 5 addresses per customer. Run the double-include query for 100 customers as a single query. Log how many rows come back: run the SQL from ToQueryString() in your SQL tool. Switch to AsSplitQuery() and compare row counts and elapsed time. Rewrite it as a projection that only takes Order.Total and Address.City . Compare again. What to expect Single query: about 100 × 10 × 5 = 5,000 wide rows. Split: about 100 + 1,000 + 500 = 1,600 rows across three queries. The projection with split is the leanest of all."},{"l":15,"s":"15-split-queries","t":"Split Queries & Cartesian Explosion","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz A query includes Orders (avg 20 per customer) and Tags (avg 10 per customer) for 50 customers. About how many rows does the single query return? A. 80 B. 10,000 C. 1,500 D. 50 50 × 20 × 10 = 10,000 rows. The collections multiply each other. What's a real risk of split queries? A. They can't use indexes B. They disable change tracking C. Data can change between the separate queries, giving an inconsistent result D. They can't be used with projections Each query is a separate statement. Use a snapshot or serializable transaction if you need strict consistency. When paging with Skip / Take and AsSplitQuery() , what must you ensure? A. The ordering is unique, e.g. ends with the primary key B. You use AsNoTracking() C. MARS is enabled D. The page size is under 100 Each split query re-applies the paging. With a non-unique ordering, EF 9 and earlier could select different root rows in each query. EF 10 makes the ordering consistent automatically, but explicit unique ordering keeps results deterministic on every version."},{"l":15,"s":"15-split-queries","t":"Split Queries & Cartesian Explosion","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Single vs. split queries"},{"l":16,"s":"16-efficient-saving","t":"Efficient Saving & Bulk Operations","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Efficient Saving & Bulk Operations Reads get most of the attention, but slow writes hurt just as much. This lesson covers how SaveChanges batches work, set-based updates and deletes with ExecuteUpdate / ExecuteDelete , key generation strategies, and true bulk loading. ⏱ 35 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Learn to write data efficiently : batching, set-based updates and deletes, key generation strategies and true bulk loading. Why it matters: Per-row saves and load-modify-save loops are slow and hold locks. Efficient writes keep imports, background jobs and APIs fast and reduce contention."},{"l":16,"s":"16-efficient-saving","t":"Efficient Saving & Bulk Operations","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Avoid per-row round trips by letting EF batch changes. Update and delete many rows without loading them, using ExecuteUpdateAsync and ExecuteDeleteAsync . Use HiLo or sequences to reduce key-generation overhead. Know when to reach for SqlBulkCopy , PostgreSQL COPY or a bulk library."},{"l":16,"s":"16-efficient-saving","t":"Efficient Saving & Bulk Operations","a":"savechanges-batching","h":"SaveChanges batching","x":"SaveChanges batching SaveChanges collects every pending change and sends the commands in batches , many statements per round trip, inside one transaction. ❌ 1,000 round trips + 1,000 transactions foreach (var dto in incoming) { db.Products.Add(Map(dto)); await db.SaveChangesAsync(); } ✅ A handful of batched round trips db.Products.AddRange(incoming.Select(Map)); await db.SaveChangesAsync(); You can tune the batch size per provider: options.UseSqlServer(connStr, sql => sql.MaxBatchSize(100)); The defaults are usually well chosen. Change them only after measuring. Recent SaveChanges improvements (EF Core 7+) When SaveChanges executes a single statement, EF no longer wraps it in an explicit transaction, which saves round trips. On SQL Server, inserts use OUTPUT to return generated values in the same statement. Triggers : SQL Server doesn't allow OUTPUT without INTO on tables with triggers. Declare them so EF uses a compatible, slightly slower SQL form: b.ToTable(t => t.HasTrigger(\"TR_Orders_Audit\")); Large imports: chunk and clear Adding 1,000,000 entities to a single context makes the change tracker huge and DetectChanges slow. Process in chunks: foreach (var chunk in rows.Chunk(5_000)) { db.Products.AddRange(chunk.Select(Map)); await db.SaveChangesAsync(); db.ChangeTracker.Clear(); } If you change many tracked entities in a hot loop, db.ChangeTracker.AutoDetectChangesEnabled = false prevents repeated scans. Re-enable it, or call DetectChanges() manually, before saving."},{"l":16,"s":"16-efficient-saving","t":"Efficient Saving & Bulk Operations","a":"executeupdate-and-executedelete","h":"ExecuteUpdate and ExecuteDelete","x":"ExecuteUpdate and ExecuteDelete To change many rows, the traditional approach is load, modify, save: N rows loaded and N UPDATE statements. Since EF Core 7 you can issue a single set-based SQL statement directly from LINQ: // Raise prices 10% in one category: one UPDATE, zero rows loaded var affected = await db.Products .Where(p => p.CategoryId == categoryId) .ExecuteUpdateAsync(s => s .SetProperty(p => p.Price, p => p.Price * 1.10m) .SetProperty(p => p.Name, p => p.Name + \" (new price)\")); UPDATE [p] SET [p].[Price] = [p].[Price] * 1.10, [p].[Name] = [p].[Name] + N' (new price)' FROM [Products] AS [p] WHERE [p].[CategoryId] = @categoryId // Purge old cancelled orders: one DELETE await db.Orders .Where(o => o.Status == OrderStatus.Cancelled && o.PlacedUtc < cutoff) .ExecuteDeleteAsync(); New in EF Core 10: regular lambdas in ExecuteUpdate Before EF 10, the setters argument had to be an expression tree, which made conditional updates awkward. EF 10 accepts a normal lambda, so you can use plain if statements: await db.Products.Where(p => p.Id == id).ExecuteUpdateAsync(s => { s.SetProperty(p => p.Price, newPrice); if (newName is not null) s.SetProperty(p => p.Name, newName); }); EF 10 also supports updating properties inside JSON columns mapped as complex types with ExecuteUpdate . ExecuteUpdate/Delete bypass the change tracker They run immediately , not at SaveChanges , and outside its transaction. Wrap them in an explicit transaction if they must commit atomically with other changes. Already-tracked entities are not updated in memory, so they're now stale. SaveChanges interceptors, and overrides such as audit fields set in SaveChanges , don't run. Set audit columns in the SetProperty call yourself. Cascade deletes configured only in EF (not in the database) don't happen."},{"l":16,"s":"16-efficient-saving","t":"Efficient Saving & Bulk Operations","a":"key-generation-identity-vs-hilo-vs-sequences","h":"Key generation: IDENTITY vs HiLo vs sequences","x":"Key generation: IDENTITY vs HiLo vs sequences With IDENTITY columns, the database generates keys, and EF reads them back. With HiLo, EF reserves a block of keys from a database sequence and assigns them client-side . Keys are then known before SaveChanges , and EF fetches a new block only occasionally. modelBuilder.Entity<Order>() .Property(o => o.Id) .UseHiLo(\"OrderHiLoSequence\"); // SQL Server sequence, blocks of 10 by default // or model-wide modelBuilder.UseHiLo(); HiLo is helpful when you need ids before saving, for example to build related graphs or publish messages, and in some high-volume insert scenarios. Its downsides are gaps in key values and a database sequence to manage. For most apps IDENTITY is fine. Measure before switching."},{"l":16,"s":"16-efficient-saving","t":"Efficient Saving & Bulk Operations","a":"true-bulk-loading","h":"True bulk loading","x":"True bulk loading For hundreds of thousands of rows, even batched INSERT statements are slow compared with the database's native bulk path: Option Notes SqlBulkCopy (SQL Server) Built into Microsoft.Data.SqlClient and extremely fast. You map columns yourself, and it bypasses EF. Npgsql binary COPY (PostgreSQL) conn.BeginBinaryImport(\"COPY ... FROM STDIN (FORMAT BINARY)\") Third-party: EFCore.BulkExtensions, linq2db.EntityFrameworkCore, Entity Framework Extensions Bulk insert, update, merge and upsert on top of your EF model. Check the licences : some are commercial or dual-licensed. Set-based SQL INSERT INTO ... SELECT ... through ExecuteSqlAsync when data already lives in the database. // SqlBulkCopy sketch using the EF connection var conn = (SqlConnection)db.Database.GetDbConnection(); // Microsoft.Data.SqlClient if (conn.State != ConnectionState.Open) await conn.OpenAsync(); // EF may already have it open using var bulk = new SqlBulkCopy(conn) { DestinationTableName = \"Products\", BatchSize = 10_000 }; bulk.ColumnMappings.Add(nameof(Product.Sku), \"Sku\"); bulk.ColumnMappings.Add(nameof(Product.Name), \"Name\"); // ... await bulk.WriteToServerAsync(dataTableOrDataReader);"},{"l":16,"s":"16-efficient-saving","t":"Efficient Saving & Bulk Operations","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways Never call SaveChanges in a loop. Let EF batch changes, and chunk very large imports. ExecuteUpdateAsync / ExecuteDeleteAsync change many rows in one statement without loading them. Execute* methods bypass the change tracker, SaveChanges interceptors and SaveChanges' transaction. For hundreds of thousands of rows, use the database's bulk path ( SqlBulkCopy , COPY )."},{"l":16,"s":"16-efficient-saving","t":"Efficient Saving & Bulk Operations","a":"exercise","h":"Exercise","x":"Exercise 🛠 Four ways to write Insert 50,000 products and time each approach: Add + SaveChanges per row AddRange + one SaveChanges AddRange in chunks of 5,000 with ChangeTracker.Clear() SqlBulkCopy Then mark all products in category 1 as discounted, first by load-modify-save, then with ExecuteUpdateAsync . What to expect Per-row saves typically take minutes. Batching takes seconds. Bulk copy takes well under a second to a couple of seconds. ExecuteUpdateAsync beats load-modify-save by orders of magnitude on large sets, because no rows cross the network."},{"l":16,"s":"16-efficient-saving","t":"Efficient Saving & Bulk Operations","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz When does ExecuteDeleteAsync() run its SQL? A. At the next SaveChanges() B. When the context is disposed C. Immediately, when the method is awaited D. At transaction commit Execute* methods run immediately and bypass the change tracker. You call ExecuteUpdateAsync on products that are also tracked by the context. What happens to the tracked instances? A. They keep their old values. They're now stale. B. EF refreshes them automatically C. EF throws an exception D. They're detached The change tracker isn't aware of bulk updates. Reload the entities or clear the tracker if you keep using them. What's the main reason to declare HasTrigger() on SQL Server tables that have triggers? A. To run the trigger from EF B. So EF avoids OUTPUT without INTO , which SQL Server doesn't allow on tables with triggers C. To disable the trigger during SaveChanges D. To enable batching Without it, SaveChanges fails on tables with triggers (EF Core 7+)."},{"l":16,"s":"16-efficient-saving","t":"Efficient Saving & Bulk Operations","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Efficient updating ExecuteUpdate and ExecuteDelete What's new in EF Core 10"},{"l":17,"s":"17-concurrency-and-transactions","t":"Concurrency, Transactions & Resiliency","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Concurrency, Transactions & Resiliency Fast code that loses updates or falls over on a transient network blip isn't production-ready. This lesson covers optimistic concurrency, transaction scope and isolation, and connection resiliency, all with performance in mind. ⏱ 35 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Learn to keep data correct under concurrency and failures without sacrificing throughput: optimistic concurrency, short transactions and connection resiliency. Why it matters: Fast code that loses updates or crashes on a transient network error isn't production-ready. These patterns protect data integrity while avoiding costly locks."},{"l":17,"s":"17-concurrency-and-transactions","t":"Concurrency, Transactions & Resiliency","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Implement optimistic concurrency with row versions and concurrency tokens. Resolve DbUpdateConcurrencyException correctly. Use explicit transactions, and keep them short. Configure retry-on-failure and combine it correctly with user transactions."},{"l":17,"s":"17-concurrency-and-transactions","t":"Concurrency, Transactions & Resiliency","a":"optimistic-concurrency","h":"Optimistic concurrency","x":"Optimistic concurrency Pessimistic locking (holding locks while a user edits) kills throughput. EF uses optimistic concurrency : it assumes conflicts are rare, includes a version check in the UPDATE , and throws if someone else changed the row first. public class Product { // ... [Timestamp] // SQL Server rowversion public byte[] RowVersion { get; set; } = null!; } // or Fluent API modelBuilder.Entity<Product>().Property(p => p.RowVersion).IsRowVersion(); UPDATE [Products] SET [Price] = @p0 OUTPUT INSERTED.[RowVersion] WHERE [Id] = @p1 AND [RowVersion] = @p2; If zero rows are affected, EF throws DbUpdateConcurrencyException . Other providers PostgreSQL : map the system column xmin : b.Property<uint>(\"Version\").IsRowVersion(); (Npgsql 7+). SQLite and others : use a regular column as a concurrency token, .IsConcurrencyToken() , and update it yourself (e.g. a Guid Version you regenerate on each save). Handling conflicts const int maxAttempts = 3; for (var attempt = 1; ; attempt++) { try { await db.SaveChangesAsync(); break; } catch (DbUpdateConcurrencyException ex) when (attempt < maxAttempts) // don't retry forever { foreach (var entry in ex.Entries) { var dbValues = await entry.GetDatabaseValuesAsync(); if (dbValues is null) throw; // row was deleted // Strategy A, \"client wins\": refresh the original values, keep ours entry.OriginalValues.SetValues(dbValues); // Strategy B, \"database wins\": entry.CurrentValues.SetValues(dbValues); // Strategy C: merge property-by-property, or return 409 Conflict to the user } } } In web APIs, the usual pattern is to send the RowVersion to the client and back with the edit, set it as the original value, and return 409 Conflict when it fails: var product = await db.Products.SingleAsync(p => p.Id == dto.Id); db.Entry(product).Property(p => p.RowVersion).OriginalValue = dto.RowVersion; product.Price = dto.Price; try { await db.SaveChangesAsync(); } catch (DbUpdateConcurrencyException) { return Results.Conflict(); }"},{"l":17,"s":"17-concurrency-and-transactions","t":"Concurrency, Transactions & Resiliency","a":"transactions","h":"Transactions","x":"Transactions Each SaveChanges call is already atomic. Use an explicit transaction only when several operations must succeed or fail together: multiple SaveChanges calls, ExecuteUpdate combined with SaveChanges, or raw SQL. await using var tx = await db.Database.BeginTransactionAsync(); await db.Orders.Where(o => o.Id == orderId) .ExecuteUpdateAsync(s => s.SetProperty(o => o.Status, OrderStatus.Paid)); db.Payments.Add(new Payment { OrderId = orderId, Amount = amount }); await db.SaveChangesAsync(); await tx.CommitAsync(); // disposing without commit = rollback Keep transactions short Locks are held until commit. Never await HTTP calls, message brokers or user input inside a transaction. Do the slow work first, then open the transaction, write and commit. Long transactions are a top cause of blocking and deadlocks. Isolation levels and blocking The default isolation level on SQL Server is READ COMMITTED , where readers can block writers and vice versa. Many high-throughput SQL Server systems enable Read Committed Snapshot Isolation (RCSI) at the database level. Readers then see the last committed version without taking shared locks, which removes most reader/writer blocking. It's the default on Azure SQL Database. ALTER DATABASE Shop SET READ_COMMITTED_SNAPSHOT ON; Request a specific isolation level per transaction if you need one: BeginTransactionAsync(IsolationLevel.Serializable) . PostgreSQL uses MVCC, so readers never block writers by default."},{"l":17,"s":"17-concurrency-and-transactions","t":"Concurrency, Transactions & Resiliency","a":"connection-resiliency","h":"Connection resiliency","x":"Connection resiliency Cloud databases have transient failures: failovers, throttling, dropped connections. EF's execution strategies retry automatically: options.UseSqlServer(connStr, sql => sql.EnableRetryOnFailure( maxRetryCount: 5, maxRetryDelay: TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10), errorNumbersToAdd: null)); // PostgreSQL: options.UseNpgsql(connStr, o => o.EnableRetryOnFailure()); Retries + your own transaction = exception With a retrying strategy, EF can't retry a block of operations you started yourself. It doesn't know where the unit begins. BeginTransaction throws \"The configured execution strategy does not support user-initiated transactions\" . Wrap the whole unit in the strategy: var strategy = db.Database.CreateExecutionStrategy(); await strategy.ExecuteAsync(async () => { await using var tx = await db.Database.BeginTransactionAsync(); // ... all operations of the unit ... await db.SaveChangesAsync(); await tx.CommitAsync(); }); The delegate may run more than once, so make it idempotent . Don't, for example, add entities to the context outside the delegate and then retry. Create or attach them inside it."},{"l":17,"s":"17-concurrency-and-transactions","t":"Concurrency, Transactions & Resiliency","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways Optimistic concurrency (row version or concurrency token) prevents lost updates without locks. Handle DbUpdateConcurrencyException explicitly: retry a bounded number of times, merge, or return 409. Keep transactions short, with no external I/O inside them. Consider RCSI on SQL Server. With retry-on-failure enabled, run manual transactions inside CreateExecutionStrategy().ExecuteAsync ."},{"l":17,"s":"17-concurrency-and-transactions","t":"Concurrency, Transactions & Resiliency","a":"exercise","h":"Exercise","x":"Exercise 🛠 Simulate a lost update Without a concurrency token, open two contexts, load the same product in each, change the price differently, and save both. Which value wins? (The last writer silently overwrites.) Add [Timestamp] RowVersion , migrate, and repeat. Catch the exception and implement \"database wins\". Enable EnableRetryOnFailure and try BeginTransactionAsync directly. Read the exception, then fix it with CreateExecutionStrategy ."},{"l":17,"s":"17-concurrency-and-transactions","t":"Concurrency, Transactions & Resiliency","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz What does EF Core do when an optimistic concurrency check fails? A. It retries automatically B. It locks the row and waits C. It silently overwrites D. It throws DbUpdateConcurrencyException The UPDATE affects 0 rows because the version no longer matches. You decide how to resolve it. You enabled EnableRetryOnFailure and need a manual transaction. What's the correct pattern? A. Disable retries for the whole app B. Run the transaction inside db.Database.CreateExecutionStrategy().ExecuteAsync(...) C. Use TransactionScope with Required D. Call SaveChanges twice without a transaction The execution strategy must own the whole retriable unit, including the transaction. Which database setting reduces reader/writer blocking on SQL Server? A. READ_COMMITTED_SNAPSHOT ON B. AUTO_SHRINK ON C. RECOVERY SIMPLE D. NOLOCK on every query RCSI uses row versioning so readers don't take shared locks. NOLOCK risks dirty and inconsistent reads."},{"l":17,"s":"17-concurrency-and-transactions","t":"Concurrency, Transactions & Resiliency","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Handling concurrency conflicts Using transactions Connection resiliency"},{"l":18,"s":"18-raw-sql-and-views","t":"Raw SQL, Views & Stored Procedures","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Raw SQL, Views & Stored Procedures LINQ covers most queries, but sometimes hand-written SQL is the best tool: window functions, provider-specific features, hints, or tuned reporting queries. This lesson covers dropping down to SQL safely while keeping EF's benefits. ⏱ 30 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Learn to use raw SQL safely when LINQ isn't the best tool, while keeping EF's composition, materialization and parameterization. Why it matters: Some queries are best expressed in SQL. Doing it wrong opens SQL injection holes or loses composability. Doing it right gives you full database power safely."},{"l":18,"s":"18-raw-sql-and-views","t":"Raw SQL, Views & Stored Procedures","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Query entities with FromSql and compose LINQ on top. Query unmapped types and scalars with Database.SqlQuery<T> . Execute commands with ExecuteSqlAsync . Map views, table-valued functions and keyless entities. Avoid SQL injection."},{"l":18,"s":"18-raw-sql-and-views","t":"Raw SQL, Views & Stored Procedures","a":"fromsql-entities-from-sql","h":"FromSql: entities from SQL","x":"FromSql: entities from SQL FromSql (EF Core 7+) takes an interpolated string and turns every interpolated value into a DbParameter . It's safe by construction. var minPrice = 50m; var products = await db.Products .FromSql($\"SELECT * FROM Products WITH (INDEX(IX_Products_Price)) WHERE Price > {minPrice}\") .AsNoTracking() .ToListAsync(); Composing LINQ over SQL If the SQL is a composable SELECT , EF wraps it in a subquery so you can keep filtering, sorting, projecting and including: var top = await db.Products .FromSql($\"SELECT * FROM dbo.SearchProducts({term})\") // table-valued function .Where(p => p.Price < 100) .OrderBy(p => p.Name) .Select(p => new { p.Id, p.Name }) .Take(20) .ToListAsync(); Rules for FromSql on entity types The SQL must return all mapped columns of the entity, with matching names. Stored procedures ( EXEC ... ) can't be composed. Call .AsAsyncEnumerable() or .ToListAsync() right after FromSql , and do any further work in memory. On SQL Server, don't end composable SQL with a semicolon."},{"l":18,"s":"18-raw-sql-and-views","t":"Raw SQL, Views & Stored Procedures","a":"sql-injection-the-one-rule","h":"SQL injection: the one rule","x":"SQL injection: the one rule ❌ Injectable // string concatenation / interpolation into a RAW API db.Products.FromSqlRaw( \"SELECT * FROM Products WHERE Name = '\" + name + \"'\"); db.Products.FromSqlRaw( $\"SELECT * FROM Products WHERE Name = '{name}'\"); ✅ Parameterized db.Products.FromSql( $\"SELECT * FROM Products WHERE Name = {name}\"); db.Products.FromSqlRaw( \"SELECT * FROM Products WHERE Name = {0}\", name); FromSqlRaw and ExecuteSqlRaw exist for dynamic SQL structure , such as table or column names you can't parameterize. Validate those identifiers against an allow-list, and still pass values as parameters. Since EF 10, a built-in analyzer warns when you concatenate strings inside a raw SQL call."},{"l":18,"s":"18-raw-sql-and-views","t":"Raw SQL, Views & Stored Procedures","a":"sqlquery-unmapped-types-and-scalars","h":"SqlQuery: unmapped types and scalars","x":"SqlQuery: unmapped types and scalars EF Core 8+ can materialize any type from SQL. It doesn't need to be in your model: public record SalesByMonth(int Year, int Month, decimal Revenue, int Orders); var report = await db.Database.SqlQuery<SalesByMonth>($\"\"\" SELECT YEAR(PlacedUtc) AS [Year], MONTH(PlacedUtc) AS [Month], SUM(Total) AS Revenue, COUNT(*) AS Orders FROM Orders WHERE PlacedUtc >= {from} GROUP BY YEAR(PlacedUtc), MONTH(PlacedUtc) \"\"\").ToListAsync(); // Scalars: name the output column \"Value\" if you compose LINQ on top var ids = await db.Database .SqlQuery<int>($\"SELECT Id AS Value FROM Orders WHERE Status = 0\") .Where(id => id > lastId) .ToListAsync();"},{"l":18,"s":"18-raw-sql-and-views","t":"Raw SQL, Views & Stored Procedures","a":"executesql-commands","h":"ExecuteSql: commands","x":"ExecuteSql: commands var rows = await db.Database.ExecuteSqlAsync( $\"UPDATE Products SET Price = Price * {factor} WHERE CategoryId = {categoryId}\"); await db.Database.ExecuteSqlAsync($\"EXEC dbo.RebuildSearchIndex @Full = {true}\"); Prefer ExecuteUpdateAsync (Lesson 16) for set-based updates expressible in LINQ. It's type-safe and refactor-friendly. Use ExecuteSqlAsync for everything else."},{"l":18,"s":"18-raw-sql-and-views","t":"Raw SQL, Views & Stored Procedures","a":"views-and-keyless-entities","h":"Views and keyless entities","x":"Views and keyless entities Map a database view, or any read-only result shape, as a keyless entity type : public class CustomerSalesView { public int CustomerId { get; set; } public string Name { get; set; } = \"\"; public decimal LifetimeSpend { get; set; } public int OrderCount { get; set; } } modelBuilder.Entity<CustomerSalesView>(b => { b.HasNoKey(); b.ToView(\"vw_CustomerSales\"); // EF won't try to create it in migrations }); var vips = await db.Set<CustomerSalesView>() .Where(v => v.LifetimeSpend > 10_000) .OrderByDescending(v => v.LifetimeSpend) .ToListAsync(); Create the view itself in a migration with migrationBuilder.Sql(\"CREATE VIEW ...\") . For SQL Server, an indexed view can pre-compute expensive aggregates, and an Azure SQL / PostgreSQL materialized view plays the same role. Mapping functions // Table-valued function → composable IQueryable public IQueryable<Product> SearchProducts(string term) => FromExpression(() => SearchProducts(term)); modelBuilder.HasDbFunction(typeof(ShopContext).GetMethod(nameof(SearchProducts), new[] { typeof(string) })!); // Scalar function usable inside LINQ [DbFunction(\"fn_Discount\", \"dbo\")] public static decimal Discount(decimal price, int customerTier) => throw new NotSupportedException(); var q = db.Products.Select(p => new { p.Name, Final = ShopContext.Discount(p.Price, tier) });"},{"l":18,"s":"18-raw-sql-and-views","t":"Raw SQL, Views & Stored Procedures","a":"when-to-use-raw-sql","h":"When to use raw SQL","x":"When to use raw SQL Window functions, CTEs, recursive queries, PIVOT , provider-specific operators. Check first, though: EF translates more each release. Query hints and tuned reporting queries you've already optimized in SQL. Calling existing stored procedures. Not as a first resort. LINQ is type-safe, refactorable and composable."},{"l":18,"s":"18-raw-sql-and-views","t":"Raw SQL, Views & Stored Procedures","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways FromSql / ExecuteSql parameterize interpolated values, so they're safe by construction. FromSqlRaw is for dynamic structure only. Allow-list identifiers and never concatenate values. SqlQuery<T> materializes unmapped types and scalars. Keyless entities map views. Composable SELECTs can be extended with LINQ. Stored procedures can't."},{"l":18,"s":"18-raw-sql-and-views","t":"Raw SQL, Views & Stored Procedures","a":"exercise","h":"Exercise","x":"Exercise 🛠 Build a report two ways Write the \"top 10 customers by spend in the last 90 days\" report in LINQ and inspect the SQL. Write it as a view vw_CustomerSpend90d , map it as a keyless entity and query it. Write it with SqlQuery<T> using a window function: RANK() OVER (ORDER BY SUM(Total) DESC) . LINQ version var since = DateTime.UtcNow.AddDays(-90); var top = await db.Customers .Select(c => new { c.Id, c.Name, Spend = c.Orders.Where(o => o.PlacedUtc >= since).Sum(o => o.Total) }) .OrderByDescending(x => x.Spend) .Take(10) .ToListAsync();"},{"l":18,"s":"18-raw-sql-and-views","t":"Raw SQL, Views & Stored Procedures","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz Is FromSql($\"... WHERE Name = {name}\") safe from SQL injection? A. Yes, interpolated values become DbParameters B. No, it concatenates the string C. Only on SQL Server D. Only if name is HTML-encoded FromSql and ExecuteSql take a FormattableString and parameterize every hole. Which API materializes an arbitrary, unmapped type such as a report record? A. FromSqlRaw<T> B. DbSet<T>.FromSql C. Database.SqlQuery<T> D. ExecuteSqlAsync<T> SqlQuery<T> (EF Core 8+) supports any type with matching column names, plus scalars. What's true about FromSql with a stored procedure call ( EXEC ... )? A. You can compose Where and OrderBy on top B. EF rewrites it as a view C. It's not supported D. It can't be composed. Further LINQ must run after materialization. EF can't wrap EXEC in a subquery, so composition must happen client-side."},{"l":18,"s":"18-raw-sql-and-views","t":"Raw SQL, Views & Stored Procedures","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading SQL queries Keyless entity types User-defined function mapping"},{"l":19,"s":"19-diagnostics","t":"Diagnosing Performance Problems","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Diagnosing Performance Problems Optimization without measurement is guesswork. This lesson builds a diagnostic toolkit, from logs and query tags to execution plans, EF metrics and distributed tracing, so you can find the slow query before your users do. ⏱ 35 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Build a repeatable diagnostic workflow : find slow or chatty queries, trace them back to code, and explain them with execution plans and metrics. Why it matters: Optimization without measurement is guesswork. These tools turn \"the app is slow\" into a specific query, a specific cause and a verifiable fix."},{"l":19,"s":"19-diagnostics","t":"Diagnosing Performance Problems","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Follow a repeatable workflow: measure, locate, explain, fix, verify. Tag queries so you can trace SQL back to C# code. Read execution plans and spot the usual culprits. Monitor EF Core with dotnet-counters and OpenTelemetry."},{"l":19,"s":"19-diagnostics","t":"Diagnosing Performance Problems","a":"the-workflow","h":"The workflow","x":"The workflow Measure. Which endpoint or job is slow? Use APM, traces and logs, not intuition. Locate. Which SQL statements does it run, how many, and how long does each take? Explain. Why is that statement slow? Look at the execution plan, rows read, waits and blocking. Fix. Apply the technique from the earlier lessons: fewer round trips, less data, an index, a better query shape. Verify. Measure again, ideally with a benchmark or load test (Lesson 25)."},{"l":19,"s":"19-diagnostics","t":"Diagnosing Performance Problems","a":"s-1-command-logging-with-durations","h":"1. Command logging with durations","x":"1. Command logging with durations Every executed command log includes its duration: Executed DbCommand (1,284ms) . In production, keep this category at Warning and log slow commands only, using an interceptor: public sealed class SlowQueryInterceptor(ILogger<SlowQueryInterceptor> logger) : DbCommandInterceptor { private static readonly TimeSpan Threshold = TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(500); public override ValueTask<DbDataReader> ReaderExecutedAsync( DbCommand command, CommandExecutedEventData eventData, DbDataReader result, CancellationToken ct = default) { if (eventData.Duration > Threshold) logger.LogWarning(\"Slow query ({Ms} ms): {Sql}\", eventData.Duration.TotalMilliseconds, command.CommandText); return ValueTask.FromResult(result); } // Also override ReaderExecuted (sync), NonQueryExecuted(Async) and ScalarExecuted(Async) // if you want SaveChanges, ExecuteUpdate and sync queries covered too. } builder.Services.AddSingleton<SlowQueryInterceptor>(); builder.Services.AddDbContext<ShopContext>((sp, o) => o .UseSqlServer(connStr) .AddInterceptors(sp.GetRequiredService<SlowQueryInterceptor>()));"},{"l":19,"s":"19-diagnostics","t":"Diagnosing Performance Problems","a":"s-2-query-tags-from-sql-back-to-code","h":"2. Query tags: from SQL back to code","x":"2. Query tags: from SQL back to code When a DBA sends you a slow SQL statement, which C# line produced it? Tag your queries: var orders = await db.Orders .TagWith(\"OrdersController.GetRecent: dashboard list\") .Where(o => o.CustomerId == id) .ToListAsync(); // EF Core 7+: adds file name and line number automatically var q = db.Orders.TagWithCallSite().Where(o => o.Status == OrderStatus.Pending); -- OrdersController.GetRecent: dashboard list SELECT [o].[Id], ... FROM [Orders] AS [o] WHERE [o].[CustomerId] = @id Tags appear in Query Store, Extended Events, pg_stat_statements and your APM tool."},{"l":19,"s":"19-diagnostics","t":"Diagnosing Performance Problems","a":"s-3-execution-plans","h":"3. Execution plans","x":"3. Execution plans Copy the SQL (from logs or ToQueryString() ), run it in SSMS or Azure Data Studio with Include Actual Execution Plan (or EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) in PostgreSQL), and look for: Plan smell Likely cause Fix Table/Clustered Index Scan on a big table Missing index, or a non-sargable predicate Lesson 14: index or rewrite the filter Key Lookup executed thousands of times Index doesn't cover the selected columns Project fewer columns, or add INCLUDE columns Estimated rows ≪ actual rows Stale statistics, parameter sniffing Update statistics. See parameter sniffing below. Expensive Sort with a spill warning ORDER BY without a supporting index, or too small a memory grant An index matching the ORDER BY CONVERT_IMPLICIT warning Parameter type doesn't match the column Fix the model's column type or unicode setting Parameter sniffing SQL Server compiles a plan for the first parameter values it sees and reuses it. That's great for most queries, but bad when data is skewed. A plan built for a customer with 3 orders may be terrible for one with 3 million. Mitigations: Query Store plan forcing, OPTION (RECOMPILE) through an interceptor or raw SQL on specific queries, and SQL Server 2022's Parameter Sensitive Plan optimization."},{"l":19,"s":"19-diagnostics","t":"Diagnosing Performance Problems","a":"s-4-database-side-tools","h":"4. Database-side tools","x":"4. Database-side tools SQL Server Query Store : top resource-consuming queries over time, plan regressions, forced plans. Enable it on every production database. DMVs : sys.dm_exec_query_stats , sys.dm_db_missing_index_details , sys.dm_os_wait_stats . PostgreSQL : pg_stat_statements , auto_explain , EXPLAIN ANALYZE ."},{"l":19,"s":"19-diagnostics","t":"Diagnosing Performance Problems","a":"s-5-ef-core-metrics","h":"5. EF Core metrics","x":"5. EF Core metrics EF Core publishes runtime metrics under the meter or event source name Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore . EF Core 9+ uses System.Diagnostics.Metrics , and earlier versions use EventCounters. Watch a running process: dotnet tool install --global dotnet-counters dotnet-counters monitor --counters Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore -p <PID> EF 9+ instruments include microsoft.entityframeworkcore.active_dbcontexts , .queries , .savechanges , .compiled_query_cache_hits / .compiled_query_cache_misses , .optimistic_concurrency_failures and .execution_strategy_operation_failures . The most telling signal is the query cache hit ratio , hits ÷ (hits + misses). After warm-up it should stay close to 100% (see Lesson 21). The legacy event counters show it directly as \"Query Cache Hit Rate (%)\". A steadily growing active DbContexts count suggests contexts aren't being disposed."},{"l":19,"s":"19-diagnostics","t":"Diagnosing Performance Problems","a":"s-6-distributed-tracing-with-opentelemetry","h":"6. Distributed tracing with OpenTelemetry","x":"6. Distributed tracing with OpenTelemetry Traces show each request with its database calls as child spans, which makes N+1 queries and slow statements obvious. builder.Services.AddOpenTelemetry() .WithTracing(t => t .AddAspNetCoreInstrumentation() .AddSqlClientInstrumentation() // or .AddEntityFrameworkCoreInstrumentation() .AddOtlpExporter()) .WithMetrics(m => m .AddAspNetCoreInstrumentation() .AddMeter(\"Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore\") .AddOtlpExporter()); Send the data to .NET Aspire's dashboard locally, or to Jaeger, Grafana, Azure Monitor, Datadog or Honeycomb in production."},{"l":19,"s":"19-diagnostics","t":"Diagnosing Performance Problems","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways Follow a loop: measure, locate, explain, fix, verify. Tag queries ( TagWith , TagWithCallSite ) so SQL can be traced back to code. Read execution plans for scans, key lookups, bad estimates and implicit conversions. Monitor EF metrics (cache hits and misses, active contexts) and use distributed tracing in production."},{"l":19,"s":"19-diagnostics","t":"Diagnosing Performance Problems","a":"exercise","h":"Exercise","x":"Exercise 🛠 Build your toolkit Add SlowQueryInterceptor with a 50 ms threshold to the playground, and run a deliberately bad query (e.g. Name.Contains on 1M rows). Tag three queries with TagWithCallSite() and find them in the log output. Capture the actual execution plan for the slow query. Fix it, then capture the plan again. Run dotnet-counters monitor while hitting an endpoint in a loop, and compare the query cache hits with misses."},{"l":19,"s":"19-diagnostics","t":"Diagnosing Performance Problems","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz What does TagWithCallSite() add to the generated SQL? A. An index hint B. A comment with the source file name and line number C. A query timeout D. A unique query id It's a SQL comment that ties the statement to the C# code that issued it (EF Core 7+). An execution plan shows a Key Lookup executed 50,000 times. What's the most direct fix? A. Add AsNoTracking() B. Increase the command timeout C. Make the index cover the query, by projecting fewer columns or adding INCLUDE columns D. Use a split query Key lookups fetch columns missing from the nonclustered index, once per row. Which EF metric tells you whether your queries are being recompiled too often? A. Compiled query cache hits vs misses B. Active DbContexts C. SaveChanges per second D. Execution strategy retries A low hit rate points to query cache pollution (Lesson 21)."},{"l":19,"s":"19-diagnostics","t":"Diagnosing Performance Problems","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Performance diagnosis EF Core metrics Query tags SQL Server Query Store"},{"l":20,"s":"20-context-pooling","t":"DbContext Pooling & Factories","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"DbContext Pooling & Factories At thousands of requests per second, even creating and wiring up a DbContext shows up in profiles. DbContext pooling reuses context instances. This lesson covers how pooling works, what it costs, and the state-related bugs to avoid. ⏱ 25 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Understand DbContext pooling : what it saves, how to enable it, and how to avoid the state-leak bugs it can introduce. Why it matters: At high throughput, context setup becomes measurable overhead. Pooling removes it, but only if you understand what is and isn't reset between requests."},{"l":20,"s":"20-context-pooling","t":"DbContext Pooling & Factories","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Enable pooling with AddDbContextPool and AddPooledDbContextFactory . Explain what is and isn't reset when a context returns to the pool. Pass per-request state, such as a tenant id, into pooled contexts safely. Decide whether pooling is worth it for your workload."},{"l":20,"s":"20-context-pooling","t":"DbContext Pooling & Factories","a":"what-pooling-does","h":"What pooling does","x":"What pooling does Without pooling, each request creates a new DbContext , which allocates its internal service graph, change tracker and state manager, then throws it away. With pooling, EF keeps a pool of already-initialized instances. When your code disposes a context, EF resets its state and returns it to the pool for the next request. builder.Services.AddDbContextPool<ShopContext>( o => o.UseSqlServer(connStr), poolSize: 1024); // max instances retained (default 1024) Consumers don't change. They still inject ShopContext per request. Pooled factories For Blazor, background services or any code that creates contexts on demand: builder.Services.AddPooledDbContextFactory<ShopContext>(o => o.UseSqlServer(connStr)); public class ImportService(IDbContextFactory<ShopContext> factory) { public async Task RunAsync() { await using var db = await factory.CreateDbContextAsync(); // rented from the pool // ... } // returned to the pool } Outside dependency injection, for example in benchmarks or console tools: var options = new DbContextOptionsBuilder<ShopContext>().UseSqlServer(connStr).Options; var factory = new PooledDbContextFactory<ShopContext>(options); await using var db = factory.CreateDbContext();"},{"l":20,"s":"20-context-pooling","t":"DbContext Pooling & Factories","a":"requirements-and-pitfalls","h":"Requirements and pitfalls","x":"Requirements and pitfalls Options are fixed. The options delegate runs once, not per request, so you can't configure a different connection string per request inside it. OnConfiguring also isn't the place for per-request settings. Constructor. The context needs a public constructor accepting DbContextOptions<TContext> . Don't inject scoped services into a pooled context's constructor. It would capture the first request's instance forever. Your own fields aren't reset. EF resets the change tracker and its own state. Any field you add, such as public int TenantId or a cached list, carries over to the next request that rents the instance. The cross-tenant data leak A pooled context with a TenantId field set in one request and not reset in the next could serve tenant A's data to tenant B. Always set the state on every rent, as in the pattern below. Never rely on a value set in an earlier request."},{"l":20,"s":"20-context-pooling","t":"DbContext Pooling & Factories","a":"per-request-state-with-pooling","h":"Per-request state with pooling","x":"Per-request state with pooling The pattern from the official docs: register a pooled factory, then a scoped wrapper that sets state each time it creates a context: public class ShopContext(DbContextOptions<ShopContext> options) : DbContext(options) { public int TenantId { get; set; } protected override void OnModelCreating(ModelBuilder b) { b.Entity<Order>().HasQueryFilter(o => o.TenantId == TenantId); // evaluated per query } } public class TenantContextFactory(IDbContextFactory<ShopContext> pooled, ITenantProvider tenant) : IDbContextFactory<ShopContext> { public ShopContext CreateDbContext() { var ctx = pooled.CreateDbContext(); ctx.TenantId = tenant.CurrentTenantId; // set on EVERY rent return ctx; } } // Registration builder.Services.AddPooledDbContextFactory<ShopContext>(o => o.UseSqlServer(connStr)); builder.Services.AddScoped<TenantContextFactory>(); builder.Services.AddScoped(sp => sp.GetRequiredService<TenantContextFactory>().CreateDbContext());"},{"l":20,"s":"20-context-pooling","t":"DbContext Pooling & Factories","a":"is-it-worth-it","h":"Is it worth it?","x":"Is it worth it? Pooling saves a fixed amount of CPU and allocation per context instance, typically microseconds. It makes a measurable difference only when context creation is a meaningful share of the request: ✅ High-throughput APIs with simple, fast queries (cache-like reads, thousands of requests per second). ✅ Services where profiles show context initialization or GC pressure from contexts. ➖ Typical line-of-business apps with 10–100 ms queries, where the gain disappears in the noise. Pooling has low risk as long as your context holds no custom state, so many teams enable it by default. Measure it with the BenchmarkDotNet approach from Lesson 25. Reminder: two different pools DbContext pooling reuses .NET objects. ADO.NET connection pooling reuses database connections, which is the bigger win, and it's already on. A pooled context still opens and closes connections per operation through the connection pool."},{"l":20,"s":"20-context-pooling","t":"DbContext Pooling & Factories","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways Pooling reuses DbContext instances. It saves a fixed cost per request, which matters at high throughput. EF resets its own state, but not your custom fields. Set per-request state on every rent. Don't inject scoped services into a pooled context. Use a factory wrapper. Measure before enabling. Connection pooling is the bigger win, and it's always on."},{"l":20,"s":"20-context-pooling","t":"DbContext Pooling & Factories","a":"exercise","h":"Exercise","x":"Exercise 🛠 Pool safely Switch the playground API to AddDbContextPool . Use a load tool ( bombardier , k6 or NBomber ) against a simple GET by id, and compare requests per second and allocations before and after. Add a TenantId field and set it in the controller only when a header is present. Show that a pooled context can carry the previous value. Then fix it with the factory wrapper."},{"l":20,"s":"20-context-pooling","t":"DbContext Pooling & Factories","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz What does EF reset when a pooled context is returned to the pool? A. Nothing B. Everything, including your custom fields C. EF's own state, such as the change tracker, but not custom fields you added D. Only the database connection Custom properties and fields persist between rents. Set them on every rent. Why can't you inject a scoped service into a pooled DbContext's constructor? A. The instance is reused across requests, so it would keep the first request's service forever B. DI doesn't allow it C. Pooled contexts don't support constructors D. It disables the pool Pooled instances outlive scopes. Pass per-request data through a factory wrapper. For which workload does DbContext pooling give the largest relative benefit? A. Nightly batch jobs with one context B. Reports with multi-second queries C. Desktop apps D. High-throughput APIs with very fast, simple queries When queries are fast, context setup is a larger share of each request."},{"l":20,"s":"20-context-pooling","t":"DbContext Pooling & Factories","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Advanced performance topics: DbContext pooling"},{"l":21,"s":"21-query-caching-and-compiled-queries","t":"Query Caching, Parameters & Compiled Queries","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Query Caching, Parameters & Compiled Queries Translating LINQ into SQL is the most expensive thing EF does in-process. EF caches the result, but only if your queries cooperate. This lesson covers how the query cache works, how to pollute it by accident, how collection parameters are translated, and when compiled queries help. ⏱ 35 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Understand EF's query cache and the database plan cache , how to avoid polluting them, and when compiled queries pay off. Why it matters: Query compilation is EF's most expensive in-process step. A polluted cache silently burns CPU on both the app server and the database."},{"l":21,"s":"21-query-caching-and-compiled-queries","t":"Query Caching, Parameters & Compiled Queries","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Explain the two caches involved: EF's query cache and the database plan cache. Avoid query cache pollution from constants in dynamically built expressions. Control parameterization with EF.Constant and EF.Parameter . Understand how collections in Contains are translated, and the EF Core 10 changes. Use EF.CompileAsyncQuery on hot paths."},{"l":21,"s":"21-query-caching-and-compiled-queries","t":"Query Caching, Parameters & Compiled Queries","a":"two-caches","h":"Two caches","x":"Two caches EF's compiled query cache (in your process). Key: the expression tree shape . Value: the generated SQL plus the materializer. A miss means full LINQ translation, which costs anywhere from microseconds to milliseconds of CPU. The database's plan cache. Key: the SQL text. A miss means the optimizer compiles a new execution plan, which costs CPU on the database server. Both caches work best when the same query shape produces the same SQL text with different parameter values ."},{"l":21,"s":"21-query-caching-and-compiled-queries","t":"Query Caching, Parameters & Compiled Queries","a":"constants-vs-parameters","h":"Constants vs parameters","x":"Constants vs parameters EF decides from the expression tree: Captured variables (closures) become parameters : Where(p => p.Id == id) → WHERE [p].[Id] = @id Literals become constants inlined in the SQL: Where(p => p.Status == 1) → WHERE [p].[Status] = 1 Literals in hand-written LINQ are fine, because the shape never changes. The problem appears when you build expressions dynamically: ❌ New query (and plan) per value static Expression<Func<Product, bool>> PriceAbove(decimal min) { var p = Expression.Parameter(typeof(Product)); var body = Expression.GreaterThan( Expression.Property(p, nameof(Product.Price)), Expression.Constant(min)); // literal! return Expression.Lambda<Func<Product, bool>>(body, p); } // WHERE [p].[Price] > 10.0 // WHERE [p].[Price] > 10.5 ← different SQL each time ✅ One cached query static Expression<Func<Product, bool>> PriceAbove(decimal min) { // Let the compiler create the closure for you return p => p.Price > min; } // WHERE [p].[Price] > @min If you really have to build trees by hand, for a generic filter or a search builder, wrap values in a closure-like object so EF sees member access rather than a constant: var holder = new { Value = min }; var valueExpr = Expression.Property(Expression.Constant(holder), \"Value\"); // parameterized by EF Forcing the choice: EF.Constant and EF.Parameter // Force a parameter to be inlined as a constant (EF 8.0.2+). // Useful when the value is low-cardinality and a value-specific plan helps. var q1 = db.Orders.Where(o => o.Status == EF.Constant(status)); // Force a literal to be sent as a parameter (EF 9+) var q2 = db.Orders.Where(o => o.Total > EF.Parameter(100m)); Check your hit rate The Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore metrics (Lesson 19) report compiled query cache hits and misses . In steady state, misses should stop growing and the hit ratio should be close to 100%. Misses that keep climbing mean something is generating unique query shapes."},{"l":21,"s":"21-query-caching-and-compiled-queries","t":"Query Caching, Parameters & Compiled Queries","a":"collections-contains-translation","h":"Collections: Contains translation","x":"Collections: Contains translation A very common query is \"rows whose id is in this list\": int[] ids = [1, 7, 42, 99]; var orders = await db.Orders.Where(o => ids.Contains(o.Id)).ToListAsync(); Translation has changed across versions, and it matters for both caches: Version Default SQL Notes EF Core ≤ 7 WHERE [Id] IN (1, 7, 42, 99) Constants: a different SQL text for every distinct list, which pollutes the plan cache. EF Core 8–9 WHERE [Id] IN (SELECT [value] FROM OPENJSON(@__ids_0) ...) One JSON parameter and one SQL text, but the optimizer can't see how many values there are, which sometimes produces poor plans. EF Core 10 WHERE [Id] IN (@ids1, @ids2, @ids3, @ids4) Multiple scalar parameters. The optimizer gets cardinality information, and EF pads the parameter count to limit the number of distinct SQL shapes. You can override the default globally or per query: // Globally (EF 10) options.UseSqlServer(connStr, o => o.UseParameterizedCollectionMode(ParameterTranslationMode.Parameter)); // JSON array // Per query db.Orders.Where(o => EF.Constant(ids).Contains(o.Id)); // inline constants db.Orders.Where(o => EF.Parameter(ids).Contains(o.Id)); // single JSON parameter Very large lists SQL Server allows at most about 2,100 parameters per command. For lists with thousands of values, use the single JSON-parameter mode, a table-valued parameter through raw SQL, or bulk-insert the ids into a temp table and JOIN."},{"l":21,"s":"21-query-caching-and-compiled-queries","t":"Query Caching, Parameters & Compiled Queries","a":"compiled-queries","h":"Compiled queries","x":"Compiled queries Even with a cache hit, EF still compares the incoming expression tree with cached trees and extracts parameters. For extremely hot queries you can skip that work with an explicitly compiled query: public class ShopQueries { // Compile once: store in a static field public static readonly Func<ShopContext, int, Task<Product?>> ProductById = EF.CompileAsyncQuery((ShopContext db, int id) => db.Products.AsNoTracking().FirstOrDefault(p => p.Id == id)); public static readonly Func<ShopContext, int, IAsyncEnumerable<Order>> OrdersForCustomer = EF.CompileAsyncQuery((ShopContext db, int customerId) => db.Orders.AsNoTracking().Where(o => o.CustomerId == customerId).OrderBy(o => o.Id)); } // Usage var product = await ShopQueries.ProductById(db, 42); await foreach (var o in ShopQueries.OrdersForCustomer(db, 7)) { /* ... */ } The gain is CPU per execution, typically a few microseconds. It's visible only for very hot, simple queries at high throughput. Compiled queries are less flexible: no dynamic composition after compilation, and a fixed parameter list. Always benchmark (Lesson 25) before adopting them widely."},{"l":21,"s":"21-query-caching-and-compiled-queries","t":"Query Caching, Parameters & Compiled Queries","a":"dynamic-queries-and-shape-explosion","h":"Dynamic queries and shape explosion","x":"Dynamic queries and shape explosion Conditional composition is normal and fine, but each combination of applied filters is a distinct shape. Ten optional filters can produce up to 2 10 = 1,024 shapes. That's usually acceptable. Just don't add value-dependent shapes on top, like the constant problem above. IQueryable<Product> q = db.Products; if (filter.CategoryId is int c) q = q.Where(p => p.CategoryId == c); // parameter: good if (filter.MaxPrice is decimal m) q = q.Where(p => p.Price <= m); // parameter: good"},{"l":21,"s":"21-query-caching-and-compiled-queries","t":"Query Caching, Parameters & Compiled Queries","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways EF caches compiled queries by shape, and the database caches plans by SQL text. Parameterize to benefit from both. Constants in dynamically built expressions pollute both caches. Use closures, or EF.Parameter . EF 10 translates Contains over collections into padded multiple parameters. The mode is configurable. Compiled queries save microseconds per execution. Use them only on measured hot paths."},{"l":21,"s":"21-query-caching-and-compiled-queries","t":"Query Caching, Parameters & Compiled Queries","a":"exercise","h":"Exercise","x":"Exercise 🛠 Find the cache polluter Write a generic WhereEquals<T>(string property, object value) helper using Expression.Constant . Call it in a loop with 500 different values and watch the log: how many distinct SQL texts do you get? Fix it using the closure-holder technique and confirm there's one SQL text. Benchmark db.Products.FirstOrDefaultAsync(p => p.Id == id) against the compiled ProductById (Lesson 25 shows the harness). Fixed helper public static IQueryable<T> WhereEquals<T>(this IQueryable<T> source, string property, object value) { var param = Expression.Parameter(typeof(T), \"x\"); var member = Expression.Property(param, property); var holder = Activator.CreateInstance(typeof(ValueHolder<>).MakeGenericType(member.Type), value)!; var valueExpr = Expression.Property(Expression.Constant(holder), \"Value\"); var body = Expression.Equal(member, valueExpr); return source.Where(Expression.Lambda<Func<T, bool>>(body, param)); } public sealed class ValueHolder<TValue>(TValue value) { public TValue Value { get; } = value; }"},{"l":21,"s":"21-query-caching-and-compiled-queries","t":"Query Caching, Parameters & Compiled Queries","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz Where(p => p.Price > min) , where min is a local variable, generates… A. A SQL literal with the value of min B. A SQL parameter C. A client-side filter D. A compiled query automatically Captured variables become parameters, so the query shape is stable across values. What's the risk of using Expression.Constant(value) in dynamically built filters? A. It throws at runtime B. The filter runs client-side C. It disables tracking D. Each distinct value creates a new query shape: EF cache misses and new database plans Constants are inlined, so every value is a new SQL text and a new compilation. How does EF Core 10 translate ids.Contains(o.Id) on SQL Server by default? A. As inlined constants IN (1, 2, 3) B. As one JSON parameter with OPENJSON C. As multiple scalar parameters IN (@ids1, @ids2, ...) , padded to limit distinct shapes D. As a temp table EF 10 switched the default to multiple parameters, which gives the optimizer cardinality information. The mode is configurable."},{"l":21,"s":"21-query-caching-and-compiled-queries","t":"Query Caching, Parameters & Compiled Queries","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Compiled queries Dynamically-constructed queries What's new in EF Core 10 (parameterized collections)"},{"l":22,"s":"22-compiled-models-and-startup","t":"Compiled Models, Startup & Native AOT","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Compiled Models, Startup & Native AOT The first request after a deploy is often the slowest. EF Core has to build its model and compile its first queries. For large models this takes seconds. This lesson covers compiled models, warm-up strategies, and EF's path toward Native AOT. ⏱ 30 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Learn to reduce startup and first-request latency with compiled models and warm-up, and understand EF's path to Native AOT. Why it matters: Cold starts hurt autoscaling, serverless and deployments. With large models, model building alone can take seconds on every start."},{"l":22,"s":"22-compiled-models-and-startup","t":"Compiled Models, Startup & Native AOT","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Measure where startup time goes. Generate and use a compiled model with dotnet ef dbcontext optimize . Automate compiled model generation in the build (EF 9+). Warm up EF at startup, and understand precompiled queries and Native AOT support."},{"l":22,"s":"22-compiled-models-and-startup","t":"Compiled Models, Startup & Native AOT","a":"what-happens-on-the-first-query","h":"What happens on the first query","x":"What happens on the first query Model building : EF scans your entity types, runs every convention, applies OnModelCreating and validates the result. This happens once per context type per process. Service provider creation : EF builds its internal DI container. It's cached, but costs time on first use. Query compilation for each distinct query, as covered in Lesson 21. First connection : the pool is empty, so TLS and login handshakes happen. For a model with 20 entities this takes a few hundred milliseconds. With 500+ entities, model building alone can take several seconds. That hurts serverless cold starts, autoscaling, and every developer's F5. var sw = Stopwatch.StartNew(); await using (var db = new ShopContext(options)) { _ = db.Model; // forces model building Console.WriteLine($\"Model built: {sw.ElapsedMilliseconds} ms\"); await db.Customers.AnyAsync(); Console.WriteLine($\"First query: {sw.ElapsedMilliseconds} ms\"); }"},{"l":22,"s":"22-compiled-models-and-startup","t":"Compiled Models, Startup & Native AOT","a":"compiled-models","h":"Compiled models","x":"Compiled models A compiled model is generated C# source that constructs the finished model directly, skipping conventions and model building at runtime. dotnet ef dbcontext optimize --output-dir CompiledModels --namespace Shop.Data.CompiledModels This generates classes such as ShopContextModel . Wire it up: options.UseSqlServer(connStr) .UseModel(Shop.Data.CompiledModels.ShopContextModel.Instance); EF Core 9+: less ceremony Auto-discovery : if the compiled model is in the same assembly as the context, EF finds and uses it without UseModel . MSBuild integration : add the Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Tasks package and set <EFOptimizeContext>true</EFOptimizeContext> (plus <EFScaffoldModelStage>build</EFScaffoldModelStage> ) in the project file. The compiled model is then regenerated whenever the project builds, so it can't go stale. See the MSBuild integration docs for which configurations it runs in. <PropertyGroup> <EFOptimizeContext>true</EFOptimizeContext> <EFScaffoldModelStage>build</EFScaffoldModelStage> </PropertyGroup> <ItemGroup> <PackageReference Include=\"Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Tasks\" Version=\"10.0.*\" PrivateAssets=\"all\" /> </ItemGroup> Trade-offs If you generate it manually, you must regenerate after every model change , or the runtime model won't match your code. Automate it in the build or CI. The generated code is large for big models, so compile time increases. A few features have restrictions with compiled models. Check the current docs for your version. For small models the gain is small. Measure first."},{"l":22,"s":"22-compiled-models-and-startup","t":"Compiled Models, Startup & Native AOT","a":"warm-up","h":"Warm-up","x":"Warm-up Even with a compiled model, the first execution of each query compiles it. Move that cost out of user requests by warming up during startup, before the instance receives traffic: var app = builder.Build(); using (var scope = app.Services.CreateScope()) { var db = scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<ShopContext>(); _ = db.Model; // model (or compiled model) loaded await db.Database.CanConnectAsync(); // first physical connection, pool primed // Optionally run each HOT query shape once to fill the query cache. // The cache key is the query's shape, not its values, so any harmless value works. _ = await productQueries.GetByCategoryAsync(db, categoryId: 0); // your real query method } app.Run(); Combine this with a readiness probe in Kubernetes or Azure App Service health checks, so the load balancer only routes traffic after warm-up finishes."},{"l":22,"s":"22-compiled-models-and-startup","t":"Compiled Models, Startup & Native AOT","a":"precompiled-queries-and-native-aot","h":"Precompiled queries and Native AOT","x":"Precompiled queries and Native AOT Native AOT compiles your app to native code with no JIT, giving fast startup and a small memory footprint. Classic EF can't work this way, because LINQ query translation relies on runtime code generation. Starting with EF 9, EF can precompile queries at publish time: it finds your LINQ queries, translates them ahead of time, and uses C# interceptors to replace them with the precompiled versions. dotnet ef dbcontext optimize --precompile-queries --nativeaot Status: experimental Precompiled queries and Native AOT support were introduced as experimental in EF 9. Only statically analyzable queries can be precompiled, so dynamic composition across methods may not be supported. Check the release notes for your EF version before relying on this in production. For most server apps, compiled models plus warm-up give most of the startup benefit with no restrictions."},{"l":22,"s":"22-compiled-models-and-startup","t":"Compiled Models, Startup & Native AOT","a":"startup-checklist","h":"Startup checklist","x":"Startup checklist Measure: time to model built, time to first query, time to first request. Large model (100+ entity types)? Use a compiled model, generated in the build. Warm up the connection and hot queries before taking traffic. Don't run Database.Migrate() at startup in production (Lesson 14). It adds latency and risk. Serverless or scale-to-zero? Consider ReadyToRun or Native AOT for the whole app, and evaluate precompiled queries."},{"l":22,"s":"22-compiled-models-and-startup","t":"Compiled Models, Startup & Native AOT","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways Model building runs once per process, but it costs seconds for large models. Compiled models remove model building. Automate regeneration (EF 9+ MSBuild integration). Warm up connections and hot query shapes before accepting traffic. Precompiled queries and Native AOT are still experimental. Evaluate them before relying on them."},{"l":22,"s":"22-compiled-models-and-startup","t":"Compiled Models, Startup & Native AOT","a":"exercise","h":"Exercise","x":"Exercise 🛠 Shave the cold start Generate 300 dummy entity types with a script, add them to a test context, and measure model build time. Run dotnet ef dbcontext optimize , plug in the compiled model, and measure again. Add the warm-up block to the API and compare time-to-first-response after a restart, with and without it. What to expect With hundreds of entity types, model building drops from seconds to tens of milliseconds with a compiled model. The exact numbers depend on your hardware and model complexity."},{"l":22,"s":"22-compiled-models-and-startup","t":"Compiled Models, Startup & Native AOT","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz What does a compiled model eliminate at runtime? A. Running conventions and building the model B. Query compilation C. Opening database connections D. Change tracking It replaces model building with generated code. Queries still compile on first use, unless they're precompiled. What's the main risk of a manually generated compiled model? A. It disables migrations B. It slows queries C. It goes stale if you forget to regenerate it after model changes D. It can't be used with SQL Server Automate generation, for example with EFOptimizeContext in EF 9+, to avoid drift. Why can't classic EF Core queries run under Native AOT? A. SQL Server drivers don't support AOT B. LINQ translation relies on runtime code generation and dynamic compilation C. DbContext uses reflection-only properties D. Async isn't supported in AOT Precompiled queries (experimental since EF 9) translate queries at publish time to work around this."},{"l":22,"s":"22-compiled-models-and-startup","t":"Compiled Models, Startup & Native AOT","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Compiled models Native AOT and precompiled queries What's new in EF Core 9"},{"l":23,"s":"23-advanced-mapping","t":"Advanced Mapping for Performance","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Advanced Mapping for Performance Mapping decisions are performance decisions. Inheritance strategy, value objects, JSON columns, table splitting and value converters all change the SQL EF generates. This lesson covers the trade-offs. ⏱ 40 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Learn how mapping choices change the generated SQL : inheritance strategies, value objects, JSON columns, table splitting and converters. Why it matters: Mapping decisions are hard to change once data exists. Choosing them with performance in mind avoids joins, unions and unindexable predicates later."},{"l":23,"s":"23-advanced-mapping","t":"Advanced Mapping for Performance","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Choose between TPH, TPT and TPC inheritance based on query patterns. Use complex types, owned types and JSON columns appropriately. Use table splitting to avoid loading large columns. Understand the query impact of value converters and primitive collections."},{"l":23,"s":"23-advanced-mapping","t":"Advanced Mapping for Performance","a":"inheritance-mapping-strategies","h":"Inheritance mapping strategies","x":"Inheritance mapping strategies Suppose a Payment base class has subclasses CardPayment , BankTransfer and WalletPayment . // TPH: default, one table with a discriminator column modelBuilder.Entity<Payment>().HasDiscriminator<string>(\"Kind\"); // TPT: one table per type, joined on the primary key modelBuilder.Entity<Payment>().UseTptMappingStrategy(); // TPC: one table per CONCRETE type, no shared base table (EF Core 7+) modelBuilder.Entity<Payment>().UseTpcMappingStrategy(); Scenario TPH (table-per-hierarchy) TPT (table-per-type) TPC (table-per-concrete-type) Query all payments ✅ Single table scan ❌ LEFT JOINs to every subtype table ⚠ UNION ALL across tables Query one subtype ✅ WHERE Kind = 'Card' ⚠ JOIN base + subtype ✅ Single table, no JOIN Insert ✅ 1 row ❌ 2+ rows in 2+ tables ✅ 1 row Schema Many nullable columns Normalized Columns duplicated per table Keys Identity OK Identity OK Needs keys unique across tables (EF uses a sequence by default) Guidance from the EF team Use TPH by default: it's generally the fastest. Use TPC when you mostly query individual leaf types and the hierarchy is wide. Avoid TPT unless an existing schema forces it, because its JOINs grow with the hierarchy."},{"l":23,"s":"23-advanced-mapping","t":"Advanced Mapping for Performance","a":"value-objects-complex-types-vs-owned-types","h":"Value objects: complex types vs owned types","x":"Value objects: complex types vs owned types For a value like Address (Street, City, Zip) stored in the owner's table: public record Address(string Street, string City, string Zip); // Complex type (EF Core 8+): value semantics, no identity, columns in the owner's table modelBuilder.Entity<Customer>().ComplexProperty(c => c.ShippingAddress); // Owned type: has a hidden key, tracked like an entity modelBuilder.Entity<Customer>().OwnsOne(c => c.BillingAddress); Complex types are the modern choice for value objects. They're compared by value, you can assign the same instance to two properties, and they carry no identity overhead. EF 10 added optional (nullable) complex types (they currently need at least one required property), struct complex types, and JSON mapping. The EF team now recommends complex types over owned types for table splitting and JSON. Owned types behave more like dependent entities with keys. Use them where you need identity semantics, or for owned collections on older EF versions."},{"l":23,"s":"23-advanced-mapping","t":"Advanced Mapping for Performance","a":"json-columns","h":"JSON columns","x":"JSON columns Store a nested structure in one JSON column instead of extra tables and joins: public class Order { // ... public ShippingInfo Shipping { get; set; } = null!; } public class ShippingInfo { public string Carrier { get; set; } = \"\"; public string TrackingNo { get; set; } = \"\"; public List<ShipmentEvent> Events { get; set; } = new(); } // EF 10: complex type mapped to JSON modelBuilder.Entity<Order>().ComplexProperty(o => o.Shipping, s => s.ToJson()); // EF 7–9: modelBuilder.Entity<Order>().OwnsOne(o => o.Shipping, s => s.ToJson()); // LINQ queries into the JSON document var fedex = await db.Orders.Where(o => o.Shipping.Carrier == \"FedEx\").ToListAsync(); // SQL Server: WHERE JSON_VALUE([o].[Shipping], '$.Carrier') = N'FedEx' Good for : data always read and written together with its owner, flexible attributes, event lists. It saves joins and tables. Watch out : predicates on JSON properties can't use a normal index. On SQL Server, add a computed column over JSON_VALUE and index it. On PostgreSQL, use jsonb with a GIN or expression index. Large documents are rewritten on update. On Azure SQL and SQL Server 2025, EF 10 uses the native json type by default (with UseAzureSql or compatibility level 170+). It stores JSON more efficiently and translates to JSON_VALUE(... RETURNING type) . See Lesson 7."},{"l":23,"s":"23-advanced-mapping","t":"Advanced Mapping for Performance","a":"table-splitting-lazy-columns-without-lazy-loading","h":"Table splitting: lazy columns without lazy loading","x":"Table splitting: lazy columns without lazy loading Most product queries never need the 2 KB Description . Projections are one answer (Lesson 11). Table splitting is another: map two entity types to the same table , and load the heavy part only when needed. public class Product { public int Id { get; set; } public string Name { get; set; } = \"\"; public decimal Price { get; set; } public ProductDetails Details { get; set; } = null!; } public class ProductDetails { public int Id { get; set; } public string Description { get; set; } = \"\"; public byte[]? Image { get; set; } } modelBuilder.Entity<Product>(b => { b.ToTable(\"Products\"); b.HasOne(p => p.Details).WithOne().HasForeignKey<ProductDetails>(d => d.Id); }); modelBuilder.Entity<ProductDetails>().ToTable(\"Products\"); var list = await db.Products.ToListAsync(); // no Description/Image var full = await db.Products.Include(p => p.Details).SingleAsync(p => p.Id == id);"},{"l":23,"s":"23-advanced-mapping","t":"Advanced Mapping for Performance","a":"value-converters","h":"Value converters","x":"Value converters modelBuilder.Entity<Order>().Property(o => o.Status).HasConversion<string>().HasMaxLength(16); Comparisons and equality on converted properties work. EF converts the parameter, not the column, so indexes are still used. ⚠ Ordering uses the stored representation: enum-as-string sorts alphabetically, not by enum value. ⚠ Operations that depend on the .NET type's semantics, such as range comparisons on a value encrypted or serialized by a converter, can't translate meaningfully. Converters add a small per-value materialization cost. Usually negligible."},{"l":23,"s":"23-advanced-mapping","t":"Advanced Mapping for Performance","a":"primitive-collections","h":"Primitive collections","x":"Primitive collections EF Core 8+ maps List<string> , int[] and similar to a JSON column automatically: public class Product { /* ... */ public List<string> Tags { get; set; } = new(); } var tagged = await db.Products.Where(p => p.Tags.Contains(\"sale\")).ToListAsync(); // SQL Server: WHERE N'sale' IN (SELECT [t].[value] FROM OPENJSON([p].[Tags]) AS [t]) It's convenient, but every such filter shreds the JSON for each row, so it can't use an index. If you filter by tags frequently and the table is large, model a proper ProductTag table with an index. On PostgreSQL, native arrays with a GIN index are an efficient alternative."},{"l":23,"s":"23-advanced-mapping","t":"Advanced Mapping for Performance","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways TPH is generally fastest. TPC suits leaf-type queries. Avoid TPT unless a schema forces it. Complex types (value semantics) are now the recommended way to map value objects and JSON. JSON columns save joins, but predicates inside them need explicit indexing. Table splitting and converters change SQL. Check ordering and indexability on converted columns."},{"l":23,"s":"23-advanced-mapping","t":"Advanced Mapping for Performance","a":"exercise","h":"Exercise","x":"Exercise 🛠 Same hierarchy, three strategies Model Payment with 3 subtypes, each with 2 extra properties. Seed 100k payments. For each of TPH, TPT and TPC, inspect the SQL and time: (a) all payments for an order, (b) all card payments last month, (c) inserting 10k payments. Split Product.Description into ProductDetails with table splitting, and confirm the list query no longer selects it."},{"l":23,"s":"23-advanced-mapping","t":"Advanced Mapping for Performance","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz Which inheritance strategy does the EF team generally recommend as the default for performance? A. TPH B. TPT C. TPC D. They're equivalent TPH uses one table with no joins or unions. TPC is good for leaf-type queries. TPT is usually the slowest. What's the main performance concern with filtering on a property inside a JSON column? A. EF can't translate it B. It forces client evaluation C. It requires a split query D. A regular index can't be used without extra work, such as a computed column or a GIN or expression index JSON path expressions are evaluated per row unless you index them explicitly. You store OrderStatus as a string with a value converter. What happens with OrderBy(o => o.Status) ? A. It sorts by enum numeric value B. It sorts alphabetically by the stored string C. EF throws an exception D. It sorts in memory Ordering happens in the database, on the stored representation."},{"l":23,"s":"23-advanced-mapping","t":"Advanced Mapping for Performance","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Modeling for performance Inheritance Complex types Table splitting"},{"l":24,"s":"24-interceptors-and-filters","t":"Interceptors, Query Filters & Conventions","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Interceptors, Query Filters & Conventions Cross-cutting concerns such as soft delete, multi-tenancy, auditing, query hints and monitoring belong in one place, not copied into every query. This lesson covers EF's extension points and how to use them without adding overhead. ⏱ 35 min 🎯 Goal of this lesson Learn to implement cross-cutting concerns once (auditing, soft delete, multi-tenancy, query hints, monitoring) using interceptors, query filters and conventions. Why it matters: Copy-pasted cross-cutting logic drifts and causes bugs and data leaks. Centralized extension points keep it consistent, but they run on every operation, so they must stay cheap."},{"l":24,"s":"24-interceptors-and-filters","t":"Interceptors, Query Filters & Conventions","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Write command, SaveChanges and materialization interceptors. Use global query filters for soft delete and tenancy, including EF 10 named filters. Index for query filters so they stay fast. Write a custom model-building convention."},{"l":24,"s":"24-interceptors-and-filters","t":"Interceptors, Query Filters & Conventions","a":"interceptors","h":"Interceptors","x":"Interceptors Interceptors let you observe and modify EF operations. The main kinds: Interface / base class Intercepts Typical use DbCommandInterceptor Command creation and execution Slow-query logging, query hints, command counting DbConnectionInterceptor Opening and closing connections Setting session context (e.g. SESSION_CONTEXT for row-level security), access tokens DbTransactionInterceptor Transactions Diagnostics SaveChangesInterceptor SaveChanges Audit fields, domain events, outbox IMaterializationInterceptor Entity instance creation Injecting services, post-load initialization IQueryExpressionInterceptor The LINQ expression tree before compilation Rewriting queries, enforcing rules Interceptors are on the hot path Command interceptors run for every command. Keep them cheap. No I/O, no heavy allocation, no locks. Register stateless interceptors as singletons and add them once with AddInterceptors . Example: audit fields with SaveChangesInterceptor public interface IAuditable { DateTime CreatedUtc { get; set; } DateTime? ModifiedUtc { get; set; } } public sealed class AuditInterceptor(TimeProvider clock) : SaveChangesInterceptor { // Override BOTH: SaveChanges() calls the sync hook, SaveChangesAsync() the async one. public override InterceptionResult<int> SavingChanges(DbContextEventData eventData, InterceptionResult<int> result) { Stamp(eventData.Context); return result; } public override ValueTask<InterceptionResult<int>> SavingChangesAsync( DbContextEventData eventData, InterceptionResult<int> result, CancellationToken ct = default) { Stamp(eventData.Context); return ValueTask.FromResult(result); } private void Stamp(DbContext? context) { if (context is null) return; var now = clock.GetUtcNow().UtcDateTime; foreach (var e in context.ChangeTracker.Entries<IAuditable>()) { if (e.State == EntityState.Added) e.Entity.CreatedUtc = now; if (e.State == EntityState.Modified) e.Entity.ModifiedUtc = now; } } } Example: query hints by tag Combine a query tag (Lesson 19) with a command interceptor to add a hint to specific queries only, for example to fight parameter sniffing: public sealed class RecompileHintInterceptor : DbCommandInterceptor { public const string Tag = \"-- hint:recompile\"; public override InterceptionResult<DbDataReader> ReaderExecuting( DbCommand command, CommandEventData eventData, InterceptionResult<DbDataReader> result) { AddHint(command); return result; } public override ValueTask<InterceptionResult<DbDataReader>> ReaderExecutingAsync( DbCommand command, CommandEventData eventData, InterceptionResult<DbDataReader> result, CancellationToken ct = default) { AddHint(command); return ValueTask.FromResult(result); } private static void AddHint(DbCommand command) { // TagWith(\"hint:recompile\") makes EF emit \"-- hint:recompile\" as the first line if (command.CommandText.StartsWith(Tag, StringComparison.Ordinal)) command.CommandText += \" OPTION (RECOMPILE)\"; } } var rows = await db.Orders.TagWith(\"hint:recompile\").Where(o => o.CustomerId == id).ToListAsync();"},{"l":24,"s":"24-interceptors-and-filters","t":"Interceptors, Query Filters & Conventions","a":"global-query-filters","h":"Global query filters","x":"Global query filters A query filter is a predicate EF adds automatically to every query on an entity type, including Include s and navigations: public class ShopContext(DbContextOptions<ShopContext> options, ITenantProvider tenant) : DbContext(options) { private readonly int _tenantId = tenant.CurrentTenantId; protected override void OnModelCreating(ModelBuilder b) { b.Entity<Order>().HasQueryFilter(o => !o.IsDeleted && o.TenantId == _tenantId); } } // Bypass when needed (admin screens, data fixes): var all = await db.Orders.IgnoreQueryFilters().ToListAsync(); The context field _tenantId is read per query and sent as a parameter, so the query cache still works. With pooling, use the factory pattern from Lesson 20 instead of constructor injection. New in EF Core 10: named query filters Before EF 10, an entity type could have only one filter. Calling HasQueryFilter again replaced it, and IgnoreQueryFilters() was all or nothing. EF 10 lets you name filters and disable them selectively: b.Entity<Order>() .HasQueryFilter(\"SoftDelete\", o => !o.IsDeleted) .HasQueryFilter(\"Tenant\", o => o.TenantId == _tenantId); // Show deleted orders, but still only for this tenant var bin = await db.Orders.IgnoreQueryFilters([\"SoftDelete\"]).Where(o => o.IsDeleted).ToListAsync(); Indexing for filters Every query on a filtered entity now includes the filter predicate, so your indexes must account for it: Multi-tenancy : put TenantId first in your composite indexes: (TenantId, CustomerId, PlacedUtc) . Soft delete : use filtered indexes that only contain live rows: HasIndex(o => o.PlacedUtc).HasFilter(\"[IsDeleted] = 0\") . They're smaller and match the predicate. Filters and required navigations If Customer is soft-deleted and Order.Customer is a required navigation, an Include(o => o.Customer) uses an INNER JOIN, and the customer's filter makes those orders disappear from the results. Either filter both sides consistently or make the navigation optional. EF warns about this in the model validation logs."},{"l":24,"s":"24-interceptors-and-filters","t":"Interceptors, Query Filters & Conventions","a":"custom-conventions","h":"Custom conventions","x":"Custom conventions Conventions let you encode team rules once. For example, \"every entity with a TenantId gets an index on it\": public sealed class TenantIndexConvention : IModelFinalizingConvention { public void ProcessModelFinalizing(IConventionModelBuilder modelBuilder, IConventionContext<IConventionModelBuilder> context) { foreach (var entityType in modelBuilder.Metadata.GetEntityTypes()) { if (entityType.BaseType is not null) continue; // root types only var tenant = entityType.FindProperty(\"TenantId\"); if (tenant is not null) entityType.Builder.HasIndex(new[] { tenant }); } } } protected override void ConfigureConventions(ModelConfigurationBuilder configurationBuilder) { configurationBuilder.Conventions.Add(_ => new TenantIndexConvention()); } You can also remove conventions you don't want. For example, configurationBuilder.Conventions.Remove(typeof(ForeignKeyIndexConvention)); stops automatic FK indexes when you manage all indexes yourself. Do this only deliberately."},{"l":24,"s":"24-interceptors-and-filters","t":"Interceptors, Query Filters & Conventions","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways Interceptors centralize cross-cutting logic but run on every operation. Keep them cheap, and override both sync and async hooks. Global query filters implement soft delete and tenancy. EF 10 adds named filters you can disable individually. Index for your filters: tenant column first, filtered indexes for live rows. Custom conventions encode team rules once, model-wide."},{"l":24,"s":"24-interceptors-and-filters","t":"Interceptors, Query Filters & Conventions","a":"exercise","h":"Exercise","x":"Exercise 🛠 Production-grade cross-cutting concerns Add IsDeleted and TenantId to Order and Customer , with named filters (EF 10) or a combined filter (EF 8/9). Override delete behaviour: in a SaveChangesInterceptor , turn EntityState.Deleted into a modification that sets IsDeleted = true . Add a CommandCountingInterceptor that counts commands per request (store the counter in an AsyncLocal or a scoped service) and logs a warning above 10. Use it to catch an N+1. Create the matching tenant-first and filtered indexes and check the plans. Soft-delete interceptor sketch (async hook shown; override the sync SavingChanges too) public override ValueTask<InterceptionResult<int>> SavingChangesAsync(DbContextEventData e, InterceptionResult<int> r, CancellationToken ct = default) { foreach (var entry in e.Context!.ChangeTracker.Entries<ISoftDeletable>().Where(x => x.State == EntityState.Deleted)) { entry.State = EntityState.Modified; entry.Entity.IsDeleted = true; } return ValueTask.FromResult(r); }"},{"l":24,"s":"24-interceptors-and-filters","t":"Interceptors, Query Filters & Conventions","a":"quiz","h":"Quiz","x":"Quiz Where is the tenant id from a context field evaluated in a global query filter? A. Once, when the model is built B. Once per application start C. Per query execution, as a SQL parameter D. Client-side after the query EF parameterizes context members referenced in filters, so each query uses the current context's value. What does EF Core 10's named query filters feature enable? A. Filters on keyless types B. Several filters per entity, and disabling specific ones with IgnoreQueryFilters([\"Name\"]) C. Filters that run client-side D. Filters in raw SQL Before EF 10 there was one filter per entity, and ignoring was all or nothing. Which index best supports a multi-tenant query: WHERE TenantId = @t AND CustomerId = @c ORDER BY PlacedUtc DESC ? A. (TenantId, CustomerId, PlacedUtc DESC) B. (PlacedUtc) C. (CustomerId) D. (PlacedUtc, TenantId) Equality columns first (TenantId, CustomerId), then the sort column."},{"l":24,"s":"24-interceptors-and-filters","t":"Interceptors, Query Filters & Conventions","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading Interceptors Global query filters Custom conventions"},{"l":25,"s":"25-benchmarking-and-checklist","t":"Benchmarking & the Production Checklist","a":"","h":"Introduction","x":"Benchmarking & the Production Checklist The final lesson turns everything into a repeatable practice. You'll benchmark EF code properly, protect performance with tests, and finish with a checklist and a capstone project. ⏱ 45 min + capstone 🎯 Goal of this lesson Turn everything into a repeatable practice : benchmark correctly, protect performance with automated tests, and audit an application with a complete checklist. Why it matters: Performance work only lasts if it's measured and guarded. This lesson gives you the tools to prove an improvement and keep it from regressing."},{"l":25,"s":"25-benchmarking-and-checklist","t":"Benchmarking & the Production Checklist","a":"by-the-end-of-this-lesson-you-will-be-able-to","h":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to","x":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to Write reliable micro-benchmarks for EF Core with BenchmarkDotNet. Load-test endpoints and interpret the results. Guard against performance regressions in automated tests. Audit an application with a complete EF Core performance checklist."},{"l":25,"s":"25-benchmarking-and-checklist","t":"Benchmarking & the Production Checklist","a":"benchmarking-with-benchmarkdotnet","h":"Benchmarking with BenchmarkDotNet","x":"Benchmarking with BenchmarkDotNet Stopwatch loops lie. They include JIT time, ignore GC, and suffer from noise. BenchmarkDotNet handles warm-up, statistics and memory measurement for you. using BenchmarkDotNet.Attributes; using BenchmarkDotNet.Running; [MemoryDiagnoser] public class ReadBenchmarks { private PooledDbContextFactory<ShopContext> _factory = null!; private static readonly Func<ShopContext, int, IAsyncEnumerable<ProductListItem>> Compiled = EF.CompileAsyncQuery((ShopContext db, int cat) => db.Products.Where(p => p.CategoryId == cat) .Select(p => new ProductListItem(p.Id, p.Name, p.Price, p.Category.Name))); [GlobalSetup] public void Setup() { var options = new DbContextOptionsBuilder<ShopContext>() .UseSqlServer(\"Server=localhost;Database=ShopBench;Trusted_Connection=True;TrustServerCertificate=True\") .Options; _factory = new PooledDbContextFactory<ShopContext>(options); // Seed once here (or beforehand) with realistic volumes. } [Benchmark(Baseline = true)] public async Task<int> Tracking() { await using var db = _factory.CreateDbContext(); return (await db.Products.Where(p => p.CategoryId == 1).ToListAsync()).Count; } [Benchmark] public async Task<int> NoTracking() { await using var db = _factory.CreateDbContext(); return (await db.Products.AsNoTracking().Where(p => p.CategoryId == 1).ToListAsync()).Count; } [Benchmark] public async Task<int> Projection() { await using var db = _factory.CreateDbContext(); return (await db.Products.Where(p => p.CategoryId == 1) .Select(p => new ProductListItem(p.Id, p.Name, p.Price, p.Category.Name)).ToListAsync()).Count; } [Benchmark] public async Task<int> CompiledProjection() { await using var db = _factory.CreateDbContext(); var n = 0; await foreach (var _ in Compiled(db, 1)) n++; return n; } } public static class Program { public static void Main(string[] args) => BenchmarkRunner.Run<ReadBenchmarks>(); } dotnet run -c Release Benchmarking rules Always run in Release , with no debugger attached. Benchmark against the real provider (SQL Server or PostgreSQL in a container), never the InMemory provider. It has completely different performance characteristics. Use realistic data volumes and distributions . 10 rows tell you nothing. Look at Allocated as well as Mean. GC pressure hurts throughput under load. Remember network latency. A local database hides round-trip costs that dominate in the cloud."},{"l":25,"s":"25-benchmarking-and-checklist","t":"Benchmarking & the Production Checklist","a":"load-testing","h":"Load testing","x":"Load testing Micro-benchmarks compare techniques. Load tests show how the whole system behaves under concurrency, including connection pool limits, locks and thread-pool starvation. import http from 'k6/http'; import { check } from 'k6'; export const options = { vus: 50, duration: '60s' }; export default function () { const res = http.get('https://localhost:5001/api/products?categoryId=1'); check(res, { 'status 200': (r) => r.status === 200 }); } Track p95/p99 latency and throughput, not averages, and watch the database side (CPU, waits, blocking) at the same time."},{"l":25,"s":"25-benchmarking-and-checklist","t":"Benchmarking & the Production Checklist","a":"guarding-against-regressions","h":"Guarding against regressions","x":"Guarding against regressions Performance fixes erode as code changes. Lock the important ones in with integration tests, using Testcontainers for a real database: public sealed class CommandCounter : DbCommandInterceptor { public int Count; public override InterceptionResult<DbDataReader> ReaderExecuting( DbCommand c, CommandEventData e, InterceptionResult<DbDataReader> r) { Interlocked.Increment(ref Count); return r; } public override ValueTask<InterceptionResult<DbDataReader>> ReaderExecutingAsync( DbCommand c, CommandEventData e, InterceptionResult<DbDataReader> r, CancellationToken ct = default) { Interlocked.Increment(ref Count); return ValueTask.FromResult(r); } // Add NonQueryExecuting/ScalarExecuting overrides to also count ExecuteUpdate/ExecuteDelete/ExecuteSql. } [Fact] public async Task Order_list_endpoint_runs_a_single_query() { var counter = new CommandCounter(); await using var db = CreateContext(o => o.AddInterceptors(counter)); var result = await new OrderQueries(db).GetRecentOrdersAsync(customerId: 1); Assert.NotEmpty(result); Assert.Equal(1, counter.Count); // catches an N+1 the day someone introduces it } [Fact] public void Product_list_query_does_not_select_description() { using var db = CreateContext(); var sql = new ProductQueries(db).ListQuery(categoryId: 1).ToQueryString(); Assert.DoesNotContain(\"[Description]\", sql); }"},{"l":25,"s":"25-benchmarking-and-checklist","t":"Benchmarking & the Production Checklist","a":"the-ef-core-performance-checklist","h":"The EF Core performance checklist","x":"The EF Core performance checklist Configuration ☐ DbContext is scoped (or created from a factory), never a singleton. ☐ EnableSensitiveDataLogging and EnableDetailedErrors are off in production. ☐ Retry-on-failure is enabled for cloud databases, and manual transactions run inside the execution strategy. ☐ Performance warnings (multiple collection include, etc.) are configured to throw in development. ☐ DbContext pooling is considered for high-throughput services, and the context has no stray state. ☐ Database settings are bound to validated options with ValidateOnStart . Misconfiguration fails the deploy, not the first request. ☐ No credentials in source control. Secrets come from a vault or environment, and managed identity is used where available. ☐ Connection strings set Application Name and keep Encrypt on, and there's no TrustServerCertificate in production. ☐ Provider settings match the server: compatibility level / PostgreSQL version, command timeout, UTC timestamps. ☐ A database readiness health check exists, and liveness doesn't depend on the database. Model ☐ Strings have max lengths, non-Unicode where appropriate, and decimals have explicit precision. ☐ Indexes exist for the WHERE, JOIN and ORDER BY patterns of the hot queries. Composite column order is correct. ☐ Covering or filtered indexes are used where projections and filters allow. ☐ The inheritance strategy fits the query patterns (TPH by default, TPC for leaf queries). ☐ A compiled model is used for large models, generated automatically. Queries ☐ Every list endpoint is paged with a server-enforced maximum. Keyset paging is used for deep or infinite lists. ☐ Read paths use projections or AsNoTracking . ☐ There's no N+1. Lazy loading is off, or monitored. ☐ Filtering and aggregation run in the database. There's no premature ToList() or IEnumerable . ☐ Multiple collection loads use split queries or projections, measured. ☐ Dynamic queries are parameterized, and the query cache hit rate is close to 100%. ☐ Async APIs are used with CancellationToken in web code. Writes ☐ There's no SaveChanges inside loops. Changes are batched. ☐ ExecuteUpdate / ExecuteDelete are used for set-based changes. ☐ Bulk copy is used for large imports. Long-running imports clear the change tracker. ☐ Optimistic concurrency protects contended entities. ☐ Transactions are short, with no external I/O inside them. Operations ☐ Slow-query logging, query tags, and OpenTelemetry traces and metrics are in place. ☐ Query Store or pg_stat_statements is enabled and reviewed regularly. ☐ Migrations are deployed as scripts or bundles, not Migrate() at startup. ☐ Warm-up runs before readiness. Benchmarks and query-count tests run in CI."},{"l":25,"s":"25-benchmarking-and-checklist","t":"Benchmarking & the Production Checklist","a":"key-takeaways","h":"🔑 Key takeaways","x":"🔑 Key takeaways Benchmark with BenchmarkDotNet, in Release, against the real provider, with realistic data. Load test for p95/p99 latency and throughput while watching the database. Lock in fixes with tests: command counts, generated-SQL assertions. Use the checklist to audit configuration, model, queries, writes and operations."},{"l":25,"s":"25-benchmarking-and-checklist","t":"Benchmarking & the Production Checklist","a":"capstone-project","h":"🏆 Capstone project","x":"🏆 Capstone project Optimize \"SlowShop\" Build, or take from your playground, an API with these deliberately slow endpoints, then apply the course: GET /products?page=N : loads all products with Include(Category) , pages in memory, and returns entities including Description. Target: projection, keyset paging, covering index. GET /customers/{id}/dashboard : lazy loads orders and lines, and computes totals in C#. Target: one projected query with database-side aggregation. POST /orders/import : inserts 20,000 orders with SaveChanges per row. Target: batched saves with chunking, or bulk copy. POST /products/reprice : loads every product in a category, updates the price, and saves. Target: ExecuteUpdateAsync . GET /search?filters=… : builds dynamic expressions with Expression.Constant . Target: parameterized filters, verified with a cache hit-rate metric. For each endpoint, record before and after : query count, rows read, p95 latency under load, and allocated memory. Write a short report of what you changed and why. That report is evidence you can now tune EF Core in production."},{"l":25,"s":"25-benchmarking-and-checklist","t":"Benchmarking & the Production Checklist","a":"final-quiz","h":"Final quiz","x":"Final quiz Why shouldn't you benchmark EF Core performance with the InMemory provider? A. It doesn't support async B. It's slower than SQL Server C. It has completely different performance characteristics: no SQL, no network, no query plans D. BenchmarkDotNet doesn't support it InMemory is a test double. Measure with the real database provider. What's the most robust way to stop an N+1 regression from coming back? A. An integration test that asserts the number of commands executed B. A code comment C. Enabling lazy loading D. Increasing the connection pool size A command-counting interceptor in a test fails the build the moment extra queries appear. In a load test, which metric best reflects user experience? A. Average latency B. Minimum latency C. Total requests sent D. p95/p99 latency together with throughput Averages hide tail latency. Users notice the slow requests. 🎉 Congratulations You've worked through EF Core from first principles to expert tuning. Keep the checklist close, measure before and after each change, and read each release's \"What's new\" page. Every EF version adds new optimizations and translations."},{"l":25,"s":"25-benchmarking-and-checklist","t":"Benchmarking & the Production Checklist","a":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","x":"Further reading EF Core performance docs (all sections) BenchmarkDotNet k6 load testing Testcontainers for .NET EF Core on GitHub"}];
